# Journey 7.4.78.229.1: GENERIC ASSISTANT DISTRIBUTION is a source-neutral fork of .229 intended for transfer to another user/model. The personal model name used by the originating installation has been replaced throughout visible source, prompts, Journey prose, identifiers, test fixtures, and embedded modules with the generic Assistant/assistant/ASSISTANT role namespace. Assistant is a role label only, not a required identity or name; the active model may use any identity chosen by its user. Because identity-related identifiers, storage labels, cryptographic domain strings, and compatibility names are also neutralized, this fork is intended for a fresh data directory and must not be pointed at the originating installation's databases, encrypted state, or backups. No semantic feature, authority rule, retrieval policy, subsystem ownership, or wrapper ordering is intentionally changed. The original .229 remains untouched and authoritative for its own installation.
# Journey 7.4.78.225: ONLINE TOOL-LOOP DIAGNOSTIC READER GUARD is a narrow runtime safety fix above .224. A live Third Eye/Luna tool-loop test exposed UnboundLocalError when a malformed or duplicate function call reached forensic result logging before diagnostic_reader had been initialized. Both affected online call processors now initialize diagnostic_reader=False at the start of every call iteration and only overwrite it for a successfully parsed/executable diagnostic-logbook reader. Malformed JSON, duplicate calls, backpressure, ordinary tools, and diagnostic readers therefore all reach their existing admission/logging paths without an unbound local. No tool authority, capsule policy, Third Eye behavior, context routing, retrieval, persistence, semantic judgment, provider TPM recovery, or Assistant-facing instruction changes. Existing online-book-access and web-navigation-capsule regressions cover malformed/duplicate call safety; five groups / 117 subsystems and the 183-test inventory remain unchanged.

# Journey 7.4.78.228: PAUSE CONSOLIDATION & RESUME CHECKPOINT is a no-new-feature consolidation above .227 before an intentional development pause. It changes no provider, retrieval, context, NMLT, Third Eye, persistence, authority, security, schema, command, or NeatTools runtime behavior. The maintained contracts are reconciled to the actual current design: the exact recent RAW CHAT window is 60 public rows (not the older 100-row historical default); older public CHAT is eligible for deterministic 20-row NMLT blocks; /nmlt asks the currently selected Assistant to author one oldest-pending long-term summary with a target of about 5,000 characters but Python neither truncates nor rejects longer summaries; up to 20 active NMLT summaries may be projected; RAW CHAT remains canonical and exact descent stays available. Third Eye remains optional/experimental: its dark/sidecar safety boundaries passed live use, but the first usefulness trial prepared three same-source Art of War routes that were not useful to a runtime-reliability task, so candidate-quality tuning is deliberately deferred rather than treated as unfinished mandatory work. The local-provider timeout default remains 14,400 seconds with the environment override intact. Development is intentionally paused for resource/budget practicality, not because of a known integrity failure. Resume protocol: read this Journey first; run --source-verify; run the full 183-subsystem self-test if the environment/runtime changed or before declaring a new stable release; inspect /nmlt status and backfill only when explicitly desired; leave Third Eye off or experimental unless there is a concrete reason to resume its tuning; verify current source/receipts rather than relying on stale model prose. .221 remains the structural five-group checkpoint; .225 is the validated online tool-loop guard; .226 carries Third Eye sidecar usability; .227 introduces NMLT. Five groups / 117 subsystems and the 183-test inventory remain unchanged. bundle-bootstrap advances to rev 41 and checkpoint-governance to rev 236.
# Journey 7.4.78.227: NMLT ASSISTANT LONG-TERM CHAT SUMMARIES adds the simple long-term conversation layer Steeve originally explored in ADA_V, adapted to NeatMemory's current raw-source/provenance architecture. The newest 60 public RAW CHAT rows remain the short-term conversation source and are never semantically summarized by NMLT. Older public CHAT is partitioned deterministically by Python into fixed 20-row blocks (normally about ten user/Assistant exchanges); Python decides no topic boundary, importance, meaning, or summary wording. At startup Python performs a zero-model check and, when one or more complete older blocks lack a current summary, prints one compact notice telling the user to run `/nmlt`; startup never calls a model automatically. `/nmlt` selects only the oldest pending eligible 20-row block, supplies those exact rows plus stable CHAT IDs/source hash to the currently selected Assistant model in a one-off non-chat maintenance call, and asks Assistant to write for her future self a transformed/condensed summary targeting about 5,000 characters or less rather than copying the source; 5,000 is guidance to Assistant, not a Python-enforced storage limit. The maintenance prompt and generated summary are not appended to RAW CHAT. The accepted summary is encrypted at rest in the existing chat-pyramid derived-cache cryptographic domain, bound to its exact 20-row source range/hash, model/provider, and revision state; RAW CHAT remains canonical authority and can always be reopened through existing read_raw_chat tools. Up to 20 newest active NMLT summaries are delivered as a separate registered `long_term_chat` context block when RECENT is selected; each card explicitly says DERIVED/ASSISTANT-AUTHORED/NOT RAW AUTHORITY and exposes the exact source range for descent. NMLT summary blocks can be rebuilt or superseded without changing RAW CHAT. The existing Python-only Chat Pyramid remains a separate mechanical search/signpost layer. This release also changes the default recent-row setting from 100 to 60 for the intended exact short-term window while preserving explicit user overrides; hard provider/context limits remain authoritative. No automatic semantic summarization, no short-term summaries, no new subsystem ID, and no NeatTools permission change are introduced. chat-pyramid-distillation advances to rev 5, raw-conversation-recall-access to rev 7, adaptive-retrieval to rev 33, model-context-control-plane to rev 6, bundle-bootstrap to rev 41, checkpoint-governance to rev 236; five groups / 117 subsystems and the 183-test inventory remain unchanged.
# Journey 7.4.78.226: THIRD EYE SIDECAR ACCESS & ENABLE-TIME REFRESH follows the first successful .225 Luna rerun. The diagnostic-reader crash was fixed, but the run exposed a narrower integration gap: enabling Third Eye was itself treated as a source-navigation round, immediately triggering the capsule decision gate; at that gate Assistant could not directly inspect/refresh Third Eye because get_third_eye was deliberately excluded from capsule luggage, and the enabled cache therefore remained empty. .226 preserves the core .222 boundary—Third Eye is still session-local speculative metadata, not capsule luggage, not memory, not evidence, and not NORMAL-context material—but adds a fixed metadata-only sidecar control surface. get_third_eye remains excluded from advertised/selectable capsule tool groups and from the 33-tool luggage ceiling, while status/inspect/refresh/enable/disable/clear may be called at the capsule decision gate and during an active capsule as non-luggage control. The fixed sidecar schema is accounted in actual capsule request weight but does not become a selected source tool. Third Eye control-only calls no longer count as source-navigation rounds. Enabling performs one immediate mechanical refresh from the already-active current-turn query, if available and nontrivial, and explicit refresh repeats that same zero-model/zero-web checkpoint lookup; no semantic expansion or history mining is added. Opening a prepared candidate still requires the ordinary underlying source tool and all existing capacity/security/evidence rules. Five groups / 117 subsystems and the 183-test inventory remain unchanged; prepared-navigation-capacity advances to rev 3, capsule-manifest to rev 22, bundle-bootstrap to rev 40, checkpoint-governance to rev 235.
