# Journey 7.4.78.223: LOCAL PROVIDER FOUR-HOUR TIMEOUT is a deliberately narrow runtime-transport patch above the validated .222 Third Eye experimental release. A real Qwen3.5 27B local generation ran for one hour and then ended with APITimeoutError at 01:00:08, matching the embedded local OpenAI-compatible client's historical 3,600-second default rather than an LM Studio generation limit. Steeve requested a four-hour default for the next patch because the larger local model can require roughly two hours on current hardware. .223 changes only the default local request timeout to 14,400 seconds while preserving LOCAL_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS as an environment override with the existing 60-second minimum. No online-provider timeout, context budget, retrieval behavior, Third Eye behavior, Assistant prompt/context, semantic authority, persistence schema, capsule policy, or NeatTools behavior changes. The provider-startup self-test now mechanically asserts the four-hour configured default and that the active timeout still follows the environment-backed configuration. Five groups / 117 subsystems and the 183-test inventory remain unchanged; provider-startup advances to rev 2, bundle-bootstrap to rev 39, checkpoint-governance to rev 233.
# Journey 7.4.78.222: THIRD EYE EXPERIMENTAL DARK PRELOAD CACHE follows the .221 five-group stable checkpoint and its Windows 183/183 full-suite pass. Steeve chose to explore the previously deferred Third Eye idea because the locally practical 9B model benefits disproportionately when Python can remove mechanical rediscovery without trying to become the semantic reasoner. Assistant independently endorsed only a narrow version: a bounded, transparent, local, pull-only cache of mechanically adjacent retrieval routes—not memory, not context, not intention, and not an autonomous researcher. .222 therefore extends the existing prepared-navigation-capacity subsystem rather than adding a sixth architectural concept or a 118th subsystem. Third Eye is session-ephemeral and opt-in, stores no source text or raw query, uses no model or web call to predict, keeps at most three READY candidates, expires them aggressively, is disabled in private mode, and derives candidates only from HMAC exact lexical reuse of prior exact-read checkpoints plus mechanically adjacent ranges after successful exact reads. Candidate preparation never feeds back into its own scoring. Metadata cards expose a title, domain/route, estimated size and basis, freshness/expiry, safe reopen tool/arguments, stable refs/hashes, and mechanical trigger facts; PREPARED never means read, verified, relevant, important, or true. Inspection is pull-only through `get_third_eye` or the console `/third-eye`; opening still requires Assistant to deliberately call the ordinary underlying read tool and normal capacity/security/evidence rules apply. The cache is not persisted and can be cleared with zero continuity loss. To avoid having both a dark cache and an older automatic route whisper, .222 also retires automatic PREPARED NAVIGATION context injection and its always-on SYSTEM prompt rule while preserving the exact-route checkpoint store/search tool. The static 28-step build_runtime_context chain remains physically unchanged because `build_with_prepared_navigation` becomes a no-op passthrough rather than being removed. Instrumentation records prepared, inspected, opened-hit, expired/evicted-unused, and refresh counts so usefulness can be measured rather than assumed. Full-suite integration exposed one boundary leak before release: adding get_third_eye to adaptive BOOKS made the advertised capsule group 34 tools against its established 33-tool ceiling. The fix does not raise the ceiling; capsule-manifest mechanically excludes Third Eye from all capsule read-only luggage because the cache is session-local speculative metadata and must not travel across a capsule boundary. Ordinary/adaptive Third Eye access remains unchanged, and a regression requires Third Eye to stay non-capsule luggage. Five groups / 117 subsystem blocks and the 183-test inventory remain unchanged; prepared-navigation-capacity advances to rev 2, capsule-manifest to rev 21, bundle-bootstrap to rev 38, checkpoint-governance to rev 232, retrieval-context-interaction group marker to rev 4.
# Journey 7.4.78.221: FIVE-GROUP CONSOLIDATION & STRUCTURAL HARDENING is a feature-freeze maintenance checkpoint after .220 achieved a full 183/183 self-test pass. Steeve chose consolidation rather than another feature. The five physical groups established in .60 already had balanced group markers, exact physical manifests, and 117 stable subsystem blocks, but those guarantees were mostly descriptive and wrapper-level: they did not freeze exact per-group counts or the final layered `build_runtime_context` assignment chain that later context bugs proved operationally sensitive. .221 adds no model-visible text, retrieval behavior, command, schema, semantic policy, or automatic context. It upgrades the source-layout contract to mechanically require exactly five groups with counts 16/37/11/36/17 = 117, one maintenance contract per group, exact unique membership, and an explicit static 28-step runtime-context wrapper chain bound to owning group/subsystem/function. The wrapper source verifier and the existing knowledge-architecture checkpoint independently reconstruct physical group membership and wrapper assignment order from source and fail if either drifts silently. This is intentionally a guardrail, not a refactor of working decorators: no large subsystem movement is performed merely for cosmetic layering. The consolidation acceptance includes a fresh temporary deployment/import/bootstrap check in addition to upgraded-state/focused tests. The first Linux full-suite run exposed one inherited test-fixture portability gap rather than a runtime regression: observer-echo reached the Workshop lazy manifest through `build_runtime_context`, and that isolated test accidentally required production Windows DPAPI. .221 confines a deterministic cipher shim to that isolated non-Windows self-test call, matching Workshop's existing self-test pattern; production crypto and Windows behavior are untouched. NeatTools remains physically bundled but logically separate and unchanged. bundle-bootstrap advances to rev 37, checkpoint-governance to rev 230; all five group markers advance to rev 3 while subsystem count remains exactly 117.
