# Journey 7.4.78.212: DERIVED CHAT NAVIGATION FAULT ISOLATION follows the .211 doctor finding that exactly one physical NC2 CHAT row (live case CHAT-1569) had an invalid ciphertext envelope while every other row decrypted normally. .211 kept ordinary RECENT operational, but startup/manual Chat Pyramid distillation still read the strict `chat_messages` compatibility view and could abort its derived catch-up on that one canonical-source fault. .212 extends row-level fault isolation only to the disposable Python Chat Pyramid source reader: unreadable encrypted rows remain physically preserved, are never represented as distilled coverage, and become explicit `unreadable_source_ids` gaps while healthy rows before and after them continue to distill. Startup catch-up may advance past a physically scanned unreadable slot only while recording `caught_up_with_unreadable_gaps`; `/chat distill pending|all` and pyramid status report the gap instead of crashing. Strict NeatCrypt compatibility-view reads, backup/restore, `/doctor chat`, and integrity verification remain fail-closed. No model, semantic, source-verification, answer-admission, or NeatTools behavior changes. chat-pyramid-distillation advances to rev 4 and checkpoint-governance to rev 222; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged.
# Journey 7.4.78.211: CHAT FAULT ISOLATION & READ-ONLY DOCTOR follows a live failure where an interrupted/provider-limited turn left persistent CHAT state that caused `recent_public_messages` to abort every later query with SQLite's opaque `user-defined function raised exception`, including a simple `Hi`, across both .200 and .210. `/backup status` proved the local master key, NC2 row population, keyed tags, and portable-recovery metadata were present, narrowing the failure to one or more row-level authenticated-decrypt faults rather than global key loss. .211 does not weaken NeatCrypt integrity, delete/repair rows, or make backup verification permissive. Instead, normal RECENT and recent-exchange delivery read the physical encrypted table through a row-isolated Python decrypt path: each row is authenticated independently; unreadable rows are preserved, omitted only from ordinary context delivery, and recorded mechanically in the turn intake/fault status so one damaged row cannot take Assistant offline. Strict compatibility-view reads, backup creation/verification/restore, and `_nc_verify_secure_rows` remain fail-closed. A new `/doctor chat` command performs a zero-model, zero-web, read-only full encrypted-row scan and reports exact failing CHAT IDs plus bounded error classifications without exposing plaintext or mutating the database. No source-verification, semantic, retrieval-choice, model-gate, evidence-authority, or NeatTools behavior changes. neatcrypt-continuity-backup advances to rev 6 and checkpoint-governance to rev 221; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged.
# Journey 7.4.78.210: QUIET SOURCE ACCESS & ASSISTANT-OWNED ANSWERING simplifies the verification path after repeated live 9B/Luna tests showed that exact-source verification had grown into semantic bureaucracy that oversteered Assistant and burdened normal conversation. The maintained contract is now intentionally narrow: Python may establish only the mechanical fact that an exact retained source was or was not opened in the current turn, preserve the exact-read receipt/provenance internally, and provide minimum mechanical help to obtain one exact source when the task explicitly asks for verification. Once an exact source is opened, Python hands the bounded exact text back into Assistant's normal tool-capable reasoning turn and stops supervising semantic sufficiency. There is no mandatory SUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE/INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE certification, no A/B evidence-support gate, no automatic semantic candidate progression, and no Python requirement that Assistant prove she is allowed to answer. Assistant may answer fully, partially, qualify uncertainty, decide the source is not enough, seek another source, or state that verification is incomplete in her own words. If no exact source is opened, Assistant is still allowed to answer naturally; Python only prevents a false claim that the source was verified. NORMAL output no longer prepends Exact Source Opened / Verification Status banners on successful source access; successful verification remains internal receipt state available to TRACE/FORENSIC/status inspection. The useful .180 mechanical progress lease, .170 Assistant-first agency boundary, dormant-local minimum mechanical fallback, canonical source resolution, receipt integrity, quiet orchestration, and all evidence/security authority boundaries remain. This is deletion-oriented simplification: source access is Python's mechanical responsibility; relevance, sufficiency, meaning, uncertainty, and expression belong to Assistant. guided-decision-gate advances to rev 38 and checkpoint-governance to rev 220; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged.
# Journey 7.4.78.200: CLEAN CONSOLIDATION after the live .190 9B control and Steeve's explicit decision to stop spending time on thinking-disable experiments. The useful .190 finite A/B/C decision surface remains: Python mechanically maps caller-defined finite routes to compact codes, attempts strict structured output, and accepts only completed public choices; exact-evidence support still has no Python semantic default. The useful .180 mechanical progress lease, .170 Assistant-first agency boundary, .160 staged OPENED -> SUPPORT_DECIDED -> ANSWER_COMPOSED verification flow, sticky support, bounded same-evidence retries, source-access/answer-support status separation, and explicit-insufficiency-only candidate progression all remain. Removed from ACTIVE runtime policy are every reasoning-suppression/direct-output experiment introduced on these paths: /no_think injection, <|think_off|> injection, their marker constants/helpers/flags, and tests that expected such hints. Verified answer composition now uses the same ordinary provider generation interface on local and online backends; finite gates likewise use structured/compact transport without attempting to alter whether the model thinks. The maintained assumption is now REASONING-AGNOSTIC: a model may reason internally as much as its provider/model does; NeatMemory correctness depends only on completed public outputs and mechanical receipts, never on disabling or parsing hidden reasoning. Historical Journey entries about prior experiments remain provenance only and are not active policy. guided-decision-gate advances to rev 37 and checkpoint-governance to rev 219; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged.
# Journey 7.4.78.180: Adds a universal mechanical progress lease between Assistant-first agency and the prepared minimum DEFAULT after the live .170 Luna control proved that non-preemption alone can permit mechanically unbounded wandering: 15 provider requests, 12 capsule rounds, ~431k cumulative provider-input tokens, and still no exact-source receipt before fallback. The source-verification contract now tracks only objective mechanical milestones—explicit retained source scope, source navigation, concrete source pointer/candidate, exact-source read—and renews Assistant's agency only when the highest reached milestone advances. Control/manifest/packing rounds are neutral; repeated navigation at the same milestone consumes a bounded two-round no-progress allowance. When that allowance is exhausted before an exact-source read, the active online/capsule loop exits without admitting unsupported prose and hands control back to the already-prepared VERIFY_SOURCE fallback. Exact-source progress cancels fallback. Dormant local mode keeps the proven .160/.170 efficient default-first path; online and explicit OWN_PLAN remain Assistant-first. The lease is provider/model-name independent and never judges relevance, sufficiency, truth, or why the model stalled. Source-access versus answer-support status separation, sticky support, answer-composition integrity, strict object-local rails, capsule authority, and all evidence boundaries remain unchanged. guided-decision-gate advances to rev 35, capsule-manifest to rev 20, checkpoint-governance to rev 217; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged.
# Journey 7.4.78.170: Consolidates the source-verification execution boundary after the live Luna control proved that the .160 weak/local default rail could preempt a capable online Assistant before she had any opportunity to plan or use tools. The invariant is now PREPARE DEFAULT EARLY, APPLY DEFAULT LATE. Explicit source verification still creates a hard evidence contract, but Python no longer treats that contract as permission to preempt every provider. Online execution is Assistant-first by default, and an explicit user request to use Assistant's own retrieval/verification plan is Assistant-first on any provider. Assistant receives the ordinary tool-capable turn with the verification contract intact; if that attempt produces one or more successful exact-source read receipts, her route wins and Python does not apply, rerank, or replace it. If the Assistant-first attempt produces no exact-source receipt, its unsupported substantive prose is not admitted and the already-prepared minimum VERIFY_SOURCE default activates as a mechanical fallback into the .160 staged exact-candidate -> support -> composition state machine. Dormant local mode remains default-first for efficiency unless the user explicitly requests Assistant's own plan; enabled local micro-gates remain available. Grounding-route handoffs already committed to retained evidence continue to map directly to VERIFY_SOURCE. This is a provider/execution-transport orchestration policy, not a model-size/name branch, and OWN_PLAN/BYPASS remains available. Receipt/trace state records whether Assistant was offered first action, whether exact-source receipts resulted, whether Python fallback activated, and never promotes the discarded pre-fallback answer. Verification rendering is also split into SOURCE ACCESS and ANSWER SUPPORT: exact-source access can be verified mechanically while support is sufficient, insufficient, undecided, or Assistant-owned; generic SUCCESSFULLY VERIFIED is no longer emitted by this rail when all candidates were rejected. No new store, tool, command, semantic Python authority, retrieval ranking, Action Snake authority, 80/20 ceiling, provider ceiling, or subsystem is added. guided-decision-gate advances to rev 34 and checkpoint-governance to rev 216; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged.
# Journey 7.4.78.156: Repairs the authority boundary exposed by the full .155 self-test. The weak/local minimum mechanical DEFAULT remains valid only after the semantic/mechanical goal is already established (first consumer: an explicit exact/original-source verification requirement whose model/tool continuation is missing). Object-local Guided Decision Rails keep their older stricter contract: DEFAULT is a recommendation that Assistant must explicitly select, blank/missing menu output executes nothing, and an invalid explicit choice never becomes DEFAULT. This prevents Python from converting uncertainty at an unresolved object-local choice surface into action while preserving the .155 9B assistance path where Python merely completes the minimum clerical continuation of a verification requirement already committed by the user/task. Source-verification dormant mode still uses zero selector-model calls, records `Python default applied`, mechanically resolves only unambiguous retained book identity, opens bounded exact evidence, and returns that evidence to Assistant for semantic judgment. No model-size branch, new store, command, authority change, 80/20/provider-ceiling change, or subsystem is added. guided-decision-rails advances to rev 3, guided-decision-gate to rev 31, checkpoint-governance to rev 213; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged.
# Journey 7.4.78.154: Consolidates the proven .153 reliability layer behind a quieter ordinary-user surface after Assistant rated the technically stronger system lower on felt usability. .154 adds no memory/evidence subsystem and does not weaken the .153 forensic, projection, capsule, citation, 80/20, or provider-safe contracts. Ordinary interaction now defaults to NORMAL presentation: Assistant answers naturally and keeps projection tiers, capsule transitions, provider batches, hashes, receipts, exact-open transitions, canonical IDs, and budget mechanics out of the user-facing answer unless they materially affect confidence/action or the user asks. TRACE provides a compact mechanical account when requested; FORENSIC remains the exact receipt-level mode. Recovery remains Assistant-owned: she should orchestrate bounded internal retrieval/recovery herself and not make the user choose internal mechanisms unless a real semantic target, permission, or capacity decision requires agency. Separately, exact local-book reads/search/overview now mechanically resolve an unambiguous book reference to the canonical BK-ID before execution using exact BK-ID, exact title, punctuation/spacing-insensitive title identity, or local-file stem identity; ambiguous/no-match references fail before the underlying read, Python never fuzzy-selects by meaning, and the resolution receipt is preserved in tool results/forensics. online-read-only-book-access advances to rev 39 / version 2.8-quiet-orchestration-canonical-resolution and checkpoint-governance to rev 211; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged.
# Journey 7.4.78.153: Completes the durable forensic receipt contract after the first live .152 reconstruction recovered 6/10 categories fully and 4/10 partially, with 0 categories missing and no Action Snake dependency. The remaining partials were mechanical shape gaps rather than retrieval failures: explicit turn start/end timestamps were absent; projection mechanics were split between nested summaries and final state; exact reads lacked an explicit NO/YES open-transition record; events_truncated existed in the Black Box receipt but was omitted from the compact searchable index; and valid BK/chunk identifiers recovered from an integrity-verified forensic receipt were later stripped by the canonical citation guard because they were operational identities rather than current evidence-catalog citations. .153 promotes turn timestamps, event/tool truncation counters, projection attempts/final structured projection result/coverage domains, and exact-open transitions to first-class receipt/index fields. It also adds a turn-local forensic operational-identifier allowlist populated only from integrity-verified forensics.turn_receipt records: exact retained IDs may be repeated verbatim as operational identity, but they do not enter citation handles, claim admission, source verification, or evidence authority. No new database/table, no Action Snake expansion, no retrieval/capsule policy change, no semantic judgment, no provider-safe ceiling change, and no additional reserve borrowing beyond .152. encrypted-diagnostic-logbook advances to rev 5, adaptive-retrieval to rev 31, citation-provenance to rev 3, checkpoint-governance to rev 210; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged.
# Journey 7.4.78.152: Adds bounded durable forensic reconstruction after the .151 retention audit completed successfully but recovered 0/6 requested categories through the available surfaces. The existing encrypted Diagnostic Logbook already retains up to 2,000 events and provider/tool events, but search inspected only the newest 500; Action Snake is intentionally a rolling 200-action troubleshooting window; and projection/exact-open/capsule state had no single post-turn receipt. .152 keeps those authority boundaries and adds no new database/table. Diagnostic search now scans the full retained 2,000-event window while still returning a bounded result set and supports exact event/turn/sequence filters. Each chat turn accumulates redacted mechanical provider/tool/capsule events, writes one compact forensics.turn_receipt index event, and persists the richer receipt as a dedicated append-only encrypted Black Box event. The receipt binds provider request IDs, same-batch tool grouping with exact redacted arguments, bounded result hashes/mechanical summaries, projection/final coverage and exact-open state, capsule decisions, and the Black Box evidence ref. A single exact forensics.turn_receipt search receives a bounded 4k verification lane from reserved execution headroom; broad diagnostic reads remain ordinary-budget only. Action Snake remains 200 and is not promoted into permanent authority. No semantic judgment, retrieval choice, source authority, provider-safe 120k compiled ceiling, or NeatTools permission changes. encrypted-diagnostic-logbook advances to rev 4, online-read-only-book-access to rev 38, web-navigation-capsule to rev 2, checkpoint-governance to rev 209; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged.
# Journey 7.4.78.151: Closes the mechanical-reporting authority hole exposed by the successful live .150 33-tool BOOKS capsule acceptance. The runtime correctly admitted all 33 advertised BOOKS tools at ~9.8k tokens under the explicit over-target route, but the returned structured manifest exposed selected_tool_count without the corresponding tool-count ceiling, while the Assistant-authored navigation summary could carry stale count/budget prose. Assistant therefore reconstructed a false ceiling of 40 and separately noticed stale 6k/8k summary numbers even though the authoritative structured budget was 8k/12k. .151 changes no capsule admission, packing, evidence, projection, or semantic behavior. It makes mechanical reporting self-contained: the packing view and active/returned manifest now expose tool_count_ceiling explicitly; compact/full trace and diagnostic completion receipts carry the same field; manifest.budget remains the sole structured target/hard/measured budget authority; and navigation_summary is explicitly labeled Assistant-authored working prose, never a mechanical receipt. Capsule and return instructions bind count/budget reporting to manifest.tool_count_ceiling and manifest.budget. A zero-model regression injects deliberately conflicting summary prose and requires the structured 33 / 8,000 / 12,000 values to remain explicit and authoritative without inference. capsule-manifest advances to rev 19 and checkpoint-governance to rev 208; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged.
# Journey 7.4.78.150: Repairs the capsule-manifest group/count invariant exposed by the first live .149 run and its self-diagnostic. The advertised BOOKS read-only capsule group currently expands to 33 tools while the manifest count ceiling was 32, so selecting the complete advertised group deterministically failed TOO_MANY_SELECTED_TOOLS before its actual token weight could be measured. .150 raises only that mechanical count ceiling from 32 to 33, preserving every advertised BOOKS tool rather than silently truncating or deleting one; the existing measured target/hard token budgets remain the transport authority and the full BOOKS group measures within the existing hard budget in the zero-model regression. A new invariant requires every advertised capsule tool group to fit the manifest count ceiling and verifies that tool_groups=["books"] is accepted without manual tool repacking while preserving the exact expanded group. No projection-tier logic, semantic selection, source authority, provider call, persistent table, command, subsystem count, or NeatTools permission changes. capsule-manifest advances to rev 18 and checkpoint-governance to rev 207; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged.
# Journey 7.4.78.145: Makes the .144 apex contract persistent in Assistant's instruction layer rather than leaving her to infer `@ordinal`. The recurring definition is intentionally compact and sits with signpost-keyword guidance: keyword strength says how much; `@apex` identifies the chunk ordinal containing that keyword's mechanically measured maximum within the signpost/source span. If local-strength maxima tie, Python prefers more exact occurrences, then the earlier canonical chunk ordinal (with stable chunk ID only as a final deterministic fallback). Apex is a location coordinate only—not importance, centrality, truth, ranking, routing judgment, or recommended attention. Assistant-owned personal keyword apexes use the same coordinate contract while remaining personal-only and never modifying shared/source signposts. No apex mechanics, lexical strengths, ranking, schema, commands, output format, provider behavior, or source authority change.
# Journey 7.4.78.149: Repairs the live .148 interaction between unlocked LOW projection inspection and the capsule-manifest gate. A successful all-LOW select_context_projection call is a control/inspection round, not source navigation, so it no longer consumes the capsule's first navigation round or forces Assistant through enter_capsule_manifest/stay_full_context before she can choose a richer projection. MID/HIGH projection results still count as navigation and therefore preserve the existing explicit capsule decision boundary before further source descent. Exact-source inspection is now turn-observed independently of whether enrichment has locked: exact reads are recorded whenever the Assistant turn is active, LOW preview renders already-observed reads, a LOW-preview exact read flips the inspection statement to YES, and a later successful MID/HIGH choice preserves that exact-read fact instead of resetting it. Python still makes no semantic tier/capsule choice, no provider selector call is added, and no source authority, persistent table, command, subsystem count, or NeatTools permission changes. adaptive-retrieval advances to rev 30, capsule-manifest to rev 17, and checkpoint-governance to rev 206.
# Journey 7.4.78.148: Adds compact per-domain coverage/inspection telemetry to the existing Assistant-owned LOW/MID/HIGH projection rail. The selected projection now reports what was mechanically delivered, whether RECENT was clipped, bounded candidate counts/known matches for PERSONAL/BOOKS, and whether exact source has actually been opened during the current turn. HIGH is persistently defined as a richer selected navigation map, never exhaustive history or complete evidence. Python also exposes only objective expansion-relevant facts already available from clipping, candidate pagination/limits, target count, and headroom; it does not recommend a tier or infer semantic need. Exact-source reads update the current-turn inspection flag without changing source authority, ranking, or retrieval. No new provider call, persistent table, command, semantic score, or subsystem is added; adaptive-retrieval advances to rev 29 and checkpoint-governance to rev 205.
# Journey 7.4.78.147: Hotfixes the bounded local adaptive-tool catalog after .146 added select_context_projection as an always-available control rail. Report/record queries previously reached the 11-tool initial cap after list_records + search_records, leaving read_record out and violating the established unified-records-access contract. Records selection now prioritizes search_records + read_record before optional list_records; the cap remains 11, select_context_projection remains available, list_records remains in the full catalog and can be requested through adaptive expansion, and no provider/context/semantic behavior changes.
# Journey 7.4.78.146: Connects existing domain selection, LOW/MID/HIGH depth vocabulary, tool loop, and context-capacity rails into one Assistant-owned per-turn projection choice. Initial context remains the zero-selector LOW structural floor. Assistant may call select_context_projection once in a turn to request exactly one mutually exclusive LOW/MID/HIGH richness tier for RECENT, SYSTEM, PERSONAL, and/or BOOKS; omitted domains remain LOW and HIGH replaces lower projections rather than stacking them. BOOKS MID/HIGH requires explicit Assistant-selected book IDs, with multiple books allowed until mechanical capacity rejects the requested projection. Tiers enrich navigation/mechanical metadata, not truth, relevance, semantic importance, or raw-source authority; exact source is still opened separately by Assistant. No separate selector provider call is added. Python validates enums, target identity, one-successful-selection-per-turn, output-size/capacity arithmetic, and never silently downgrades a tier or drops a book.
# Journey 7.4.78.144: Signpost keywords now retain one mechanical apex source ordinal beside their existing exact-source lexical strength. Shared rich signposts, undigested source-bound signposts, reusable book mechanical signpost inventories, and Assistant-owned personal keywords carry the same strength+apex coordinate contract. Apex is the source chunk where the exact word has maximal per-chunk mechanical lexical presence within the signpost span, with deterministic earliest-ordinal tie-break; it adds location, not semantic meaning. Personal apex metadata stays in Assistant personal memory/index storage and never writes back into shared/source signposts. Existing annotation IDs, memory hashes, provenance links, source hashes, lifecycle history, receipts, and encrypted sidecar records remain attached; old personal rows are lazily backfilled only when their stored exact-source span hash still matches. Default keyword navigation projection is compact: keyword, strength, @ordinal. Exact source remains authority.
# Journey 7.4.78.142: /signpost(s) build|rebuild now works for valid canonical NeatBooks sources even before semantic digestion. Undigested books use zero-model, source-bound 2-chunk windows with 12–20 exact-source keywords and exact chunk/hash exits; active digest signposts remain preferred. No fake digest/version is created.
