# 7.4.78.86 MINIMAL ORIGINAL-WORD REFLECTION PROMPT RESET follows the user correction that the pre-.84 custom-keyword reflection behavior was working and the desired change was simply that Assistant use words actually written in the original text. .84 over-engineered that requirement into a source-word audit/repair/enforcement subsystem, which made a single morphological choice such as `concentration` versus source `concentrated` reject an otherwise useful revision and complicated rebuild/reflection behavior. .86 keeps the .85 semantic-unit-first scheduler fix, keeps Python automatic keyword distillation source-derived with the Unicode-safe tokenizer, but removes the .84 audit/repair layer and post-hoc source-membership rejection from Assistant edits. The reflection instruction now plainly tells Assistant to copy 12-20 words exactly from the original source range she is reading; Python validates only list mechanics, current-head identity, append-only lineage, and indexing, and does not choose or correct her semantic words. Original text remains evidence authority and signposts remain navigation. Journey/checkpoint governance advances to rev 151.
# 7.4.78.85 SEMANTIC-UNIT-FIRST BOOK REFLECTION follows the first live `.84` continuation run after exact source-word grounding. The keyword membership invariant itself held, but the scheduler exposed a category error: dozens of structural-resume RSR heads legitimately shared broad 32-51 and 32-99 source ranges, so treating every RSR as a bounded local reading unit caused rewind attempts, hard-token abandonment, huge deferred lists, zero Assistant-reviewed signposts, and checkpoint advance past those ranges. .85 leaves source-word grounding and structural signpost searchability intact but removes structural_resume heads from normal `/reflect book` rewind/completion/review gating. Assistant reflection is semantic-unit-first because SDU rows are the model-authored reading units intended to be inspected against exact source. The 12k soft target and 20k ordinary hard source bound remain; one semantic unit may mechanically extend to a bounded 30k completion ceiling so a slightly oversized leaf such as the observed 32-51 unit is not starved. Book/chapter-scale SDR ranges never trigger that extension. Python uses only persisted node_type/range/token mechanics and makes no semantic relevance decision. Journey/checkpoint governance advances to rev 150.
# 7.4.78.84 EXACT SOURCE-WORD KEYWORD GROUNDING follows the first broad live `.83` `/reflect book` enrichment pass over The Art of War. The bounded reflection mechanics were healthy (chunks 17-31, 18,432 source tokens, 14 complete signposts, 13 append-only revisions), but inspection of Assistant's revised lists exposed a semantic-navigation contract leak: Assistant was encouraged to choose "better" or distinctive keywords and Python validated only count/shape, so a revision could introduce a synonym/conceptual label absent from the stored source range; meanwhile the Python baseline inherited an ASCII-biased tokenizer that could split Unicode source words such as `chüan` into fragmentary pseudo-keywords. .84 makes source-word membership a hard mechanical invariant for both keyword owners. Python distillation now ranks only Unicode NFKC+casefold word tokens actually present in the exact source text. Assistant may still choose relevance and replace a 12-20-word list, but the edit path reopens the complete immutable source range and fails closed if any proposed word is absent; no synonym, paraphrase, implication, or substring fragment is accepted. Pre-.84 current heads are mechanically audited: valid source-grounded choices are preserved, unsupported entries are removed, and only if needed to restore the 12-word minimum does Python fill from deterministic literal source tokens; every repair is a new append-only RSR revision and old history is untouched. A small audit cache prevents rereading already-verified unchanged revisions on every reflection session. Signposts remain navigation only and original book text remains authority. Journey/checkpoint governance advances to rev 149.
