# 7.4.78.60 FIVE-GROUP ARCHITECTURE CONSOLIDATION is a non-semantic maintenance pass after the .59 live stability test finally validated the complete high-pressure path: tier-boundary RECENT gates reached 150k; Assistant selected REDUCE_RECENT_USE_HIGH; HIGH remained HIGH; request #1 reconciled to 149,713/150,000 with exact preview/preflight hash+accounting parity; the reserved one-receipt lane admitted an ~808-token durable receipt under backpressure; tool phase closed; request #2 received a fresh SYNTHESIS_AFTER_TOOL_RESULTS envelope at 133,972/150,000 with independent exact parity and completed successfully. With the capacity/retrieval path stable, this release consolidates source ownership instead of adding behavior. The original five-group one-file architecture had drifted as later patches accumulated: authority-clarity, authority-flow-diagnostic, guided-decision-rails, guided-decision-gate, and rear-mirror were physically stranded in runtime-storage-foundation despite being interaction/orchestration surfaces; action-snake-light-benchmark was physically stranded in Knowledge despite measuring the runtime flight recorder; research-pyramid-legacy-compat and capsule-manifest were physically present but absent from their manifests. .60 moves complete subsystem blocks only, preserving each block's internal text and install behavior; rebuilds all manifests from physical membership; adds explicit balanced NEAT-GROUP start/end markers; and strengthens source verification so the five group order, end markers, exact manifest membership, unique block coverage, and responsibility-map keys must agree. The maintained Journey now records the consolidated .52-.59 provider-fit evolution as one stable architectural arc rather than requiring future maintainers to reconstruct it from patch lore. No memory/retrieval/provider arithmetic, tier-gate behavior, Assistant authority, evidence semantics, encryption, backup, or model-size behavior changes. Journey/checkpoint governance advances to rev 125.
# 7.4.78.59 RESERVED REQUEST-RECEIPT VERIFICATION LANE follows the first full HIGH-pressure .58 stability run. The core architecture passed: actual RECENT tier gates reached 150k, Assistant selected REDUCE_RECENT_USE_HIGH, HIGH stayed HIGH, request #1 and request #2 each received fresh envelopes, preview/preflight input counts matched exactly, request hashes matched, and both provider calls completed. The remaining verification-integrity gap was narrower: `get_request_envelope_receipts` itself was blocked by ordinary read-result backpressure immediately after request #1, preventing Assistant from reopening the durable encrypted receipt precisely when pressure was highest. .59 adds no capacity arithmetic and changes no retrieval choice. Only the single-receipt form of `get_request_envelope_receipts` (limit omitted or limit=1) receives a tiny fixed 2k-token mechanical verification reserve. It may execute despite ordinary room being zero or an earlier read in the same batch being blocked. If that reserve is actually used, the tool phase closes immediately; subsequent reads are deferred and the next provider request must pass the existing per-request envelope reconciliation, hash parity, and hard preflight before any send. Multi-receipt reads (limit 2..5) stay on ordinary backpressure. The reader still returns only hashes/accounting/outcomes from the existing encrypted Black Box and duplicates no prompt, CHAT, or tool-result payload. No provider-safe limit is relaxed, no semantic evidence is truncated or summarized, no new authority store exists, and no model-size branch is introduced. Journey/checkpoint governance advances to rev 124.
# 7.4.78.58 RECENT TIER-BOUNDARY AUTHORIZATION GATES replaces the .50-era midpoint/retrogression UX after the operator simplified the intended contract. The old ratchet allowed RECENT to grow past an already-authorized tier to a midpoint (21k, 35k, 49k, ...), then asked Assistant whether to continue; DEFAULT subsequently moved the target backward to the lower tier. That created unnecessary state and made the console describe a grow-then-regress operation. .58 removes that mechanism. D remains 14,000 estimated tokens, but gates now sit on the actual authorized tier boundaries: 14k before expansion toward 28k, 28k before 42k, 42k before 56k, and so on. DEFAULT simply holds the current tier; CONTINUE authorizes only the next tier; OWN_PLAN/BYPASS remains available and is still bounded by the hard provider-safe limit. A selector failure is not treated as permission to cross a gate: the mechanical default holds the already-authorized tier visibly. Compatibility aliases remain for old ledger readers (`alerts`, `next_soft_alert_tokens`, `soft_regression_applied`), but their active semantics are tier-gate/hold semantics and no midpoint threshold is generated. The .56 per-request request-envelope coordinator and .57 encrypted request-envelope evidence receipts are unchanged. No new persistent authority, semantic ranking, provenance reinterpretation, or model-size branching is introduced. Journey/checkpoint governance advances to rev 123.
# 7.4.78.57 REQUEST ENVELOPE EVIDENCE RECEIPTS follows the first live verification audit after the .56 per-request coordinator redesign. No new arithmetic defect was established; instead Assistant correctly identified a proof-integrity gap: .56 enforced request SHA/input/tools/safe/output parity internally but retained its envelope receipts only in a process-local 16-row list, while broad Action Snake/records inspection could itself hit the ordinary evidence budget. .57 leaves capacity arithmetic and Assistant route ownership unchanged and promotes each actual-preflight-verified provider request into the existing encrypted append-only Black Box as one compact `request_envelope_evidence` event. The provider_call_id is generated before reconciliation, an envelope_id is deterministically bound to provider_call_id + request_seq + base request hash, and both identifiers are present in the request-scoped ledger before final hashing. The parity receipt stores only mechanical hashes/accounting: request/envelope/provider IDs, phase, Assistant route, retrieval depth, selected RECENT binding, RECENT before/after, preview vs actual request SHA-256, input/tool/ledger-overhead/safe/output values, and parity booleans. Raw prompts, CHAT text, and tool-result payloads are not duplicated into the receipt. Provider completion/error is appended as a compact Black Box outcome linked to the same event. A dedicated bounded read-only `get_request_envelope_receipts` tool returns at most five matching receipts by provider_call_id/envelope_id, avoiding broad Action Snake or records windows; each receipt also exposes its stable `record:blackbox-event:BBE-*` reference for independent reopening. Ledger-overhead equality is added to the existing parity gate so the durable proof covers the full accounting tuple Assistant requested. This uses the already-authoritative encrypted Black Box evidence plane and creates no new persistent authority store, no provenance reinterpretation, no semantic judgment, and no model-size branching. Journey/checkpoint governance advances to rev 122.
# 7.4.78.56 PER-REQUEST CONTEXT ENVELOPE COORDINATOR follows the live .55 targeted stress test. .55 proved its first-request contract exactly: Assistant chose REDUCE_RECENT_USE_HIGH, the final send-boundary preview and real preflight both measured 149,908/150,000 tokens with 15,022 tool-schema tokens, request parity matched, and the first provider call completed. The turn then entered tool-loop backpressure and a later synthesis provider request grew to 163,972 tokens; final preflight correctly blocked it because .55 stored `final_request_boundary_fit_verified=true` as a turn-level shortcut and later sends bypassed reconciliation. .56 redesigns fit lifetime rather than patching one branch. The Assistant-selected capacity route is turn-scoped (for example preserve HIGH and treat RECENT as the authorized elastic dimension), but every invocation of the provider-send wrapper receives a fresh process-local Request Envelope with monotonically increasing request_seq, mechanical phase, request hashes, tool shape, per-request RECENT target, and independent fit status. No previous request FIT receipt can bypass a later request. Each request is normalized to the route's current RECENT target when the selected FINAL packet is present, then the exact canonical preflight preview is rerun; later tool/synthesis requests may mechanically reduce RECENT further under the same Assistant authorization while HIGH remains HIGH. A final preview request SHA-256 is compared to the actual immediately-following real-preflight request SHA-256 plus exact input/tools/safe/output accounting; any mismatch blocks before provider send. If a request overflows and the authorized RECENT packet is absent, RECENT reaches zero, or no elastic route was selected, Python blocks and requires a new choice rather than changing another context source. The Context Budget Ledger now distinguishes the current request envelope from prior provider rounds so 15k-tool and zero-tool requests are not conflated. A zero-provider two-request regression deliberately grows the second request with a function-call result and requires request #2 to obtain a new envelope, reuse the route but not request #1's fit receipt, reduce RECENT further, preserve HIGH, and achieve exact hash/accounting parity independently. No persistent authority store, provenance semantics, model-size branching, or Python semantic judgment is introduced. Journey/checkpoint governance advances to rev 121.
# 7.4.78.55 FINAL REQUEST BOUNDARY RECONCILIATION follows the first live .54 selected-packet stress test. .54 fixed the .53 identity failure: Assistant selected REDUCE_RECENT_USE_HIGH, the correct FINAL HIGH RECENT packet was found, RECENT was mechanically reduced again, HIGH remained HIGH, and the provider request completed. The live console nevertheless exposed a smaller contract defect: the .54 "exact" preview measured 149,019 tokens, then the actual provider preflight measured 149,918 tokens (+899) because capsule-manifest packing text was appended after the earlier reconciliation. Final safety still worked and the request fit by 82 tokens, but exact-fit authority was still located too early. .55 moves normal-turn exact reconciliation into `_ora_responses_create_with_tpm_recovery` immediately before the real `_ora_request_preflight`, after every caller has already assembled its request instructions/input/tools/output kwargs. The earlier adaptive reconciler remains only as compatibility/test code and is no longer invoked by normal online chat. The final-boundary reconciler uses the selected FINAL RECENT packet binding, may further reduce only RECENT when Assistant selected a REDUCE_RECENT route, and uses `_ora_request_preflight(..., preview_only=True)` as the exact estimator. The real preflight runs immediately afterward on the same base request and Python compares input/tools/safe/output accounting exactly; any parity drift blocks before the provider call. Capsule Packing View now labels its own estimate PRE_FINAL_REQUEST_BOUNDARY so it is not mistaken for final authority. A zero-provider regression appends a synthetic post-adaptive packing surface and requires final-boundary reduction plus exact preview/immediate-preflight equality. No provenance semantics, model-size branching, persistent authority, or Python semantic selection is introduced. Journey/checkpoint governance advances to rev 120.
# 7.4.78.54 SELECTED RECENT PACKET BINDING follows the first live .53 canonical-fit stress test. .53 correctly stopped using an independent adaptive estimator as final authority and refused to send an oversized request, but its late exact reconciler still assumed the globally last-rendered RECENT block belonged to the selected retrieval candidate. Real LOW/MID/HIGH candidate construction and final context wrappers can render RECENT multiple times; the synthetic .53 regression manually made the global-last block identical to the selected context and therefore missed the live failure. .54 gives every RECENT materialization a deterministic turn/query + retrieval-level + phase binding. The FINAL materializer wraps the candidate's RECENT span in explicit begin/end markers and stores its components under that binding ID. Exact provider reconciliation derives the selected candidate's FINAL binding from the query and Assistant-selected retrieval level, requires exactly one matching bound span in the selected context, and may replace only that span when Assistant previously chose REDUCE_RECENT. Mutable `_RECENT_RENDERED_*_LAST` fields remain compatibility diagnostics and no longer authorize replacement. Missing or ambiguous selected-packet bindings still fail closed; Python never guesses another context span. The behavioral regression now deliberately poisons the global-last RECENT state while preserving a correctly bound HIGH candidate, proving exact reconciliation follows the selected packet rather than mutable global-last state. Canonical provider preview remains the sole exact fit estimator. No provenance semantics, model-size branching, persistent authority, or Python semantic selection is introduced. Journey/checkpoint governance advances to rev 119.
# 7.4.78.53 CANONICAL PROVIDER-FIT RECONCILIATION follows the first live .52 capacity-conflict stress test. The .52 rail correctly returned tradeoff ownership to Assistant: after RECENT soft-selector failure failed open to the 150k hard scan ceiling, Assistant explicitly chose REDUCE_RECENT_USE_HIGH and Python rebuilt HIGH with less RECENT. Expanded CHAT intake also worked (legacy 100-row/30k-character settings were reported as hints, not hard limits), and final provider safety correctly blocked the request before any API call. The defect was measurement authority: the conflict rail declared an adaptive rebuilt estimate of 149,947/150,000 tokens, while the later final provider preflight measured the real assembled request at 174,193 tokens because tool schemas, input items, wrappers/protocol overhead, and the model-visible ledger were not all represented by the rail's independent estimator. .53 establishes one authoritative provider-fit estimator. `_ora_request_preflight(..., preview_only=True)` executes the exact same full request measurement and self-referential ledger convergence as final preflight without sending or committing a provider request. After all adaptive wrappers finish, an Assistant-selected REDUCE_RECENT route is reconciled against the exact first Responses request shape, including tool schemas and ledger; Python may mechanically reduce only RECENT further because Assistant already selected that tradeoff, and it iterates until the canonical preview fits. KEEP_RECENT and OWN_PLAN are never silently rewritten if exact validation fails; the request is blocked and a new Assistant choice is required. The adaptive estimate remains visible but is explicitly provisional, while the final ledger separately reports canonical exact-provider validation. The misleading no-safe-current-RECENT default label is corrected from KEEP_RECENT to REDUCE_RECENT when every current-target depth overflows. No provenance semantics, model-size branching, persistent authority, or Python semantic selection is introduced. Journey/checkpoint governance advances to rev 118.
# 7.4.78.52 CONTEXT CAPACITY CONFLICT RAIL + COMPLETE MECHANICAL LEDGER follows the first live .51 stress test. The RECENT midpoint ratchet itself passed exactly: Assistant CONTINUEd through successive midpoint alerts and DEFAULT at 105k regressed only to the current 98k/7x tier. The .51 provider ledger also reached Assistant and its arithmetic was self-consistent. The remaining autonomy defect was elsewhere: base adaptive retrieval still automatically replaced Assistant's requested HIGH with the largest safe fitting level when the combined packet exceeded the hard safe-input budget, thereby choosing which context dimension to sacrifice. .52 preserves the non-overridable provider-safe boundary but adds a second explicit Assistant-owned capacity-conflict rail. Python presents current RECENT target, LOW/MID/HIGH measured packet sizes, overflow amounts, a safe keep-RECENT default, reduce-RECENT alternatives that preserve higher retrieval depth, and OWN_PLAN/BYPASS. If Assistant chooses a RECENT reduction, Python rebuilds and remeasures the chosen combination before accepting it; an arithmetic estimate alone never proves fit. Invalid/failed conflict choices may fall back only to an already-measured hard-limit-safe combination and are visibly labeled as mechanical hard-limit fallback, never semantic preference. The model-visible Context Budget Ledger is expanded to include configured CHAT hints versus whether they were actually hard-applied, raw CHAT availability/intake, RECENT target/tier/alert history/soft-vs-hard status, capacity-conflict choice, per-section soft-share/borrowing receipts, delivery reasons, and final provider accounting. Console wording now separates RECENT material available beyond the scan ceiling from a hard truncation actually applied. The ambiguous legacy `raw` adaptive metric is explicitly labeled as not the expanded RECENT CHAT intake. No provenance semantics, model-size branching, new persistent authority store, or Python semantic selection is added. Journey/checkpoint governance advances to rev 117.
# 7.4.78.51 CONTEXT BUDGET LEDGER + SOFT SECTION UNIFICATION follows two read-only online Assistant diagnostics over .50. The first proved RECENT itself could report zero soft/hard omission while context delivery was still incomplete because an independent section budget clipped material; the second showed Assistant could see some intermediate budget fields but not the full stage-by-stage accounting that the operator console already knew. Direct source inspection then resolved the unknowns mechanically: configured RECENT CHAT load/count bounds are applied before .50 assembly/ratcheting; the roughly 205-token RECENT available-vs-delivered difference is the mechanical soft-budget status envelope; `_fit_context_sections` applies per-section character ceilings before the selected adaptive packet is full; and later context wrappers reuse the selected adaptive total character ceiling. .51 therefore keeps LOW/MID/HIGH breadth selection intact but changes per-section budgets from hard walls into soft allocation shares that may borrow unused room from sibling sections up to the selected adaptive packet's total cap. Normal adaptive RECENT intake may expand past legacy configured CHAT count/character hints until the current provider-derived hard token scan ceiling, while explicit diagnostic bounds still behave explicitly. A process-local end-to-end Context Budget Ledger records raw CHAT availability/intake, RECENT content vs control overhead, selected section raw/delivered/omitted sizes, context-delivery loss signals, and final provider preflight numbers. Provider preflight injects a compact read-only ledger into Assistant's instructions before the answer call and then remeasures the request, so final input, tool-schema cost, output reserve, safe-input limit, remaining room, and projected total are no longer console-only. Console delivery summaries now distinguish whole sections skipped from section clip markers and omitted characters. Python still performs no semantic selection, no provenance reinterpretation, no model-size branching, and no new persistent authority store. Journey/checkpoint governance advances to rev 116.
# 7.4.78.50 PROGRESSIVE RECENT SOFT-BUDGET RATCHET follows the live local 9B Kestrel provenance audit and the realization that the model may be blamed for facts NeatMemory filtered before delivery. The .49 RECENT materializer still imposed a hard 14,000-character final slice even though the deployed profile exposes a 200,000-token model window and a 150,000-token NeatMemory safe-input ceiling; it also overrode the existing configurable raw-chat intake with an 8-row / 7,000-character projection and clipped each rendered turn to 2,200 characters. Those economical defaults could erase mechanical provenance/runtime evidence before Assistant judged it. .50 removes those hidden live hard projections from normal adaptive RECENT delivery and replaces the final bound with an Assistant-owned token soft-budget ratchet. The base soft anchor is D=14,000 estimated tokens. No alert fires before 1.5D=21,000; at each midpoint (1.5D, 2.5D, 3.5D, ...) Python shows Assistant the current alert size, full RECENT size available, lower retained tier, next tier, whole-input hard safe limit, and remaining distance to that hard limit. DEFAULT soft-regresses only to the lower tier for that alert (21k -> 14k, 35k -> 28k, 49k -> 42k); CONTINUE advances the authorized tier and the next midpoint; OWN_PLAN/BYPASS skips remaining soft alerts for the turn and may choose a bounded target. Selector failure fails open to preserve information instead of silently applying a soft omission. Python owns only measurement, tier arithmetic, deterministic newest-contiguous CHAT packing, structural provenance/runtime blocks, receipts, and hard provider limits; it does not decide semantic importance, truth, or model-size behavior. The existing configured RECENT row/aggregate-character settings remain honored as upstream user/mechanical settings, while the historical hard-coded 8/7000/2200 live overrides are removed. Context-delivery receipts record explicit Assistant soft regression without falsely calling it incomplete; only a true hard safe-input loss is an incomplete-delivery reason. Journey/checkpoint governance advances to rev 115.
# 7.4.78.49 EXACT-OBJECT PRIOR OUTPUT PROVENANCE BOUNDARY follows the live .48 woodchuck retest and a read-only online Assistant autodiagnostic. .48 correctly delivered PRIOR_MODEL_OUTPUT / UNVERIFIED_IN_RECENT / NONE_BY_RECENT_ALONE to the local Qwen 9B, yet the model still reused its earlier unsupported "3 million" prose and then treated an RF identifier mentioned inside prior assistant text as if it were a source-tracing object. The forensic diagnostic found the exact .48 labels in the delivered local context, identified the "3 million" object as a prior assistant CHAT turn, found no attached origin/source reference for that exact turn and no observed stored link from RF-20260818-031138-15f7bcb1 to the woodchuck claim, and found no NeatMemory provenance-selection operation that bound that RF to the claim. .49 therefore fixes the provenance boundary rather than adding another semantic memory rule: RECENT now retains the existing stable CHAT message_id/content hash, and provenance/origin questions receive a query-local read-only candidate surface that binds only mechanically matching prior assistant message spans with exact span coordinates/hashes. Repetition never increases their authority; prior output alone remains unverified; and RF/NB/BK/etc. identifiers merely mentioned inside prior model prose are explicitly CONTEXT_TEXT_ONLY and cannot become provenance edges unless separately selected/bound through a real Python evidence operation. The CHAT schema contains no recorded generation-origin source refs, so .49 reports that mechanical absence as NO_RECORDED_ORIGIN_BINDING_ON_CHAT_ROW rather than inventing an origin. Independent evidence may still verify a claim separately, but cannot retroactively prove where an earlier model output came from without a recorded binding. No claim ledger, mirror cache, new persistent store, semantic truth judgment, forced answer sentence, model-size branch, or deletion/rewrite of conversation is added. Journey/checkpoint governance advances to rev 114.
# 7.4.78.48 RECENT CONVERSATION AUTHORITY SEPARATION follows a live Qwen 9B reasoning trace that explicitly treated earlier assistant woodchuck hallucinations ("3 million" / "9 million") as established recent-conversation premises even after automatic Rear Mirror injection was disabled. The bug was in RECENT packaging: prior public turns were emitted only as plain [USER] and [ASSISTANT] text inside an "actual answer input" block, so prior assistant generation had no mechanical authority distinction from conversational evidence. RECENT now preserves the same raw text, chronology, and bounded delivery while tagging each prior user turn as USER_CONVERSATION_INPUT / USER_STATEMENT and each prior assistant turn as PRIOR_MODEL_OUTPUT / UNVERIFIED_IN_RECENT / NONE_BY_RECENT_ALONE. The retrieval director receives the same role metadata. The current task is mechanically anchored as CURRENT_USER_TURN. This is classification, not model teaching: no content is corrected, rewritten, deleted, or semantically judged; canonical evidence can still independently verify a prior assistant claim. Rear Mirror remains manual-only, decision rails are unchanged, and there is no model-size branching. Journey/checkpoint governance advances to rev 113.
# 7.4.78.47 REAR MIRROR MANUAL-ONLY CONTEXT ISOLATION follows Steeve's live-model finding that the automatically read Rear Mirror may bias or confuse small and mid-size models by carrying the previous operational action into every ordinary turn. Rear Mirror remains a derived read-only diagnostic over the encrypted Action Snake and /mirror plus /mirror full remain explicit operator commands, but _install_simple_rear_mirror no longer decorates build_runtime_context and normal chat/query packets receive no REAR MIRROR block automatically. Action Snake recording, integrity, retention, and authority are unchanged; no historical records are deleted. A compact rear_mirror_status exposes that auto_context_injection=false. This is context isolation only: it changes no retrieval ranking, lifecycle semantics, decision rails, provider protocol, or Assistant semantic authority. Journey/checkpoint governance advances to rev 112.
# 7.4.78.46 STRICT LOCAL CHAT PROTOCOL NORMALIZATION fixes a live LM Studio/Ministral protocol rejection where the model never began inference because its Jinja chat template required strict user/assistant alternation. The embedded local runtime trims public history from the tail by token budget, so a retained suffix can begin with an orphan assistant turn or contain adjacent same-role messages even though the semantic history itself is valid. The fix is provider-boundary only: every local chat.completions.create request is normalized after NeatMemory context/history/tool assembly and immediately before transport. Multiple system/developer messages are consolidated into one initial system message; adjacent ordinary user or assistant messages are concatenated without semantic reinterpretation; an orphan assistant/tool prefix created by tail trimming is omitted rather than promoted to system or replaced by a synthetic user; native assistant tool_calls and tool result IDs are preserved exactly. The normalized sequence is mechanically validated and fails visibly if it cannot be made safe. Online provider behavior, retrieval, authority, decision rails, and semantic content remain unchanged. Journey/checkpoint governance advances to rev 111.
# 7.4.78.45 OBJECT-LOCAL CHOICE GATES follows Steeve's correction that NeatMemory should not teach weak models governance prose or pre-trigger decisions from unrelated context. Search/navigation may expose candidates without forcing a choice. Once Assistant actually opens/selects a concrete object through an object-level read surface, Python reads that object's mechanical authority facts and presents a compact menu: one marked DEFAULT, valid alternatives, and universal OWN_PLAN/BYPASS. Assistant must explicitly pick; no valid pick after bounded retries stops safely and executes nothing. DEFAULT is easy for a weak model but never automatic, while a stronger model can override or bypass with a short reason. RECENT now carries hard authority facts only—no passive decision defaults or pre-answer reflection gate. The same object-local gate wraps both local execute_tool and the online evidence executor, with active backend/query state supplied by the current turn. Python still controls only mechanics and allowlisted read-only execution; it does not select meaning, promote lifecycle state, infer provenance, or choose a semantic conclusion. Journey/checkpoint governance advances to rev 110.
