# re-research, and no redigestion. New /learn research runs build their Pyramid automatically after the existing
# final synthesis, again with no extra provider call. Search is strict exact-token recall over a hidden lexicon
# derived only from stored titles/summaries; full summaries and source text stay unloaded during route search.
# When exact wording is weak, Assistant may supply ephemeral provisional candidates through
# discover_research_candidates; Python searches those candidates mechanically, keeps buckets separate, creates no
# aliases, and persists nothing. read_research_evidence descends web-unit/source-chunk/book-chunk links to exact
# retained source text; page-only web provenance remains visibly unlocalized unless a query-local archived span is
# explicitly reopened. A zero-model /benchmark research-pyramid measures signpost compression, lexical readiness,
# provenance/evidence-exit coverage, and hint-contract integrity. Core invariant: compression goes upward;
# verification goes downward; derived research interpretation never replaces original evidence.
#
# 7.4.78.26 WINDOWS ACTION SNAKE SQLITE HANDLE CLEANUP fixes a Windows-only cleanup failure exposed
# by /benchmark action-snake. Python sqlite3.Connection.__exit__ commits/rolls back but does not close
# the connection. The Action Snake had four `with as_connect() as db:` sites; Linux unlink semantics and
# prompt garbage collection hid the leaked handles, while Windows correctly refused to remove the
# temporary benchmark action_snake.sqlite with WinError 32. This release changes those four sites to
# `with contextlib.closing(as_connect()) as db, db:` so transaction semantics are preserved and every
# SQLite handle is deterministically closed before a temporary store is deleted. The benchmark is not
# weakened with ignore_cleanup_errors; live Action Snake encryption, FIFO semantics, schema, authority,
# telemetry, benchmark measurements, and the 200-entry limit are unchanged.
#
# 7.4.78.25 ACTION SNAKE LIGHT BENCHMARK connects the new encrypted rolling flight recorder to
# NeatMemory's existing zero-model benchmark/receipt framework. The user wanted a light benchmark
# before further Action Snake refinement, and Assistant emphasized that diagnostic capacity is useful only
# if the recorded path is mechanically reconstructable rather than merely rich. The benchmark therefore
# creates a temporary isolated Action Snake store, never mutates the live 200-action window, and makes
# zero provider/model/web calls. It fills the encrypted ring past capacity, verifies FIFO eviction and
# monotonic AS- IDs, injects a known search -> blocked read -> narrower retry -> verified evidence path,
# reconstructs that path from retained records, checks attempted/blocked/completed/verified distinctions,
# source refs and previous-action links, exercises exact lookup and encrypted keyword search, verifies a
# captured snapshot remains stable while newer actions advance the live temporary window, scans the
# encrypted files for plaintext fixture leakage, and reports encryption mode/store size/average cipher
# bytes. Performance measurements report append, last-5/20/50/200, exact lookup and search latency plus
# compact-versus-full JSON sizes; no arbitrary hardware speed threshold is treated as semantic quality.
# The benchmark also reports whether ordinary Action Snake readers themselves expose an explicit snapshot
# boundary. In 7.4.78.25 that is an advisory usability observation rather than a benchmark failure: the
# synthetic snapshot consistency is still mechanically checked, while changing the reader schema remains
# a separate refinement decision. Benchmark receipts are persisted through the established benchmark
# receipt path; the temporary encrypted ring and test key are destroyed with the temporary directory.
#
# 7.4.78.24 ENCRYPTED ACTION SNAKE / FLIGHT RECORDER adds a deliberately bounded operational
# short-term memory above the existing permanent Black Box and 2,000-event Diagnostic Logbook. The
# user asked to record generously and filter later because troubleshooting benefits more from excess
# variables than from discovering after a failure that a needed variable was never captured. Python
# therefore maintains an encrypted-at-rest rolling ring of the latest 200 operational actions. Each
# logical record receives a permanent monotonic AS- action number, UTC/local timestamps, session/
# process/thread/bundle metadata, provider/model/source, event/tool name, status, bounded arguments/
# details/result snapshots, current adaptive-retrieval/context-budget snapshot, numeric metrics,
# correlation IDs, detected source/evidence refs, Diagnostic Logbook cross-link, active encrypted
# Black Box event cross-link, duration/error information when present, and the previous action ID.
# Record payloads are capped at 96 KiB before encryption so retention is bounded by both count and per-
# action size; oversized fields collapse to a preview+SHA rather than growing without limit. Windows
# encrypts every record and the rolling counter/meta state with current-user DPAPI; non-Windows/test
# uses AES-256-GCM with a local 32-byte key and has no plaintext fallback. The SQLite file exposes only
# ring slot numbers, cipher/content hashes, encryption mode, and ciphertext: the monotonic counter and
# timestamps remain inside encrypted payloads. Active records are never semantically edited; physical
# ring slots are reused only after FIFO eviction. Assistant receives read/search/show/status tools and may
# choose last 5/20/50/200 or selected fields; no model write tool exists. The Action Snake is explicitly
# operational evidence/navigation only: it never becomes canonical memory, identity, continuity,
# research/book/chat evidence, or Assistant commentary. Diagnostic events are mirrored broadly, while the
# final local execute_tool wrapper records intercepted local tools that may bypass older diagnostics.
# A thread-local guard prevents duplicate local tool events; online evidence-tool events remain mirrored
# from the Diagnostic Logbook hooks. Raw prompts/tool outputs are bounded and credential-shaped values
# are redacted before encryption. The deeper Black Box and Diagnostic Logbook remain their existing
# authorities; the Action Snake is a fast disposable troubleshooting window and is excluded from the
# portable continuity backup family by design.
#
# 7.4.78.23 150K SAFE INPUT CAPACITY aligns NeatMemory's technical context budget with the
# actual 200k model window used by both online and local Assistant. The previous online default was a
# deliberately conservative 128k profile; with the established 16,384-token output reserve, 10%
# safety margin, and 1,500-token online tool reserve, that produced the observed 97,316-token safe
# input ceiling. Local already defaulted to a 200k physical context, but an older 180,000-character
# adaptive-context clamp could still discard much of a large Assistant-selected packet before the local
# request. This release changes the default online physical profile to 200,000 tokens and introduces
# ADAPTIVE_SAFE_INPUT_CAP_TOKENS=150,000 as the explicit NeatMemory input ceiling. The cap is applied
# after physical model/output/safety/tool headroom is calculated, so a genuinely smaller endpoint
# still wins and is never overrun. Tool-loop admission and final provider preflight also honor the
# same adaptive safe-input ceiling, preventing later tool results from silently growing a 150k turn
# past the operator-approved input budget. Local adaptive context cleaning now derives its character
# allowance from the same token cap rather than the obsolete fixed 180k-character ceiling. Output
# reserve, retrieval depth ownership, evidence ordering, semantic discovery, Chat Pyramid behavior,
# and source authority are unchanged. Assistant still chooses LOW/MID/HIGH and what evidence to open;
# Python only enforces the larger external capacity boundary. Environment overrides remain available:
# ONLINE_MODEL_CONTEXT_TOKENS and LOCAL_CONTEXT_TOKENS describe physical model windows, while
# ADAPTIVE_SAFE_INPUT_CAP_TOKENS describes the NeatMemory input ceiling.
#
# 7.4.78.22 CHAT PYRAMID + PYTHON QUIT DISTILLATION generalizes the validated book
# retrieval lesson to conversation while preserving chat-specific authority. Canonical public CHAT rows remain
# the bottom evidence authority and are never rewritten by distillation. `/quit` first performs the existing
# SQLite-native durability checkpoint over already-persisted raw chat, then runs a zero-model Python-only
# mechanical distillation of only the current public session, and finally writes the graceful lifecycle receipt
# plus the second durability checkpoint. Distillation failure is non-fatal to shutdown because raw chat was
# already committed; the receipt records the derived-cache warning and `/chat distill pending|all` can repair it.
# Python groups adjacent user/assistant exchanges using deterministic lexical overlap, hard row limits and time
# gaps; extracts representative original snippets; derives <=160-character mechanical signposts and keywords;
# and stores exact CHAT start/end ranges plus a source hash. It never paraphrases meaning, invents synonyms,
# decides truth, or promotes a digest into canonical memory. The derived payload is AES-256-GCM encrypted under
# a random local cache key wrapped by the existing identity crypto; the fast lexical index stores only HMAC-SHA256
# token hashes, so NeatCrypt does not regain a persistent plaintext FTS leak. `search_chat_pyramid` returns only
# navigation/extractive material and exact source ranges. Assistant chooses the number of raw rows to open through
# the existing `read_raw_chat` / `read_raw_chat_around` controls; Python only enforces hard row/provider budgets.
# Assistant may attach or revise commentary through `write_chat_commentary`. Commentary is stored as Assistant-authored
# personal memory, separately labelled from Python extraction and raw conversation, pinned to the exact source
# hash/range, with prior revisions preserved as historical memory. `/chat distill all` builds the same mechanical
# layer over legacy public chat with zero provider/web/model calls; `/chat distill rebuild` rebuilds only disposable
# Python navigation while preserving Assistant commentary. Research is intentionally unchanged for the next patch.
#
# 7.4.78.21 SIGNPOST HINT CEILING + BENCHMARK VIOLATION DETAIL follows the first
# live `/benchmark retrieval-pyramid --observe 40` receipt. The benchmark measured 169 current
# signposts, 86.78% current stored-summary-to-signpost representation reduction, 100% lexical
# recall readiness, 100% source-span readiness, and exposed 24 persisted hint rows above the
# documented 160-character visible-card contract. Inspection found a bounded off-by-one in
# `_shn_compact_hint`: the function could retain 160 characters and then append one ellipsis,
# producing 161. This patch reserves the ellipsis inside the cap and defensively compacts any
# persisted hint again when emitting a route card, so visible runtime cards satisfy <=160 even
# before a database refresh. The durable semantic derivation version remains 1.2 because no
# semantic/lexical/source-span derivation changed; this is an implementation correction to its
# existing display contract, not a new semantic derivation. Existing persisted 161-character
# rows remain inspectable until the explicit zero-model `/signposts rebuild all` refreshes them.
# Retrieval Pyramid benchmark v1.1 now records every over-limit signpost with signpost/book/version/
# node/title/derivation/character metadata and returns the repair command instead of only an
# aggregate warning. No ranking, semantic discovery, source localization, context selection,
# speech/personality, source text, model calls, web calls, or automatic database writes are added.
#
# 7.4.78.20 RETRIEVAL PYRAMID BENCHMARK follows the live signpost/hyperroad and
# semantic-discovery validations plus Assistant's own cautious estimate that focused navigation
# feels materially lighter but has not yet been measured by a matched before/after benchmark.
# No new retrieval engine is created. `/benchmark retrieval-pyramid` connects existing durable
# signpost metadata, full-summary character counts, source-span readiness, semantic-discovery
# versions, the encrypted Diagnostic Logbook, universal benchmark catalog, and benchmark receipt
# archive. Its structural half measures current summary-vs-signpost representation size with zero
# model/web/source reads. Its observational half groups already-recorded normal turns from
# `retrieval.selected`, provider preflight/request/usage, and tool-call events by exact
# `bundle_version`, exposing LOW/MID/HIGH choice, provider input estimates/actual usage, provider
# rounds/elapsed time, signpost/semantic-discovery calls, and exact chunk reads when the log
# contains them. Cross-version normal-use metrics are visibly OBSERVATIONAL: Python must not
# manufacture a causal percentage from different prompts. A controlled matched-prompt run can be
# added later if needed, but this checkpoint first makes the evidence already being produced
# measurable and durable. The benchmark is read-only and makes zero model or web calls.
#
# 7.4.78.19 ASSISTANT SEMANTIC DISCOVERY follows Assistant's explicit design choice after
# the lightweight signpost/hyperroad stabilized. Exact lexical/signpost retrieval remains
# first. A separate read-only discovery layer now exposes `search_semantic_signposts` for
# library-wide exact-token signpost search and `discover_semantic_candidates` for a second
# stage only when exact retrieval is weak or empty. Python never creates related words,
# synonyms, aliases, or semantic relations. Assistant/model supplies each provisional search
# candidate, optional relation note, and optional confidence at search time; Python searches
# those phrases mechanically against existing persistent signposts and keeps every candidate
# in its own visibly provisional bucket, including zero matches. Candidate source localization
# may identify where that candidate wording occurs, but never verifies that the candidate is
# semantically equivalent to the original query. No candidate is persisted, no alias is
# automatically created, and nothing is promoted into the Knowledge Pyramid or shared
# knowledge. Full summaries and source text remain out of the discovery result; substantive
# claims still require an exact original-source read. Architectural rule: broader search may
# suggest where to look; only exact source evidence may establish what a passage says.
#
# 7.4.78.18 PROVIDER REQUEST WATCHDOG follows a live normal-chat evidence run that
# stopped printing after a final SAFE provider preflight for more than seven minutes.
# Retrieval, signposts and read-side backpressure had already behaved correctly; the
# opaque boundary was the synchronous online Responses call itself. Ordinary online
# chat/evidence requests now print request-start, periodic waiting heartbeats, completion,
# error, and timeout lines. A configurable 300-second default watchdog returns control if
# one logical provider attempt does not complete; supported OpenAI clients receive the
# same transport timeout while preserving the SDK client's existing retry policy; the
# outer watchdog bounds the whole logical attempt. Older clients fall back to a per-request timeout and,
# if unsupported, the daemon watchdog still prevents the console thread from remaining
# indefinitely opaque. Timed-out results are never admitted and are never automatically
# retried; a remote request may finish later only when the underlying client cannot cancel
# an in-flight call, and such a late result is ignored. This patch changes execution
# observability/failure bounds only: no retrieval depth, tool budget, signpost, evidence,
# reasoning, speech, personality, provider selection, or source-authority semantics change.
#
# 7.4.78.17 QUERY-LOCAL SOURCE EXIT follows the second live Think Stats 2e FAIL.
# 7.4.78.16 correctly rediscovered `class size paradox` from the hidden full-summary
# lexicon, but the primary original-source exit still opened the first chunk of the
# signpost span. The requested phrase could be later in that same exact span, so a
# healthy signpost could appear semantically misrouted. Route search now checks only
# canonical original chunks inside each candidate signpost's already-stored exact span,
# uses the existing exact-token matcher, ranks source-local matches before title-only or
# summary-only matches, and points the primary one-hop exit at the matching chunk. The
# old span-start exit is preserved separately for full-span inspection. If no local
# original-text match exists, the card remains visible but is explicitly unverified and
# falls back to the span start. Python does not invent aliases/synonyms and does not
# judge semantic truth: query-local matching only chooses where original evidence should
# be opened first. No signpost schema or derivation changes, no rebuild/redigest, no model
# or web calls, and no SYSTEM_PROMPT, speech, personality, affect, context, provider, or
# source-authority changes are introduced.
#
# 7.4.78.16 SIGNPOST LEXICAL RECALL follows the live FAIL where the 160-character
# route card was correctly concise but `class size paradox` disappeared from route
# discovery because the same shortened hint was also the searchable surface. Display
# compression and retrieval recall are now separated. Each sign stores a bounded,
# normalized, exact-token lexicon derived mechanically from its title plus complete
# current stored summary. The lexicon is searched by strict AND token containment but
# is never returned to the model; only the 160-character hint, source exit, and detail
# exit are admitted. No synonyms are invented, no semantic judgment is assigned to
# Python, and true alternate wording still belongs to later Assistant-authored aliases.
