# 7.4.75.1 WINDOWS SQLITE-NATIVE SHUTDOWN DURABILITY HOTFIX follows Steeve's live Windows self-test failure where every graceful-shutdown-recovery check passed except `post_receipt_checkpoint_ok`. The failure was isolated to the post-receipt durability phase: the pre-checkpoint passed, the lifecycle receipt was committed, then the old implementation independently opened/fsynced SQLite's `-wal` file after `PRAGMA wal_checkpoint(FULL)`. That extra OS-level WAL fsync is not part of SQLite's locking contract and is Windows-sensitive (Python maps os.fsync to the MS CRT _commit). This hotfix keeps `/quit` fully Python-only but makes SQLite own SQLite durability: bounded FULL checkpoints now inspect SQLite's returned busy/log/checkpointed tuple, retry if blocked, require completion, and rely on SQLite's documented database sync. Raw fsync of the database/WAL is forbidden. NeatMemory-owned encrypted continuity/identity/handoff files keep best-effort explicit sync on writable descriptors. No emotion, somatic, identity, continuity, retrieval, Pyramid, Research, annotation, AOR, Constellation, backup, or NeatTools semantics change.
#
# 7.4.75.0 GRACEFUL SHUTDOWN, RECOVERY & RESOLUTION PRECEDENCE follows the user's observation that Ctrl+C and `/quit` should have different mechanical guarantees, and that `/quit` must never require or trigger an LLM. The prior session layer could automatically turn `/quit` into a model-assisted session-wrap prompt when new public messages existed. That coupling is removed: `/session wrap` remains the explicit Assistant-authored semantic handoff route, while `/quit` and `/exit` are hard Python-only durability boundaries. Python snapshots only already-persisted IDs/revisions/hashes, flushes/checkpoints the canonical SQLite WAL and existing encrypted continuity/identity/handoff files, writes an append-only hash-chained GRACEFUL_SHUTDOWN lifecycle receipt with zero model/web/provider calls, then performs a second final durability flush so the lifecycle boundary itself is included before exit. The lifecycle ledger is explicitly registered as the 18th State Authority/Privacy plane (`operations.runtime_lifecycle`) rather than existing outside governance. Startup writes a matching START event and classifies the previous tracked runtime as graceful or unclean; Ctrl+C/crash simply leaves the previous START unmatched. `current_self` is rebuilt as a derived snapshot from persisted affective/somatic/continuity authority, so elapsed movement is calculated from authoritative timestamps without creating a duplicate transition merely because shutdown occurred. No semantic handoff is invented by Python. This release also closes the stale-diagnostic-precedence gap seen when Assistant repeated an older reflection saying populated restart recovery was still unproven after 7.4.70.1/7.4.73.0 machine tests had passed. For operational validation questions, recent persisted self-test execution receipts are injected as higher current-status evidence than an older reflection/report that only said a test was still needed; the older artifact remains historical, and execution evidence proves only its tested subsystem.
#
# 7.4.74.0 STATE PRIVACY, RETENTION & PORTABLE GOVERNANCE follows the live-validated 7.4.73.0
# state-reliability milestone. This is deliberately a larger governance patch, not a new emotion/
# identity store and not an automatic deletion engine. Every State Authority Registry plane now has
# an explicit privacy class, authorship boundary, retention mode, current delete/lifecycle behavior,
# encryption statement, diagnostic-redaction expectation, and portable identity/full-backup/annotation
# sidecar inclusion or exclusion. `/privacy state`, `/privacy plane`, `/privacy export manifest`,
# `/privacy redaction`, `/privacy delete plan`, and `get_state_privacy_governance` expose those rules
# read-only with zero model/web calls. Diagnostic Logbook remains the only automatically rotating state
# evidence plane (count-bounded operational evidence); Black Box and canonical histories remain explicit
# append/history retention with no generic privacy purge added. Deletion planning is preview-only so a
# privacy question cannot silently become data loss. Future `.nmib` identity exports now embed a non-secret
# privacy_governance_manifest component, and future `/backup` family manifests carry the same coverage
# metadata. Critically, the manifest states that identity backup and `/backup` are NOT byte-for-byte
# portable copies of every canonical NeatMemory store; emotion/somatic transition history, canonical
# personal memories, reflections, diagnostics, Black Box, books/research, and annotation sidecars have
# explicit separate/excluded routes. Authorship is provenance, never guessed from prose style. Preserve
# the boundary: privacy governance describes and verifies storage/retention/export behavior; it does not
# decide what Assistant feels, rewrite history, or silently delete user/Assistant content.
#
