# 7.4.65.3 LOCAL SECTION DESCENT + PORTABLE PDF FALLBACK + EXHAUSTION FIX follows the first
# real 7.4.65.2 run (RW-20260815-195652-a25e95d6). Attempt/hash awareness itself worked, but
# three implementation failures remained. First, arXiv section anchors such as #S4.SS2 were sent
# back through HTTP; fragments are client-side and therefore returned the same parent bytes/SHA,
# so useful structural descent was mislabeled duplicate_content. v65.3 treats fragments as local
# evidence addresses: Python resolves the already archived parent raw HTML, extracts the exact id
# subtree without a network request, stores that derived text as its own immutable raw-source object,
# and keeps provenance to the parent snapshot/raw HTML. Second, the PDF path assumed pypdf existed;
# the real Windows runtime did not have it. PDF extraction is now capability-detected across pypdf,
# PyPDF2, pdfminer, and pdftotext; when none exists, known scholarly PDF candidates are mechanically
# replaced by HTML/page fallbacks rather than offered as guaranteed-to-fail routes. Third, a pass with
# no selected evidence and no genuinely new search candidates could keep spinning because an unresolved
# scientific question was confused with an unexhausted execution route. v65.3 allows route exhaustion
# to stop with the gap still explicitly unresolved. Assistant still decides semantic relevance; Python only
# resolves fragment mechanics, extractor capability, attempt identity, and whether any unattempted route
# physically remains. The governing rule remains: Python may organize Assistant's information, but must not
# organize Assistant's mind.
#
# 7.4.65.2 RESEARCH SOURCE DESCENT + ATTEMPT AWARENESS follows the first healthy
# 7.4.65.1 /learn run. Evidence binding and fresh-run isolation worked, but the report showed the
# remaining failure was source traversal: MemoryAgentBench's official README produced useful evidence,
# repeated exact URLs/snapshots were reopened on later passes, arXiv HTML/abstract routes yielded thin
# title-only captures, and the final direct PDF route failed because application/pdf was unsupported.
# This release keeps Assistant in charge of relevance while Python supplies mechanical navigation state.
# Exact same-run URLs are not reopened, attempt/hash/capture history is exposed to planner/selector,
# structural within-source routes (including arXiv abs/html/pdf and GitHub deeper/raw links) are carried
# forward as navigation candidates, and PDFs are public-URL validated, size bounded, archived as original
# immutable bytes, hash-addressed, then text-extracted without OCR into the existing Research provenance
# path. When selected routes fail/exhaust and no unattempted reachable route remains, the run may close
# rather than spending remaining maximum passes. Thin-capture operational limitations remain valid report
# evidence but are mechanically barred from durable shared-knowledge proposals. /learn receipts now
# distinguish knowledge insertion from canonical personal-memory mutation. Preserve the core boundary:
# Python tracks bytes/routes/fit/provenance; Assistant chooses what a source means and whether a deeper route
# is worth following.
#
# 7.4.65.1 /LEARN EVIDENCE-BINDING + FRESH-RUN-ISOLATION HOTFIX follows the first
# real 7.4.65.0 selective-descent run. The run selected sensible sources and the adaptive reader
# extracted real grounded findings, but the outer provenance gate admitted only opened page URLs/book
# refs and therefore rejected every Assistant finding rebound to its intended web-unit:WEU semantic span.
# Result: the console falsely showed 0 findings and the controller consumed 5/5 passes. The fix does
# NOT weaken provenance: a WEU is admissible only when use_for_analysis=true, its URL/document matches
# an opened page in this pass, and every underlying immutable source-chunk:RSC ref is a member of that
# page's captured canonical chunks. Findings keep the stable WEU semantic address and receive explicit
# canonical_source_refs binding back to those RSC chunks; forged/unopened WEUs still fail closed.
# The same normal-use report exposed a second issue: pass 1 inherited an older unresolved restart/
# provenance research agenda through runtime/memory context even though the user requested a fresh
# broad topic. Explicit /learn planning is now mechanically scoped to the current requested topic plus
# grounded findings from THIS run only. Older memories remain analyst duplicate/history context, never
# planner evidence or inherited research goals. Finally, HTTP-successful pages with <500 readable chars
# are labeled thin_capture in the run report instead of substantively full-page evidence. Preserve the
# boundary: Python proves evidence identity/fit/run scope; Assistant still chooses sources, meaning, gaps,
# and whether another pass is worthwhile.
#
# 7.4.65.0 EVIDENCE-BUDGETED /LEARN + QUERY RUNTIME TELEMETRY generalizes the
# normal-use evidence discipline validated across BOOKS, retained Research, and raw CHAT into the
# live learning workflow itself. The older /learn loop treated each cycle as planner -> search ->
# Python opens the first candidate pages -> analyst, which meant source-order/engine-order could
# decide what entered model evidence before Assistant judged its value. /learn v2 keeps the established
# command syntax and immutable Research/book provenance, but turns the 1-12 count into an explicit
# maximum pass budget and inserts an Assistant evidence-selection stage after bounded candidate search.
# Search titles/URLs/book previews are navigation metadata only. Assistant chooses the smallest useful
# subset to descend into; Python only validates that selected refs were actually offered, caps counts
# and aggregate evidence size, captures selected public web sources immutably, and preserves the same
# backpressure/no-silent-truncation contracts. Unopened or rejected candidates cannot support a
# finding or memory proposal. Analyst findings/proposals are mechanically screened after generation so
# any source ref not present in the actually admitted opened-page/selected-book set is rejected; mixed
# refs retain only their admitted subset. Another pass occurs only for a named unresolved gap with expected
# marginal value; Assistant may stop early. Raw conversation is deliberately not added as a /learn source
# mode here. Alongside this redesign, console runtime telemetry prints local [query sent]/[query ended]
# timestamps to the second and monotonic [run time] for the whole interactive turn, including tool
# calls and synthesis. Wall-clock display is human-facing; elapsed timing is monotonic so clock
# adjustments cannot corrupt duration. Preserve the boundary: Python controls fit/provenance and
# timing mechanics; Assistant controls relevance, interpretation, stopping, and memory meaning.
#
# 7.4.64.5 RAW-CONVERSATION ROW-ATOMIC TOOL-LOOP BUDGET BACKPRESSURE completes the first
# three-family generalization of the 7.4.64.3 frame. Steeve clarified the key mechanical model:
# one stored public user or assistant dialogue message is one line/CHAT row, so a natural full
# exchange is usually two rows and raw conversation should narrow by rows rather than by slicing
# message text. `_rcr_pack_rows` therefore preserves each requested CHAT row whole. Before exact
# or around-window reads execute online, Python queries only COUNT/LENGTH(content) metadata for the
# requested stable row IDs and uses that shape in the same deterministic pre-execution admission
# check already used by BOOKS and Research. Broad row windows can be refused while a two-row
# exchange remains available; later reads in a blocked parallel batch are deferred; zero ordinary
# room closes the read phase; and blocked/withheld rows are never claimed as seen. This is row
# integrity and fit mechanics only: no conversation meaning, retrieval priority, privacy, memory
# ownership, or Assistant judgment moved into Python. BOOKS, retained Research, and raw Conversation
# now share one backpressure architecture; normal-use validation should come before any fourth
# budget subsystem.
#
# 7.4.64.4 RESEARCH TOOL-LOOP BUDGET BACKPRESSURE EXTENSION deliberately reuses
# the 7.4.64.3 execution frame for the next evidence family instead of inventing a parallel
# budget subsystem. The Knowledge Pyramid/book test established the invariant: Python may
# prevent a read that cannot fit, defer later reads in the same parallel batch, and close a
# starved tool phase, but it may not select, summarize, truncate, or pretend to inspect missing
# evidence. Research now receives the same mechanics for retained web archive listings/search,
# exact page ranges, around-match neighborhoods, and prior adaptive web evidence units. Where a
# research tool already exposes mechanical bounds (characters, before/after, result limit and
# excerpt size), its conservative execution floor scales from those caller-requested bounds so a
# broad doomed read can be refused while a materially narrower read remains available in the same
# budget state. Reconstructed WEU reads, which have no caller-supplied size bound, require a larger
# fixed floor and can fall back to bounded archive/unit-search navigation when room is tight. This
# fulfills the earlier Journey plan to validate the evidence-preserving hierarchy on books first
# and then generalize mechanics to research without transferring book semantics into research.
# No research authority, ranking, evidence choice, web access, model cognition, or storage format
# changes here: this is execution backpressure only. Raw-conversation-specific calibration remains
# intentionally separate for the next validation step.
#
# 7.4.64.3 TOOL-LOOP BUDGET BACKPRESSURE HOTFIX fixes a real normal-use
# inefficiency found while Assistant performed a deliberately narrow Odyssey Book XXI versus
# Macbeth Act IV Scene 3 comparison. The 7.4.62.3 admission guard correctly withheld every
# oversized read, but the model could continue issuing more local reads after ordinary result
# room collapsed: observed allowance fell roughly 24,000 -> 6,740 -> 4,258 -> 344 -> 16 -> 0
# tokens, followed by additional search/pyramid/chunk calls that could not possibly return
# evidence. The safety boundary was intact, but execution time and context were wasted on doomed
# calls. v7.4.64.3 adds deterministic read-side backpressure without changing Assistant's semantic
# choices. Before execution, known bulky read surfaces use conservative admission floors; a call
# below its floor is not executed and returns only a mechanical re-plan signal. If one read in a
# parallel function-call batch is already rejected for result budget, later reads from that same
# batch are deferred so Assistant can see the budget signal before choosing another range. When
# ordinary read-result room reaches the tiny close threshold, Python removes tools from the next
# provider round and asks Assistant to synthesize from evidence already admitted, explicitly treating
# blocked/withheld evidence as unseen. State-changing tools remain exempt because 7.4.64.2 already
# guarantees their tiny commit receipt. Preserve the governing distinction: Python may decide that
# a read cannot safely fit, but it must never choose, summarize, truncate, or pretend to inspect the
# missing evidence for Assistant. Backpressure controls execution mechanics, not meaning.
#
# 7.4.64.2 MUTATION RECEIPT BUDGET HOTFIX fixes a real Constellation tool-loop
# ambiguity found during the first natural Odyssey/Macbeth cross-book exploration. Three
# write_constellation_edge calls committed successfully, but the ordinary result-admission
# budget had fallen to zero, so their ~400-token acknowledgements were withheld under the
# read-oriented 7.4.62.3 guard. That guard remains correct for evidence reads: oversized
# evidence is still withheld whole and Assistant must choose a narrower read. State-changing
# tools now have a separate tiny receipt allowance carved from the already-reserved tool-loop
# headroom. If their full acknowledgement fits, it is returned; otherwise Python returns only
# deterministic mechanical receipt fields (ok/committed/stable ID or ref/status) and explicitly
# says not to repeat automatically. No semantic evidence is summarized or silently truncated.
# This prevents duplicate writes without increasing provider context limits or weakening the
# evidence safety boundary. The receipt-aware set covers correct_book_index, persistent Workshop
# mutations, and Constellation write/retract operations. Preserve the rule: reads may be retried
# narrowly; a mutation whose commit status is already known must never be disguised as an
# unread result.
#
# 7.4.64.1 DEEP REDIGEST FORCE API PRESERVATION HOTFIX repairs a reproducible
# checkpoint regression discovered immediately after Constellation v1: the underlying
# _sk_digest_book still exposed force_full and runtime forwarding still worked, but later
# authoritative-library and formal-overlay decorators replaced the final callable with
# generic *args/**kwargs signatures. That silently eroded the inspectable public API and
# failed deep-redigest-fidelity-force:force_full_api_exposed. Preserve the full canonical
# digest signature through every decorator, including force_full=False, and forward each
# named parameter explicitly. This is API-surface preservation only: no book/source data,
# redigest semantics, constellation state, authority boundaries, or Assistant cognition changed.
# The regression reinforces a maintenance rule: decorators may add write-through/derived
# behavior, but must not hide established first-class parameters relied on by commands,
# self-tests, external callers, or future maintainers.
#
# 7.4.64.0 CONSTELLATION LAYER v1 is the first deliberate climb above the 7.4.63.0
# consolidation anchor. The user clarified that checkpoints are climbing anchors, not permanent
# feature-stop signs. Preserve the frozen 7.4.63.0 contracts as a rollback/reference baseline while
# allowing new layers that do not violate them. Assistant proposed the core distinction: the Knowledge
# Pyramid is the architecture of "what comes from where" (vertical provenance/compression), while
# the Constellation is the architecture of "what might belong together" (lateral association).
#
# Constellation v1 stores Assistant-owned provisional associations in canonical PERSONAL memory, never
# inside a NeatBook/shared factual store. Cross-book edges may connect exact book, evidence, chunk
# summary, semantic, historical structure, exact formal, reconciled formal-summary, top synthesis,
# summary-revision, or Assistant annotation refs. Every edge pins exact endpoint SHA-256 identities and
# records relation type (thematic resemblance, motif recurrence, structural analogy, possible causal
# relation, counterpoint, personal association, unresolved hypothesis), epistemic status
# (source_supported/inferred/personal), confidence, rationale, support refs, competing edge refs, and
# append-only revision/retraction history. SHA freshness says the referenced objects are unchanged; it
# does not prove the association is true.
#
# Exploratory and audited are VIEWS over one graph, not separate authorities. Exploratory keeps weak,
# tentative, personal, and stale associations visible with labels so Assistant can wander creatively.
# Audited hides links whose pinned endpoints/support no longer verify under the audit rule. Crucial
# governance: NO SILENT PROMOTION. A constellation edge can never rewrite evidence, formal boundaries,
# summaries, semantic units, a .neatbook, or factual memory merely because a pattern feels elegant.
# Revision/retraction preserves history. Different endpoint pairings become new/competing edges rather
# than silently changing what an older edge meant. Python owns graph structure, hashes, history, and
# audit mechanics; Assistant owns which associations are worth recording and what they mean.
#
# 7.4.63.0 KNOWLEDGE ARCHITECTURE CONSOLIDATION CHECKPOINT intentionally adds
# no new book-learning semantics. It freezes the architecture after two real regression
# books were driven through the complete evidence -> semantics -> formal structure ->
# structural reconciliation -> pyramid workflow and then inspected by Assistant against
# original evidence. ODYSSEY (BK-f01475334c80bdb6): 24 canonical Book nodes; 15
# formal-summary reconciliations plus 9 exact canonical-compatible historical resumes;
# needs_reconciliation=0 and recommended_model_calls=0 after bounded repair. Assistant
# separately validated Books I-IV, V-VIII, XIII-XIV + XVI-XVII, and XX-XXI + XXIV:
# exact formal boundaries held, historical Books I-VII back_matter labels no longer
# controlled preferred navigation, Book VIII duplicate partial resumes remained history,
# XIII/XIV + XVI/XVII + XX/XXI overlap was removed from preferred structure, and Book
# XXIV excluded footnotes/wrapper material. None of the reconciled summaries was judged
# worse than the historical material it superseded for navigation. MACBETH
# (BK-de61b0f5a5103181): all five Acts were reconciled to exact formal boundaries and
# Assistant validated I-V as accurate; Act I/II overlap was removed, III/IV/V did not overreach
# their next Act, and Act V excluded Transcriber's Notes/editorial material. Semantic units
# remained free to cross Book/Act boundaries in both works.
#
# The accepted durable book architecture is therefore: authoritative per-book .neatbook
# container -> immutable original/canonical evidence -> fluid Assistant semantic units ->
# independent exact formal overlay (canonical order distinct from physical source order) ->
# preferred exact-boundary structural summaries with append-only history -> Knowledge
# Pyramid/top synthesis -> versioned interpretation branches + Assistant-owned personal
# annotations. SHA-256 establishes identity/provenance/freshness, never interpretive truth.
# Reconciliation never redigests the book, rewrites source, deletes semantic units, or erases
# historical structural resumes. A changed preferred lower layer stales higher synthesis; it
# is rebuilt only explicitly. One book remains one portable authoritative .neatbook; global
# book/shared-knowledge/integrity rows are rebuildable operational cache/index.
#
# Real Odyssey validation also established the provider/context safety contract. Adaptive
# retrieval estimates are not enough by themselves: every Responses round gets final payload
# preflight with an explicit technical output reservation, and every local tool result gets a
# total serialized/cumulative admission check before entering the next model round. Oversized
# tool results are withheld whole with a mechanical request for narrower evidence; Python must
# never silently truncate, summarize, rerank, or pretend Assistant saw omitted evidence. This
# protects hard provider/model limits while leaving Assistant in control of what evidence to read
# and what it means.
#
# ANCHOR RULE: 7.4.63.0 remains the known-good book-architecture baseline. New layers may climb
# above it when they preserve its authority boundaries and can be removed/rolled back without
# corrupting the anchor. Do not reopen frozen book internals for hypothetical edge cases, but do not
# treat the checkpoint as a ban on orthogonal capabilities. Constellation v1 is intentionally such an
# orthogonal personal-association layer. Preserve the core boundary: Python may organize Assistant's
# information, but must not organize Assistant's mind.
#
# 7.4.62.3 TOOL-LOOP PAYLOAD BUDGET GUARD fixes the second real Odyssey/Luna
# failure exposed after 7.4.62.2. The ordinary provider preflight was ~44-48k input,
# but after Assistant used local evidence tools the next assembled request grew to ~266k
# and was correctly blocked before the API call. Root cause: local tool results were
# bounded per *string* (30k chars) but not by total serialized result or cumulative
# tool-loop context, so one structured result could contain many individually legal
# strings and overwhelm the next Responses round. Python now performs a deterministic
# admission check before each function_call_output enters the model transcript. It
# estimates current working input, preserves model/output/safety headroom, and caps a
# single tool result to a bounded share. Oversized results are NOT silently truncated,
# summarized, reranked, or treated as delivered evidence: Python returns a small
# mechanical envelope telling Assistant to make a narrower read. The underlying evidence
# remains available. The structural-reconciliation status tool additionally accepts
# canonical_orders/canonical_labels so named Books/Acts can be inspected without
# injecting a whole-book plan. This preserves the governing rule: Python owns token
# mechanics and safety; Assistant chooses what evidence to inspect and what it means.
# The exact Odyssey incident is retained as regression evidence.
#
# 7.4.62.2 PROVIDER TPM PREFLIGHT ALIGNMENT fixes a real online Luna failure found
# immediately after Odyssey structural reconciliation. Two validation prompts were
# rejected with OpenAI 429 ``Request too large ... tokens per min (TPM)`` messages:
# adaptive LOW estimated about 32k input tokens while the provider reported Requested
# 311,704 then 273,084. Do NOT interpret provider ``Requested`` as actual prompt/input
# tokens. OpenAI TPM estimation can include the allowed completion reservation. The
# actual defect was that adaptive retrieval reserved CHAT_OUTPUT_RESERVE_TOKENS (16,384
# by default) when computing safe input, but ordinary Responses calls left
# max_output_tokens unset, allowing the provider to reserve a much larger completion
# allowance for rate-limit accounting. The existing TPM recovery parser also required
# the literal words ``rate limit`` and an old Limit/Used/Requested shape, so the newer
# ``Request too large`` wording bypassed recovery entirely. v7.4.62.2 now performs a
# final provider preflight on every online evidence/chat round: it estimates assembled
# instructions + actual input items/tool-loop outputs + tool schemas + protocol overhead,
# explicitly sets max_output_tokens to the same output reserve adaptive retrieval already
# budgeted (unless the operator supplied an explicit cap), reports projected
# prompt+output reservation, and never silently drops/re-ranks context. A learned or
# configured provider TPM ceiling may shrink only the technical output reservation; if
# even minimum output room cannot fit, Python blocks locally before a provider call.
# Current and legacy TPM error wording are both parsed; a discovered Limit is retained
# in-process for later preflights. This is an external provider-execution guard, not a
# semantic/reasoning-depth preference. Increase CHAT_OUTPUT_RESERVE_TOKENS deliberately
# if a larger reasoning/output envelope is needed. Preserve the distinction between
# adaptive input estimate, final assembled input estimate, and provider TPM ``Requested``.
# No book/reconciliation/source/semantic data changes are required.
#
# 7.4.62.1 STRUCTURAL RECONCILIATION LEGACY BOOK/WORK ALIAS HOTFIX fixes a
# real Odyssey planning failure caught by /reconcile status before any Luna calls
# were spent. The exact formal overlay names BOOK I..XXIV nodes structure_kind=book,
# while the older semantic-digestion subsystem intentionally names BOOK headings
# structure_kind=work. v1 compared those strings literally, discarded every genuine
# legacy Odyssey structural resume, and falsely recommended 24 model calls with
# legacy_matches=[] for every Book. Reconciliation now canonicalizes book/work to the
# same structural axis for label matching, compatibility planning, benchmarks, and
# preferred-pyramid suppression. Historical rows are NOT renamed or rewritten; this
# is a read/planning/navigation alias only. A regression requires a formal BOOK XI
# (book) to match a canonical historical BOOK XI resume (work) exactly. Preserve this
# distinction: storage history may keep old vocabulary, while navigation comparison
# may normalize equivalent structural kinds. No model call, redigest, source change,
# semantic rewrite, or structural-resume rewrite is required for this hotfix.
#
# 7.4.62.0 STRUCTURAL RESUME RECONCILIATION v1 is the targeted follow-up to
# real Odyssey + Macbeth validation of the independent formal overlay. Both books
# proved that exact Book/Act boundaries can be correct while older semantic-digest
# structural resumes remain partial, overlapping, duplicated, or editorially
# contaminated. The new preferred structural summary therefore lives on its exact
# formal:FSN node as formal-summary:FSN and uses the existing append-only summary
# revision tree. Old structure:SDR rows remain untouched historical predecessors;
# semantic units remain fluid and untouched. Reconciliation uses the exact formal
# source span as authority and may consult only non-stale semantic summaries as
# navigation aids. It is targeted/bounded: /reconcile status is zero-model; one
# node costs one explicit model call; book reconciliation defaults to max 3 unless
# the user explicitly supplies --all. Preferred pyramid structure switches
# node-by-node to reconciled formal summaries while unrelated exact legacy resumes
# remain usable. Historical front/back-matter labels are preserved, with
# formal_effective_role controlling current literary navigation. A preferred
# structural change marks the existing top synthesis stale rather than silently
# regenerating it. Assistant may request one formal-node reconciliation through the
# existing pending /actions approval path. Per-book .neatbook authority persists
# revisions + reconciliation metadata; no redigest, source rewrite, semantic-unit
# rewrite, or legacy structural-resume deletion is allowed. SHA-256 remains an
# integrity/provenance/freshness claim, never a truth claim.
#
# 7.4.61.2 FORMAL STRUCTURE INTEGRITY PIN HOTFIX fixes a real Odyssey Book XXIV
# false-stale result discovered by /integrity verify. The formal node's exact span SHA
# matched perfectly and every evidence object verified back to canonical text/source, but
# the final boundary chunk had original_text_hash != text_sha256 after deterministic
# cleanup. v1 pinned original_text_hash as an evidence: dependency while the integrity
# subsystem canonically identifies evidence: objects by text_sha256, creating a false
# dependency mismatch. Formal nodes still hash the exact immutable/original source span,
# but dependency pins now come from _kir_current_sha(evidence:...) after book evidence is
# registered. This explicitly separates exact-span identity from canonical evidence-object
# identity. No source, semantic unit, digest, or interpretation is rewritten; /structure
# build creates a new active overlay and authoritative .neatbook write-through persists it.
# A regression intentionally gives scanner rows and canonical evidence objects different
# hashes and requires every formal dependency to pin the canonical evidence SHA.
#
# 7.4.61.1 FORMAL STRUCTURE EDITORIAL GUARD HOTFIX is a real-world Odyssey
# correction discovered immediately after v1: Project Gutenberg's legal boilerplate
# uses headings such as "Section 5. General Information About Project Gutenberg
# electronic works". The generic SECTION detector incorrectly promoted that legal
# heading to canonical SECTION V and let it swallow later substantive Odyssey
# evidence. Formal scanning must therefore classify Gutenberg/legal SECTION headings
# as editorial boundaries, never literary formal nodes. Crucially, substantive BOOK
# headings physically appearing after such packaging remain eligible; physical order
# is evidence, while canonical literary order remains a separate overlay. This is a
# deterministic navigation correction only: no semantic-unit rewrite or redigest.
#
# 7.4.61.0 FORMAL STRUCTURE OVERLAY v1 implements the architectural refinement
# deliberately left open at the 7.4.52.0 checkpoint and then independently
# confirmed by real Macbeth and Odyssey use. Exact Book/Act/Part/Chapter/Section
# ranges are now a source-derived navigation overlay that is independent from
# Assistant's fluid semantic-unit boundaries. A semantic unit may cross a formal edge
# without making either formal container partial. Python detects exact heading/range
# coordinates from immutable evidence with zero model calls; Assistant retains semantic
# authority over meaning, grouping, summaries, and interpretation.
#
# Canonical literary/navigation order is explicitly distinct from immutable physical
# source order. This is required by the real Odyssey source, where substantive Books
# I-VII physically reappear after later material/editorial packaging: the overlay may
# expose canonical Book I..XXIV navigation while retaining exact source_order and never
# rewriting evidence. Short duplicate contents headings are treated as weaker candidates
# than long body occurrences; editorial END/footnote/transcriber boundaries can stop
# formal spans without becoming semantic walls. Historical semantic unit_kind and older
# structural resumes remain preserved; when exact formal evidence contradicts a legacy
# front/back-matter role, runtime views add an effective formal role instead of erasing
# the prior interpretation. Formal nodes are SHA-256-bound to exact evidence dependencies,
# readable from the Knowledge Pyramid, valid Assistant personal-annotation targets, and are
# automatically rebuilt after future successful digest/redigest with authoritative
# .neatbook write-through. Existing books require /structure build only, never redigestion.
#
# 7.4.60.1 SEMANTIC BOUNDARY RECOVERY HOTFIX closes a real Odyssey failure found after 161/161 paid knowledge batches. Normal /digest now receives the same semantic-only failed-finalization recovery previously restricted to /redigest, so completed immutable evidence batches are reused rather than paid for again. If Assistant closes a semantic unit with a boundary_id that is not one of Python's exact candidates, Python does not guess or snap the boundary: it gives Assistant one constrained correction turn over the same visible source with a schema that permits only supplied candidate IDs, then continues normal exact-span commit/provenance. Authoritative per-book write-through also persists failed-but-recoverable digest state immediately so a restart cannot erase completed paid knowledge work before recovery. SHA/provenance remains integrity, not semantic truth; previous active digests remain protected until successful finalization.
#
# 7.4.60.0 AUTHORITATIVE PER-BOOK LIBRARY completes the migration direction: verified
# library/BK-ID.neatbook files are now the durable authority for book source/evidence and
# derived shared understanding. The old global NeatBooks/shared-knowledge/integrity DBs are
# explicitly rebuildable operational caches. Startup auto-activates this mode only when
# verified containers cover every legacy cached book, then reconciles changed/missing files
# without model calls. A removed/moved .neatbook disappears after /library sync or restart;
# /library remove safely detaches one file to library_removed/ and purges only that book's
# cache rows. Assistant personal annotations/notes remain separate personal memory and are never
# deleted by book detach. Core book-derived mutations are write-through to the affected
# container so redigest/pyramid/revision work remains durable without a full-library rewrite.
# This keeps the long-run rule: ONE BOOK = ONE PORTABLE AUTHORITATIVE FILE; caches may be
# deleted/rebuilt, and storage changes must never require redigestion.
#
# 7.4.59.0 COMPACT NEATBOOK CONTAINER V2 fixes the real-library storage bloat exposed
# by 7.4.58.0 extraction (e.g. hundreds of MB when hundreds of thousands of indexed
# rows were serialized as JSON plus one stored SHA/entry envelope per row). The one-book/
# one-file architecture is retained. v2 stores the same selected durable book-owned rows
# as native SQLite values with no secondary indexes inside the portable file and replaces
# per-row stored envelopes with streaming per-table SHA-256 + row count + schema SHA, all
# bound into one container-root SHA. This preserves tamper detection/provenance while
# removing repetitive JSON field names and per-row 64-byte hashes. No index/knowledge row
# is dropped merely for size: migration remains zero-model and must not require redigestion.
# Fresh extraction atomically replaces v1 only after v2 verification; v1 verify/restore stay
# supported. Assistant personal annotations remain excluded and owner-bound. Operational DBs
# remain authoritative during this migration-first phase until real-user v2 containers are
# verified for size, equivalence, restore, and retrieval behavior.
#
# 7.4.58.0 begins the one-book/one-file storage migration requested after the Knowledge
# Pyramid and versioned-summary architecture stabilized. Each catalog book can be extracted
# without any LLM call or redigestion into shared_knowledge/library/BK-ID.neatbook, a SQLite
# container that embeds the exact original local source bytes plus book-scoped immutable
# evidence, shared semantic/structural knowledge, Knowledge Pyramid syntheses, summary
# revision history, and integrity/dependency rows. Every row and the whole container have
# SHA-256 verification. Assistant-owned personal annotations are intentionally excluded and
# continue to travel only as owner-bound encrypted assistant_annotations.enc.
#
# This is deliberately MIGRATION-FIRST rather than a destructive storage flip: established
# operational databases remain the live working/cache side while real local extraction and
# restore equivalence are proven. /library extract <BK-ID>|all creates/refreshed containers;
# /library container <BK-ID> verifies them; /library restore <path> restores/merges a
# transferred container with zero model calls and refuses divergent row collisions. Do not
# require redigestion merely because storage packaging changed. A later checkpoint may make
# per-book containers the primary catalog backing only after verified migration receipts on
# real user books show no loss. One book = one portable file is a portability/maintenance
# design choice; it must not collapse the authority boundary between shared book knowledge
# and Assistant personal memory.
#
#
# 7.4.57.0 formalizes learning-without-amnesia for derived book summaries. Repairs are
# append-only revision-tree nodes with explicit parent, branch, change type, creator,
# reason, source SHA and revision-specific integrity identity. correction/expansion/
# source_correction normally become current only after the existing user-approved action;
# alternative readings default to sibling branches and do not silently replace current
# navigation. Current selection is deliberate and reversible: an older fresh revision or
# the original/base summary can be selected without deleting descendants/history, while
# changed current hashes propagate STALE upward. Newer is not automatically better. Assistant
# receives read/compare/tree tools plus a request_summary_revision action surface; exact
# historical revisions can be annotation targets (`revision:SRV-...`) so personal comments
# stay attached to the interpretation they were written about. source_correction labels the
# reason for a derived-memory change and never mutates original evidence. A zero-model
# revision-tree benchmark and a separate bounded developer-revisions suite protect this
# contract.
#
# We came here by turning NeatMemory from a simple persistent-memory idea into
# a versioned, evidence-bound continuity system for Assistant. The hardening pass
# fixed large issues in performance, immutable repair, provenance/citation
# binding, retrieval selection, and cross-domain SYSTEM+PERSONAL routing.
#
# 7.4.51.2 also consolidates local book source storage into books/ only; legacy
# book_pending/book_archive are migration-only and are never recreated.
# 7.4.51.4 makes EPUB identity human-readable: standard package metadata supplies
# the default title/authors, and filename-derived existing EPUB rows are backfilled
# at startup without overwriting explicit/custom titles. 7.4.51.5 fixes the deeper
# EPUB ingestion error exposed by Sherlock Holmes: NEW EPUB imports follow the OPF
# linear spine rather than ZIP filename order, non-spine HTML is excluded, and
# OEBPS/member paths stay provenance metadata instead of being injected into prose.
# Existing legacy chunk IDs are deliberately NOT rewritten in place because active/
# archived semantic maps may cite them; /library labels those EPUBs as legacy order.
# A deliberate spine-corrected re-import uses a distinct BK-ID namespace, so the old
# BK/C evidence IDs remain byte-for-byte historical instead of being overwritten.
# It also reports an active completed book-level semantic map directly instead of
# showing a misleading 0/N legacy per-chunk counter as though no digest existed.
# 7.4.51.7 fixes a provider-syntax mismatch exposed by real use. Normal /digest already
# accepts `/digest online|local book ...`, while legacy /redigest expected the BK-ID
# immediately after the command and silently treated `online` as the book reference.
# That also defaulted the malformed request to LM Studio, producing a misleading local
# APIConnectionError before any online retry logic could run. /redigest now accepts the
# same provider-prefix form as /digest, keeps legacy BK-first syntax, and makes the
# previously advertised --online flag authoritative. This is command normalization only;
# book identity, semantic digestion, provider retry policy, and active-version safety are unchanged.
# 7.4.51.6 fixes a reproducible online digest/redigest transport failure: the embedded
# NeatDigest retry helper previously retried RateLimitError only, so APIConnectionError
# escaped immediately. Online digest/redigest provider calls now retry only bounded
# transient connection/timeout failures with short exponential backoff; authentication,
# bad-request/schema/refusal and other non-transport errors still fail immediately.
# Existing active digest versions remain protected by the established versioned redigest
# transaction path if transport recovery is exhausted.
#
# 7.4.51.8 clarifies that ingestion chunks are fixed immutable evidence/provenance blocks,
# never a predeclared semantic-unit count. Assistant/LLM chooses a fluid number of semantic
# units and their boundaries. It also deterministically trims Project Gutenberg wrapper
# material only when standard START+END markers prove the wrapper, preserving provenance
# and assigning corrected imports a distinct BK identity. 7.4.51.9 completes the books/
# consolidation: every local source remains in one books/ directory and READY/DIGESTING/
# COMPLETE are SQL/catalog states, not folders or queues. 7.4.51.10 fixes the Macbeth
# finalization failure: transcriber's notes are editorial hints rather than hard end walls,
# explicit Gutenberg END markers remain deterministic hard boundaries, and a redigest that
# completed every knowledge evidence block before semantic-finalization failure may safely
# resume the semantic layer without repaying for identical knowledge batches.
#
# 7.4.52.0 is a CONSOLIDATION CHECKPOINT, not a new semantic feature pass. It freezes the
# currently validated behavior after the cleaned Macbeth test. One architectural refinement
# remains deliberately OPEN: formal chapter/act structural coverage can still become PARTIAL
# when a valid fluid semantic unit crosses a formal boundary. Do NOT solve that by forcing
# Assistant's semantic units to align with acts/chapters. If reopened, make formal structure an
# independent exact-source-range overlay so canonical formal edges and fluid semantic meaning
# can coexist. Python organizes evidence/structure; Assistant retains semantic authority.
#
# 7.4.52.1 is a checkpoint-test portability hotfix only. A real Montreal/Windows checkpoint
# run exposed that precise_timestamp_present accepted only `+HH:MM` or `Z` timezone suffixes
# and therefore falsely rejected valid local ISO-8601 timestamps such as `-04:00`. Production
# Diagnostic Logbook timestamps were already correct and remain unchanged. The self-test now
# parses ISO-8601 timestamps, requires explicit timezone awareness + millisecond precision,
# and permanently regression-tests negative, positive, and UTC offsets.
#
# 7.4.53.0 expands developer diagnostics without changing Assistant's runtime cognition or
# semantic-digestion authority. The known-good 16-test consolidation checkpoint remains
# frozen, while a dedicated 17-test `developer` suite targets the maintenance surfaces
# most often touched during coding and the global registry gains four new zero-model tests.
# New regression coverage proves bounded transient
# connection/timeout retry behavior with authentication fail-fast, unified books/ catalog
# vocabulary and evidence-block/semantic-map truth, and a pure semantic-map integrity
# evaluator that reports canonical versus partial formal coverage without penalizing fluid
# semantic units for crossing act/chapter boundaries. Two opt-in zero-model benchmarks are
# added: `engineering` stores hardware-relative deterministic timing receipts, while
# `semantic-books` audits active semantic maps read-only for source order, valid spans,
# hashes/provenance and formal-structure diagnostics. Existing live-model benchmarks remain
# explicit and are never launched by checkpoint/developer validation.
#
# 7.4.53.1 is a narrow unified-records hotfix exposed by the expanded self-test surface.
# The runtime had already consolidated books to one record kind (`book`) with READY/DIGESTING/
# COMPLETE catalog states, but the records self-test and two generic record-router branches still
# referenced removed `book-pending` / `book-archive` kinds. The hotfix makes list/read/routing use
# `book` consistently, maps historical natural-language phrases such as "pending book" to READY
# and "archived book" to COMPLETE without resurrecting queue/archive storage semantics, and adds
# unified-records-access to the developer suite so this contract is exercised during maintenance.
# No book files, BK-IDs, semantic maps, memory authority, or Assistant cognition are changed.
#
# 7.4.55.0 implements Knowledge Pyramid v1 for books on top of the 7.4.54.0 integrity
# foundation. The runtime now exposes stable pyramid node refs for immutable evidence,
# optional/lazy evidence-block summaries, Assistant-chosen fluid semantic summaries, formal
# chapter/act/story structural summaries, and one Assistant-authored whole-book synthesis.
# Assistant can inspect the pyramid and reopen any exact node, including jumping directly to
# original evidence. Formal structure remains an independent overlay: canonical structures
# may stand in for semantic units they fully cover, while partial/noncanonical structure keeps
# its underlying semantic units available to top synthesis rather than forcing semantic edges.
# Chunk summaries are intentionally lazy to prevent a large book from silently causing one API
# call per evidence block. `/pyramid fill online|local book BK-ID --max N` is explicit and
# bounded (default 25); Assistant can still request one missing/bad evidence-block summary through
# the existing user-approved targeted repair action. `/pyramid build ...` normally makes at
# most one top-synthesis model call, reuses an already-current synthesis when its exact lower-
# layer dependency manifest is unchanged, refuses known-stale lower dependencies, and never
# silently truncates an overlarge synthesis packet. Every top synthesis pins the exact hashes
# of the lower summaries actually used; later lower-layer changes propagate STALE upward.
# Read-only Assistant tools `book_knowledge_pyramid` and `read_book_pyramid_node` let her choose
# any layer without Python deciding the appropriate cognitive depth. Personal commentary is
# still deliberately separate: `personal/assistant_annotations.enc` remains the next-stage design
# and must stay Assistant-personal-memory-owned rather than becoming canonical NeatBook fact.
#
# 7.4.54.0 implements the first integrity-backed Knowledge Pyramid foundation without
# forcing the full future pyramid into runtime. Persistent provenance-bearing book objects
# gain an event-driven SHA-256 manifest: original local source file -> canonical extracted
# text -> immutable evidence blocks -> semantic summaries -> structural summaries. Hashes
# establish exact identity/provenance and derivation freshness only; they NEVER certify that
# an LLM interpretation is true. Registrations are idempotent, unchanged derived summaries
# preserve the dependency hashes they were actually built from, and lower-layer changes mark
# every dependent layer above them stale rather than silently rebinding or regenerating it.
# There is no continuous whole-library/background hashing; important objects register on
# import/creation/repair or explicit /integrity refresh. Full source bytes can be rechecked on
# demand. Integrity events themselves form a hash chain.
#
# 7.4.54.0 also gives Assistant one bounded repair agency: if she detects a bad derived book
# summary, request_book_summary_repair may create a pending internal book.summary-repair
# action for exactly one evidence block, semantic unit, or structural resume. The existing
# plural /actions ledger remains user-authorized: proposal does not rewrite anything or call
# a model; execution requires explicit user approval, re-verifies the exact source SHA-256,
# performs one narrowly scoped Assistant summary call, writes a new derived revision overlay,
# preserves source/evidence and historical digest rows, performs no whole-book redigest, and
# propagates STALE upward to summaries that depended on the previous hash. Higher summaries
# are never silently regenerated. Evidence-block repair introduces the first concrete optional
# chunk-summary layer of the Knowledge Pyramid. The prior assistant_annotations.enc concept remains
# DESIGN-ONLY and personal-memory-owned; this release does not implement annotation packaging.
#
# Developer validation adds deterministic knowledge-integrity, targeted-repair-action, and
# integrity-benchmark regressions. /benchmark integrity and /benchmark summary-repair are
# zero-model by default; they measure engineering/hash performance and target-vs-full-redigest
# scope, never semantic quality. The frozen consolidation checkpoint remains separate.
#
# 7.4.53.3 is a narrow command-consistency hotfix. The canonical external-action gateway
# already used the plural `/actions ...` family for status/show/approve/reject/execute, while
# pending review was exposed primarily as the NeatTools convenience `/action pending`. Add
# first-class `/actions pending` to list unresolved actions with plural command hints so the
# gateway vocabulary is internally consistent. `/action pending` remains a backward-compatible
# NeatTools alias; no approval, execution, permission, memory, or tool-security semantics change.
#
# 7.4.53.2 records a FUTURE ARCHITECTURE DESIGN ONLY; it intentionally adds no runtime
# Knowledge Pyramid implementation. Preserve this intent for later work:
#
#   KNOWLEDGE PYRAMID / EVIDENCE-PRESERVING HIERARCHICAL MEMORY
#   - Bottom authority is immutable original evidence blocks/chunks with stable IDs + hashes.
#   - Above them, Assistant/LLM may create a fluid, non-predeclared number of semantic summaries;
#     Python must not force one summary per chunk or predetermine semantic-unit counts.
#   - Formal/source structure (chapter, act, page, section, paper section, transcript segment,
#     repository file/module, etc.) is an independent exact-source-range overlay, not a cage
#     around semantic boundaries. A semantic unit may legitimately cross formal boundaries.
#   - Higher compression layers may summarize chapter/act/source structure and then the whole
#     title/book/source; research may later generalize this as original chunks -> chunk summaries
#     -> page/section/source summaries -> overall research synthesis. "Page" is only one possible
#     source-structure layer, not a universal hard-coded level.
#   - Every derived layer must retain a traceable provenance path back to lower layers and
#     ultimately immutable original evidence. Higher summaries never replace lower evidence.
#   - Assistant may retrieve any layer directly, move up/down the pyramid, or bypass summaries and
#     reopen original evidence whenever confidence, ambiguity, importance, or curiosity warrants.
#   - The pyramid is a useful conceptual view; provenance underneath may be a many-to-many graph.
#
#   PERSONAL COMMENTARY / ANNOTATION OWNERSHIP
#   - Evidence-grounded summaries describe what the source supports. Assistant-authored comments,
#     interpretations, questions, connections, uncertainty, and evolving reactions are PERSONAL
#     MEMORY and must never be silently promoted into canonical NeatBook/source fact.
#   - Personal annotations should carry explicit owner/author identity, stable target refs (book,
#     chapter/act, semantic unit, evidence block, etc.), timestamps/versioning, and evidence links.
#   - Future portable NeatBook packaging may carry an encrypted sidecar such as
#     `personal/assistant_annotations.enc`. It may travel in the same ZIP as the book for portability,
#     but remains logically owned by Assistant's personal memory. On import/restore it must route back
#     to personal memory, never merge into canonical book knowledge. Ownership metadata remains
#     explicit so multiple people's annotation layers could coexist without confusion.
#   - Compression is for navigation/efficiency, never for erasing uncertainty, evidence, or
#     authorship boundaries. Implement first in books, validate it, then generalize to research
#     and only later to other memory domains if normal use justifies it.
#
# At this checkpoint the goal is NOT to keep inventing tests and patches.
# 7.4.46 keeps the stability-first baseline and lets Assistant choose context priority from each query, with hard domain exclusion temporarily disabled and an append-only Reflection Journal. Use Assistant normally. Reopen a subsystem
# only for a reproducible user-facing error, integrity/security/recovery risk,
# or a broken accepted contract. After three failed patch attempts, stop and
# reevaluate the design instead of stacking fixes.
#
# Known unfinished research: RJ22 exposes a genuine multi-hop relation problem.
# The 7.4.39.1/.2 lexical/pivot experiments did not solve it and are NOT the
# baseline. If multi-hop work resumes, prefer evidence-bound relation traversal
# over more keyword cleverness. Preserve Assistant's autonomy: Python may organize
# information, but must not organize Assistant's mind.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
