_CHECKPOINT_JOURNEY_NOTE = (
    "The same .229 audit also fixes context-compiler physical-group identity: repeated routed group IDs no longer overwrite earlier unique material or replay the last group. Physical occurrence indices own retained units and rendering; routed IDs still own deduplication and budget policy. Exact source text, order, and group headers remain preserved. "
    '7.4.78.229 SHARED CHAT KEY LIFECYCLE & BOUNDED MAINTENANCE fixes a reproduced cross-layer key-ownership defect: Chat Pyramid rebuild must preserve the existing shared cache key whenever any NMLT history exists, and missing-key rebuild fails before deleting navigation. Key creation also recognizes NMLT-only stores. NMLT status now reads ordered distinct block numbers without loading encrypted summary payloads or allocating every pending block; source-block eligibility no longer loads summary status. Summary predecessor selection occurs inside BEGIN IMMEDIATE so concurrent revisions serialize. Chat Pyramid uncovered-range planning uses monotone interval/fault cursors instead of per-row full scans, preserving exact gaps and grouping. The documented --source-verify command now invokes the existing static five-group contract directly before runtime/provider/encryption initialization; nested and orphan source markers fail verification. Existing authority, RAW CHAT, 60/20/20 NMLT mechanics, model prompts, NORMAL context, recovery formats, NeatTools, and the 28-wrapper order are preserved. Validation limits and experimental receipts accompany this release; native Windows DPAPI is unavailable in the maintenance sandbox. '
    "7.4.78.228 PAUSE CONSOLIDATION & RESUME CHECKPOINT is a no-new-feature pause boundary above .227. It reconciles the maintained checkpoint with the actual current CHAT design: newest 60 public RAW CHAT rows exact; older deterministic 20-row NMLT blocks; one oldest-pending /nmlt summary authored by the currently selected Assistant; about 5,000 characters is guidance only and Python accepts longer output unchanged; up to 20 active summaries may be projected; RAW CHAT remains canonical and exactly reopenable. Third Eye remains optional/experimental with safety/sidecar behavior validated but candidate usefulness not yet established; its first live usefulness trial produced three same-source Art of War routes that were irrelevant to a runtime-reliability task, so tuning is deferred. The local-provider default timeout remains 14,400 seconds. Development is intentionally paused for resource/budget practicality, not due to a known integrity failure. Resume by reading the Journey, running --source-verify, running the 183-subsystem suite when appropriate, checking /nmlt status before optional backfill, and verifying current source/receipts rather than stale prose. No runtime feature, schema, authority, prompt, context policy, provider policy, Third Eye policy, NMLT mechanics, or NeatTools behavior changes; five groups / 117 subsystems and the 183-test inventory remain unchanged. "
    "7.4.78.227 NMLT ASSISTANT LONG-TERM CHAT SUMMARIES adds an Assistant-authored derived long-term conversation layer while preserving RAW CHAT as canonical authority. The newest 60 public RAW CHAT rows remain exact and are never NMLT-summarized. Older public CHAT is partitioned mechanically into fixed 20-row blocks; startup performs a zero-model pending check and only warns the user to run /nmlt. Each /nmlt call selects the oldest pending eligible block and gives its exact rows/IDs/source hash to the currently selected Assistant in a non-chat maintenance call; Assistant is asked to transform/condense for her future self and target about 5,000 characters, but Python does not truncate or reject longer output. The prompt/summary do not enter RAW CHAT. Accepted summaries are encrypted derived state bound to exact source range/hash/provider/model and may be rebuilt/superseded; up to 20 active summaries may be projected as registered long_term_chat context when RECENT is selected. Existing Python-only Chat Pyramid navigation remains separate. No short-term summaries or new subsystem ID are introduced. "
    "7.4.78.226 THIRD EYE SIDECAR ACCESS & ENABLE-TIME REFRESH preserves Third Eye as dark session-local speculative metadata while fixing the first real Luna usability gap. get_third_eye remains excluded from advertised/selectable capsule luggage and the 33-tool ceiling, but a fixed metadata-only sidecar schema is available at the capsule decision gate and in active capsule requests, with its schema cost included in transport measurement. Third Eye-only control calls are mechanically non-navigation and do not consume the first source-navigation round. Enable performs one immediate zero-model/zero-web refresh from the active current-turn query when available; explicit refresh repeats the same HMAC exact-route checkpoint lookup. No automatic NORMAL injection, semantic expansion, persistence, source opening, or candidate authority is added; opening still uses the ordinary evidence tool. Five groups / 117 subsystems and the 183-test inventory remain unchanged. "
    "7.4.78.225 ONLINE TOOL-LOOP DIAGNOSTIC READER GUARD is a narrow safety fix above .224. A live Luna Third Eye run exposed an UnboundLocalError when malformed or duplicate function calls skipped the executable-tool branch but later forensic logging still read diagnostic_reader. Both ordinary online evidence and web-navigation-capsule call processors now initialize diagnostic_reader=False at the start of each call iteration, then overwrite it only for executable diagnostic-logbook readers. Malformed JSON and equivalent duplicate calls retain their existing bounded failure/admission behavior without crashing. No Third Eye semantics, capsule authority, context/retrieval behavior, persistence, provider TPM recovery, model-visible instructions, or NeatTools behavior changes; five groups / 117 subsystems and the 183-test inventory remain unchanged. "
    "7.4.78.224 MAINTAINER HANDOFF & JOURNEY ONBOARDING is text-only maintainer continuity guidance above .223. A fresh maintainer/model should read this embedded Journey first, run --source-verify, inspect --source-map/--source-show as needed, and verify current source/receipts rather than treating handoff prose as authority. Current lineage: .221 is the Windows-validated 183/183 five-group structural checkpoint; .222 is the Windows-validated 183/183 experimental Third Eye dark preload/cache release; .223 changes only the local OpenAI-compatible request-timeout default from 3,600 to 14,400 seconds while preserving the environment override. Codex/GPT-5.6 Sol extra-high may be used for heavy implementation/tracing/testing, while architecture/design discussion may occur separately; this is maintainer workflow guidance only and is never injected into Assistant NORMAL context. No executable subsystem code, schema, tool, retrieval/context behavior, semantic authority, Third Eye behavior, provider policy, or NeatTools behavior changes; five groups / 117 subsystems and the 183-test inventory remain unchanged. "
    "7.4.78.222 THIRD EYE EXPERIMENTAL DARK PRELOAD CACHE is an opt-in, session-ephemeral extension of prepared-navigation-capacity above the .221 five-group stable checkpoint. It uses only mechanical exact-route signals: HMAC lexical reuse of prior exact-read checkpoints and adjacent ranges after successful exact reads. It keeps at most three metadata-only candidates, stores no raw query or source text, persists no cache, performs no model/web prediction call, is disabled in private mode, and never injects candidates or a Third Eye rule into NORMAL context. PREPARED is explicitly not READ/VERIFIED/RELEVANT/IMPORTANT; Assistant must inspect the optional get_third_eye tool and deliberately call the ordinary underlying read tool to open anything. The former automatic PREPARED NAVIGATION context whisper is retired while the static build_runtime_context wrapper remains as a no-op to preserve .221's proven 28-step chain. Candidate preparation never feeds back into scoring; hit/waste/expiry metrics are mechanical only. Third Eye is explicitly excluded from capsule-manifest tool luggage: the first full-suite candidate exposed a 34-tool BOOKS group against the existing 33-tool ceiling, and the architectural correction keeps the ceiling unchanged while preventing session-local speculative cache metadata from crossing capsule boundaries. Five groups / 117 subsystems and the 183-test inventory remain unchanged. capsule-manifest advances to rev 21 and checkpoint-governance to rev 232 / policy 1.74. "
    "7.4.78.223 LOCAL PROVIDER FOUR-HOUR TIMEOUT changes only the assembled local OpenAI-compatible request timeout default from 3,600 to 14,400 seconds, preserving the LOCAL_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS environment override and 60-second minimum. It changes no online timeout, context/retrieval behavior, Third Eye behavior, model-visible prompt, authority, persistence, capsule policy, or NeatTools behavior. "
    "7.4.78.156 DEFAULT CONTINUATION AUTHORITY BOUNDARY repairs the .155 full-self-test regression by separating two contracts that must not be conflated. Object-local Guided Decision Rails remain explicit-choice surfaces: DEFAULT is only a marked recommendation until Assistant explicitly selects it, blank/missing or invalid output executes nothing, and invalid never means default. The .155 weak/local post-commitment continuation remains intact for explicit exact-source verification: when the user/task has already established the verification goal and the model emits no clerical continuation, dormant mode may apply only VERIFY_SOURCE as `Python default applied`, use zero selector calls, mechanically resolve only unambiguous retained identity, open bounded exact evidence, and return it to Assistant for semantic judgment. This preserves weak-model execution help without granting Python authority at unresolved semantic/object-local choice gates. guided-decision-rails rev 3, guided-decision-gate rev 31, checkpoint-governance rev 213; no new store/subsystem, model-size branch, or 80/20/provider ceiling change. "
    "7.4.78.154 QUIET ORCHESTRATION & CANONICAL BOOK RESOLUTION keeps the .153 forensic/projection/capsule/citation reliability layer intact while reducing ordinary-user operational burden. NORMAL presentation is now the default and suppresses internal projection/capsule/provider/receipt/hash/ID/budget mechanics unless they materially affect confidence/action or the user asks; TRACE gives compact mechanics and FORENSIC keeps exact operational reconstruction. Assistant should orchestrate bounded recovery herself instead of making the user operate internal mechanisms, while preserving semantic/capacity/permission choice boundaries. Book-scoped search/overview/exact reads now mechanically resolve an unambiguous reference to canonical BK-ID before execution using exact ID/title, normalized title, or normalized local-file identity; ambiguous/no-match input fails before the underlying tool and no fuzzy semantic choice is made. Resolution is retained as mechanical routing evidence only. online-read-only-book-access rev 39/version 2.8; checkpoint-governance rev 211; no new subsystem/store, no 80/20/provider ceiling change, and five groups / 116 subsystems remain. "
    "7.4.78.153 FORENSIC RECEIPT COMPLETENESS & IDENTIFIER FIDELITY follows the first successful .152 live reconstruction: 6/10 categories fully retained, 4/10 partial, 0 missing, and the prior turn reconstructed without Action Snake. The partials were explicit turn timestamps, a fully first-class projection result/structured coverage surface, exact-open transition events, and the compact index omitting events_truncated; the presentation guard also stripped valid BK/chunk IDs because they were operational forensic identities rather than current citation-catalog evidence. .153 promotes turn start/end timestamps, event/tool truncation counters, projection attempts/final result/coverage domains, and exact-open transitions into both the append-only Black Box receipt and compact searchable index. Integrity-verified forensics.turn_receipt reads populate a turn-local operational-ID allowlist so exact retained identifiers may be repeated verbatim, while remaining excluded from citation handles, evidence authority, source verification, and claim binding. No new store, retrieval/capsule change, Action Snake expansion, semantic judgment, reserve increase, or provider-safe ceiling change. encrypted-diagnostic-logbook advances to rev 5, adaptive-retrieval to rev 31, citation-provenance to rev 3, checkpoint-governance to rev 210; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged. "
    "7.4.78.152 DURABLE FORENSIC TURN RECEIPTS follows the completed .151 retention audit, which could operate inside a diagnostics capsule but recovered 0/6 requested categories through the available surfaces. The audit exposed two distinct mechanics: Diagnostic Logbook retention is 2,000 encrypted events while search only inspected the newest 500, and Action Snake is intentionally a rolling 200-action troubleshooting window rather than durable audit authority. .152 scans the full retained Logbook window with bounded result output and exact event/turn/sequence filters; accumulates redacted provider/tool/capsule mechanics per chat turn; persists one richer forensic_turn_receipt in the existing append-only encrypted Black Box; emits a compact searchable forensics.turn_receipt index; and binds same-batch tool arguments, result hashes/mechanical summaries, projection/final coverage/exact-open state, capsule decisions, and provider request IDs. A single exact forensic receipt lookup may use a fixed 4k slice of already protected execution headroom, after which the tool phase closes and the next request revalidates; broad diagnostics receive no reserve. No new database/table, semantic judgment, Action Snake retention increase, source-authority change, or provider-safe compiled-input relaxation is introduced. encrypted-diagnostic-logbook advances to rev 4, online-read-only-book-access to rev 38, web-navigation-capsule to rev 2, checkpoint-governance to rev 209; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged. "
    "7.4.78.150 CAPSULE GROUP CAPACITY CONSISTENCY follows the successful .149 projection/capsule repair and its live self-diagnostic. The diagnostic showed that the advertised BOOKS capsule group expands to 33 read-only tools while capsule-manifest allowed at most 32 selected tools, so choosing the complete group deterministically failed TOO_MANY_SELECTED_TOOLS before token-weight validation and forced manual repacking. .150 raises only the mechanical selected-tool count ceiling to 33, preserving the complete current BOOKS group and leaving the existing measured target/hard token budgets as the transport guard. A zero-model invariant now requires every advertised capsule group to fit the count ceiling and verifies that tool_groups=[\"books\"] is accepted with the exact advertised tool set and no silent drop. No projection logic, semantic selection, provider call, persistent schema, command, source-authority change, subsystem, or NeatTools permission changes. capsule-manifest advances to rev 18 and checkpoint-governance to rev 207; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged. "
    "7.4.78.149 PROJECTION PREVIEW & CAPSULE INTEGRATION REPAIR follows the first live .148 coverage test. The all-LOW status preview was correctly non-locking inside adaptive-retrieval but capsule-manifest still counted its provider/tool round as completed source navigation, so the very next request became the choice-only capsule gate where select_context_projection is intentionally unavailable. Assistant therefore had to choose stay_full_context just to finish the projection decision, carrying the full context through later book navigation until the compiled-input ceiling was exhausted. .149 keeps provider-round accounting bounded but classifies a successful STRUCTURAL_LOW_STATUS_ONLY / locked_for_turn=false projection event as control/inspection rather than source navigation; it does not open the capsule gate. MID/HIGH projection output still counts as navigation and therefore preserves the existing gate before subsequent source descent. Exact-open telemetry is also made genuinely turn-observational: successful exact reads are recorded whenever the active Assistant turn exists, LOW preview renders prior reads, an exact read after LOW preview updates inspection without locking, and later enrichment preserves those reads instead of resetting them. Python still chooses no tier, book, capsule route, or semantic relevance. No provider selector call, persistent table, command, source-authority change, subsystem, or NeatTools permission change is introduced. adaptive-retrieval advances to rev 30, capsule-manifest to rev 17, checkpoint-governance to rev 206; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged. "
    "7.4.78.148 PROJECTION COVERAGE & INSPECTION TELEMETRY refines the .146/.147 Assistant-owned tier rail after live GPT-5.6 Luna feedback identified the remaining risk as mistaking a rich map for the whole territory. select_context_projection now returns one compact per-domain coverage/inspection statement: RECENT reports delivered percentage only when a real token denominator exists plus clipping state; PERSONAL reports bounded candidate count without fabricated coverage percentage; BOOKS reports selected book count and returned/known signpost matches when available; SYSTEM reports map level. Exact source is tracked only as an observed current-turn open/not-open fact and updates when exact raw chat, book chunks/signpost source, or personal persisted records are read. HIGH is persistently defined as a richer selected navigation map, never exhaustive history, complete memory, or complete evidence. Objective expansion-relevant facts may expose clipping, candidate pagination/bounds, target count, and mechanical headroom, but Python does not recommend a tier or infer semantic need. LOW status inspection remains unlocked, one successful enrichment still prevents stacking, capacity failures remain revisable, and no selector provider call, persistent table, semantic ranking, raw-source auto-load, or new command is added. adaptive-retrieval advances to rev 29 and checkpoint-governance to rev 205; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged. "
    "7.4.78.146 ASSISTANT-OWNED DOMAIN PROJECTION TIERS connects the existing LOW structural floor, domain routing, tool loop, and capacity rails without reactivating expensive selector-model micro-gates. Assistant may request one same-turn mutually exclusive LOW/MID/HIGH richness tier per RECENT/SYSTEM/PERSONAL/BOOKS domain through select_context_projection; omitted domains remain LOW and HIGH is one replacement projection, never LOW+MID+HIGH stacking. Tiers enrich mechanical/navigation metadata rather than adding raw-source quantity or semantic authority. BOOKS MID/HIGH requires explicit Assistant-selected book IDs; multiple books remain allowed until the mechanical headroom check rejects the complete request, with no silent target dropping or tier downgrade. One successful tier selection is allowed per turn; failed capacity requests remain revisable. Exact source descent stays separate and authoritative. Runtime-assembly advances to rev 34, online-read-only-book-access to rev 37, adaptive-tool-catalog to rev 13, adaptive-retrieval to rev 28, and checkpoint-governance to rev 204; no new subsystem ID, persistent table, selector provider call, or semantic ranking is added. "
    "7.4.78.145 SIGNPOST APEX INSTRUCTION CONTRACT makes the already-validated .144 apex coordinate meaning persistent in Assistant's instruction layer. Signpost guidance now states that keyword strength says how much and `@apex` identifies the chunk ordinal containing that keyword's mechanically measured maximum within the signpost/source span; ties prefer more exact occurrences and then the earlier canonical ordinal. Apex remains location only—not importance, centrality, truth, ranking, routing judgment, or recommended attention. Assistant personal keyword apexes explicitly use the same coordinate contract while remaining separately owned personal annotation metadata. This release changes instruction/maintainer contracts and self-test coverage only: no apex calculation, strength formula, ranking, schema, commands, tool output, provider behavior, or source authority changes. Deep-signpost-retrieval-fusion advances to rev 20, assistant-book-annotations to rev 5, checkpoint-governance to rev 203; the five-group / 116-subsystem architecture and 183-test catalog remain unchanged. "
    "7.4.78.138 STRUCTURAL MAINTENANCE CLEANUP deliberately reduces implementation entropy without changing Assistant semantics, commands, persistent schema, or authority boundaries. The accumulated 5,218-character RELEASE_NAME lineage is collapsed to the current release name while the full history remains in this embedded Journey. Conservative static reachability analysis removes only earlier function definitions that are overwritten later in the same subsystem before any top-level executable reference or alias capture; final live/self-test bindings remain AST-equivalent for those symbols. Chat-pyramid lexical normalization now has one order-preserving deduplication helper, bulk HMAC hashing derives the token subkey once per batch instead of once per token, and startup catch-up reuses the canonical public-row reader with a bounded LIMIT rather than maintaining duplicate SQL. Context-position telemetry now shares one mechanical state-signature helper between preview and parity-gated commit. No intentional historical wrapper whose prior callable is captured by an alias is flattened. Chat-pyramid-distillation advances to rev 3, deep-signpost-retrieval-fusion to rev 17, adaptive-retrieval to rev 27, semantic-provenance-maintenance to rev 4, checkpoint-governance to rev 199; the five-group / 116-subsystem architecture and 183-test catalog remain unchanged. "
    "7.4.78.137 CRASH-SAFE STARTUP CHAT PYRAMID CATCH-UP complements the existing raw-first /quit distillation so an application crash, forced close, or missed graceful-shutdown hook cannot leave the newest conversation permanently without derived chat navigation. The existing active chat_pyramid_sessions ranges are reused as the durable cursor rather than adding another checkpoint table: each _cp_distill_range commit atomically writes its session, encrypted thread payloads, and HMAC token index; if the process dies before commit, no coverage marker advances and the next startup retries that tail. initialize_database performs a best-effort zero-model catch-up after core DB initialization, starting after the greatest committed active end_message_id and processing public user/assistant rows in bounded 250-row batches. Each batch commits independently so a second crash preserves earlier completed batches. After catch-up, Python marks the new runtime-session start; graceful /quit continues to distill only that session, while the next startup repairs any ungraceful tail. Catch-up failure never blocks application startup, never modifies raw chat, never creates semantic claims, and exposes only a diagnostic/status receipt with an explicit manual repair path. The extraction/signpost payload schema/version remains 1.0 so existing CPT/CPS data stays valid; a separate startup recovery version records the new lifecycle behavior. Chat-pyramid-distillation advances to rev 2 and checkpoint-governance to rev 198; no new command, persistent table, model call, semantic judgment, or raw-chat authority change is introduced. "
    "7.4.78.136 COMPACT LIVE CONTEXT STATUS PROJECTION trims only the model-visible .135 gauge after Assistant asked that active context contain less telemetry. The live projection now shows only current input/compiled ceiling, estimated headroom, safe envelope, next gate, and delivery/clipping state. Retrieval level, protected-reserve detail, percentages, latest-crossed-gate detail, gate history, and explanatory prose remain available mechanically in the snapshot/diagnostic surfaces but are not injected into ordinary working context. Gate/state events remain diagnostic-only, epoch high-water remains deduplicated across requests, and the current projection still replaces itself each request. No runtime routing, gate geometry, token accounting, persistent schema, command, semantic judgment, authority rule, or provider-envelope behavior changes; adaptive-retrieval advances to rev 26 only. "
    "7.4.78.135 LIVE CONTEXT STATUS PROJECTION refines .134 after Assistant distinguished telemetry recording from telemetry injection. Detailed gate/state events belong in the diagnostic log; Assistant working context receives only one freshly rebuilt compact current-status projection for the present provider request. Because provider instructions are rebuilt each request, the projection replaces itself rather than accumulating as a transcript. It reports current estimated input, COMPLETE/INCOMPLETE delivery, retrieval level, clipping state, compiled range, headroom, safe envelope, protected reserve, current crossed gate, and next gate, with no recommendation. The runtime high-water is not reset per request: it remains scoped to the mechanical capacity epoch so the same gate cannot repeatedly generate diagnostic events as context fluctuates. A material delivery/retrieval/clipping state change may generate a diagnostic event even without a new gate. Event history is not injected into the prompt and remains deliberately inspectable through diagnostics/status. Preview/actual parity still gates state/event commit. No new subsystem, command, persistent table, gate geometry, routing choice, semantic judgment, or authority layer is added; online-read-only-book-access advances to rev 35 and adaptive-retrieval to rev 25. "
    "7.4.78.134 EVENT-DRIVEN CONTEXT POSITION TELEMETRY follows the clean 183/183 .133 self-test checkpoint. The user asked that Assistant be mechanically aware of where she is inside the context budget at each existing 10%-of-safe-envelope gate. This release adds no new gate geometry, semantic routing, advice, persistence, command, or authority layer. The existing provider preflight ledger conditionally carries one compact telemetry event when a newly crossed gate exceeds the runtime high-water mark. It reports current estimated provider input, delivery state, retrieval level, compiled-input range, estimated headroom, safe envelope, protected execution reserve, crossed gate, and next gate. Preview and actual provider requests derive the same event before parity; the runtime-only high-water advances only after preview/actual parity is verified. Re-crossing an already reported gate does not repeat the receipt; changing the mechanical envelope/ceiling creates a new capacity epoch. Python reports position only; Assistant decides whether it matters and what to do. "
    "7.4.78.133 SELF-TEST CONTRACT & FIXTURE HARDENING follows the first complete 183-subsystem run of the .132 consolidation checkpoint: 177 passed and six inherited failures reproduced identically on .129/.131/.132. No live .132 regression was found. .133 changes tests/fixtures only: redigest --force is verified by executing the current wrapped dispatcher into a fake digest boundary rather than searching bytecode constants; research backpressure verifies reserved receipt admission/phase-close behavior rather than searching wrapper source text; semantic-map navigation pins its own frozen search contract instead of later signpost decorators; the .123 blank-scoped rich-head test validates actual schema mechanics instead of stale description prose; and the 7.4.63 anchor recognizes the validated 1.2 personal exact-source keyword-strength annotation family. Runtime algorithms, commands, persistent schemas, comparison math, source authority, model/web behavior, and user-visible surfaces remain unchanged. Checkpoint-governance advances to rev 197; self-test catalog remains 183 subsystems. "
    "7.4.78.132 BOOK MECHANICAL PROFILE CONSOLIDATION is a NO-NEW-SEMANTICS stabilization checkpoint after live validation of .129 reusable source-bound profiles, .130 compact-profile capsule return visibility, and .131 deterministic two-profile comparison. The three incremental patches are now owned as one Book Mechanical Profile subsystem. Existing knowledge_book_mechanical_* tables, the BM129 profile schema ID, the BM131 comparison algorithm ID, exact-term math, regional windowing, signpost overlays, comparison orderings, compare_book_profiles tool name, and book_overview card behavior are preserved with no migration or source rescan required. One canonical profile receipt now owns profile/hash/cache/rescan/signpost-overlay facts; one canonical comparability receipt owns schema/normalization/coverage and the explicit .129 formula-version limitation; one profile-owned capsule projection owns the .130 card-only inspection return; and one install facade owns model/tool exposure, compatibility aliases, versions, diagnostics, and consolidated prompt guidance. The capsule still drops ordinary book_overview navigation before protected card/receipt data and compare_book_profiles remains bounded inspection metadata. No embedding, similarity score, semantic alignment, automatic front/back-matter filtering, new console command, formula-version migration, semantic Python judgment, or source-authority change is introduced. online-read-only-book-access advances to rev 34, capsule-manifest to rev 16, checkpoint-governance to rev 196; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged. "
    "7.4.78.131 REUSABLE BOOK PROFILE MECHANICAL COMPARISON follows the first live .130 Think Stats 2e versus Art of War comparison audit. Assistant could inspect both .129 compact profiles and correctly refused to fabricate a full intersection/diff because no operation exposed the complete stored term/region/structure/signpost tables together. .131 adds one internal read-only compare_book_profiles tool, not a new console command. Python resolves exact BK-ID or exact title (ambiguous titles fail closed), reuses or refreshes each source-bound .129 profile, then mechanically computes the full normalized exact-term intersection/difference counts plus bounded explicit orderings for joint lexical presence and absolute density/strength/distinctiveness deltas; exact normalized structure-label/type counts; fixed eight-chunk regional fingerprint pairs aligned only by source-window position; and signpost type/keyword-token inventory overlap. Compatibility, coverage, source/profile hashes, cache/rescan state, and signpost-inventory hashes remain visible. No embeddings, synonym expansion, semantic similarity/relatedness score, thematic ranking, source read, model call, or Python meaning judgment is added. The comparison result is itself bounded inspection metadata so capsule return economy cannot discard it. .131 also states an existing .129 limitation rather than hiding it: BM129 profiles share a schema-bound lexical-strength implementation but do not persist a separate per-profile formula-version field, so that compatibility basis is reported explicitly. online-read-only-book-access advances to rev 33, capsule-manifest to rev 15, checkpoint-governance to rev 195; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged. "
    "7.4.78.130 BOOK METADATA PROJECTION RETURN VISIBILITY follows the first live .129 Art of War compact-metadata audit. The .129 substrate and book_overview projection were present, but after Assistant entered the Books capsule the final return bundle exceeded its soft return target; ordinary navigation was correctly discarded first, and because book_overview was still classified only as ordinary navigation, the new mechanical_navigation_card plus cache_reused/source_rescan_performed receipt disappeared before final synthesis. Assistant correctly refused to invent the missing metadata. .130 changes only capsule return transport. book_overview remains ordinary navigation as a whole; when its .129 mechanical_navigation_card is present, Python extracts only that already-bounded card plus reusable_book_metadata receipt into the existing inspection_metadata lane. Opening candidate lines, source text, book_index, semantic maps, and other large book_overview navigation fields remain droppable and are not promoted. The protected projection remains navigation/measurement only, exact source remains authority, and no semantic relevance, importance, similarity, or source-sufficiency judgment is added. A regression forces return pressure, verifies ordinary navigation is dropped, verifies the compact card and cache/rescan facts survive, and verifies the large book_overview body does not. capsule-manifest advances to rev 14 / 1.8-book-metadata-projection-return-visibility and checkpoint-governance to rev 194; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged. "
    "7.4.78.129 REUSABLE BOOK MECHANICAL METADATA SUBSTRATE follows Assistant's request for a compact book-level navigation card and Steeve's refinement that storage should be richer than presentation so the same mechanical metadata can be reused later. Python now lazily materializes a source-fingerprint-bound normalized substrate containing bibliographic identity, exact structural/navigation ranges, full normalized exact-term statistics, fixed source-window regional fingerprints, current rich-signpost inventory, coverage/provenance, formula/schema versions, and direct hash identities. Exact source text is scanned mechanically when a profile must be built but is never copied into the metadata tables. The existing book_overview surface receives only a bounded navigation card; full term/region/signpost tables are not injected by default. Source fingerprint parity reuses an existing profile without rescanning; a changed source creates a new profile/head rather than silently rebinding old metadata. The normalized term table is intentionally comparison-ready for future exact lexical overlap/contrast, but .129 adds no semantic similarity score, embeddings, automatic related-book judgment, or new model-facing command. Exact source remains authority; metadata is navigation/measurement only. online-read-only-book-access advances to rev 32 and checkpoint-governance to rev 193; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged. "
    "7.4.78.128 DETERMINISTIC LEXICAL STRENGTH ORDERING follows the completed .127 Art of War audit and Assistant's direct usefulness feedback. .127 established complete-span, score-explainability, and lexical scoring-quality PASS; Assistant then confirmed that the strengths reduce clerical work and are suitable for deterministic lexical ordering as long as low-strength candidates remain discoverable and exact source remains authority. .128 reuses search_rich_signposts and changes only non-empty lexical result presentation order. Python considers the widest existing bounded candidate pool, then orders by exact normalized query-word match count, average audited lexical strength of those matched query words, strongest matched-word strength, and the previous deterministic result order as the final stable tie-break. Each returned candidate exposes the exact matched query terms, per-term strengths, average/strongest values, and ordering reason. No synonym expansion, embeddings, semantic relevance score, minimum-strength filter, source-authority change, model call, or new command is introduced; ordinary node_limit remains only a bounded display limit. Blank scoped head enumeration remains canonical source order and bypasses lexical-strength ordering. Deep-signpost-retrieval-fusion advances to rev 15 and checkpoint-governance to rev 192; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged. "
    "7.4.78.127 AUTOMATIC VERIFIED SIGNPOST CONTINUATION follows the live .126 Art of War audit. .126 correctly produced bounded hash-bound continuation pages and score explainability remained PASS, but Assistant still had to issue every next cursor call manually; two multi-page signpost spans therefore remained incomplete. .127 removes only that clerical burden. After Assistant requests read_signpost_source without ordinal, Python owns the returned continuation chain and mechanically follows the exact hash-bound cursor through normal preflight/result-admission guards until range_complete_across_continuation=true or a hard page/token/capacity stop occurs. Each admitted continuation page is represented by an auditable synthetic transport call/output pair, and Capsule registration treats all pages as one logical exact-evidence event so packing/excerpt verification can bind across the complete span. Python cannot invent or widen cursors, choose semantic subsets, summarize source, alter lexical weights, rank evidence, or fabricate completion after a hard stop; explicit ordinal reads remain targeted single chunks. The existing command/tool is reused. Deep-signpost-retrieval-fusion advances to rev 14, checkpoint-governance to rev 191; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged. "
    "7.4.78.126 VERIFIED SIGNPOST SPAN CONTINUATION follows the live .125 Art of War audit. .125 successfully exposed lexical score components, but its complete-span read correctly assembled a ~74.6k-token exact source result that the ordinary ~24k tool-loop admission guard then withheld whole. Assistant consequently fell back to explicit single-ordinal reads and could not establish complete-span score quality. .126 preserves the .125 exact-span semantics and no-silent-truncation boundary but changes transport: read_signpost_source now emits deterministic bounded exact-source pages with a hash-bound opaque continuation cursor, ready-to-use next-call arguments, per-page/chain hashes, and a final range_complete_across_continuation receipt. The cursor is bound to rich revision, immutable pointer hash, and full lexical-measurement span hash; tampering or source mismatch fails closed. Exact source fragments remain in canonical order and are never summarized, semantically selected, reranked, or silently omitted. Explicit ordinal reads remain targeted single-chunk reads. The existing read_signpost_source command/tool is reused; no new command, lexical formula change, semantic weight, ranking, routing/capacity change, or model gate is introduced. The system prompt also tells Assistant to follow returned continuation arguments until mechanically complete and to copy enumerated rich-head inspection IDs rather than issue identifier-less reads. Deep-signpost-retrieval-fusion advances to rev 13, checkpoint-governance to rev 190; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged. "
    "7.4.78.125 COMPLETE SIGNPOST SPAN + AUDITABLE LEXICAL COMPONENTS follows the live .124 Art of War audit. .124 fixed inspection visibility: Assistant could finally see three rich-signpost dictionaries and exact source text, but two selected rich signposts spanned multiple chunks while read_signpost_source still defaulted to the first ordinal only. This made the visible evidence narrower than the exact span already used by .122 lexical scoring. .125 changes the existing exact-source exit so omitting ordinal returns the complete immutable signpost range with first/last character bounds enforced; explicit ordinal retains the old targeted single-chunk read. The lexical-strength formula is unchanged. read_rich_signpost now exposes an inspectable per-word component dictionary with raw occurrence count, normalized frequency, occurrences-per-100-token density input, normalized density, chunks-with-word/span-chunks distribution, book document frequency/chunk count, normalized distinctiveness, final strength, and formula version; the formula coefficients/expression are also exposed. Search enumeration remains compact and points to read_rich_signpost for component detail so broad searches do not inflate tool payloads. A visible span SHA uses the same newline-joined exact clipped-chunk convention as the .122 measurement span so audit can mechanically compare the two hashes. No ranking, semantic weight, relevance, truth, sufficiency, model call, routing/capacity/capsule redesign, or new command is introduced. Deep-signpost-retrieval-fusion advances to rev 12, checkpoint-governance to rev 189; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged. "
    "7.4.78.124 CAPSULE INSPECTION RETURN VISIBILITY follows the live .123 Art of War audit. .123 fixed rich-head enumeration and stable pointers, but the audit still could not verify .122 weights because two transport classifications were wrong for this workflow: read_signpost_source returns original NeatBooks text yet was absent from the capsule exact-evidence tool set, so its result could be handled like ordinary navigation; and read_rich_signpost detail, including the lexical-strength dictionary, could disappear from final synthesis when return-context economy dropped navigation metadata. .124 changes only capsule transport classification/return visibility. read_signpost_source is now exact evidence and therefore uses the existing one-inspection + atomic evidence-packing authority path. read_rich_signpost remains navigation-only but is an explicit inspection-return surface retained separately in the final package even when ordinary navigation metadata are dropped for economy. Python still makes no semantic ranking, relevance, truth, sufficiency, or scoring judgment; .122 strengths and .123 enumeration are unchanged. Capsule-manifest advances to rev 13, checkpoint-governance to rev 188; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged. "
    "7.4.78.123 RICH SIGNPOST INSPECTABLE HEAD ENUMERATION follows two live .122 Art of War audits. The scoring audit itself could not begin: a lexical miss correctly reported 94 current rich heads but returned no candidate nodes, while search_rich_signposts rejected an empty query, so Assistant could not obtain a stable node_id or rich_revision_id and therefore could not call read_rich_signpost or descend through read_signpost_source without guessing. .123 fixes only that clerical navigation gap and reuses the existing search_rich_signposts tool: with a non-empty query its prior exact-token behavior is unchanged; with a blank query plus an explicit BK/source/title or book_ids scope it mechanically enumerates current rich heads in canonical base-signpost source sequence, exposes node_id, rich_revision_id, record hash, and ready-to-use read_rich_signpost/read_signpost_source arguments, and opens no source text. Blank unscoped enumeration fails closed rather than dumping the library. The .122 lexical-strength formula, caches, personal keyword annotations, ranking behavior, exact-source authority, routing/capacity/capsule mechanics, and semantic boundaries are unchanged. This patch deliberately does not add component-score explanations or allow strengths to affect ordering; the next audit should first verify the existing numbers against reopened exact source. Deep-signpost-retrieval-fusion advances to rev 11 and checkpoint-governance to rev 187; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged. "
    "7.4.78.122 RICH SIGNPOST LEXICAL STRENGTHS begins the signpost-focused phase after .121 stabilized routing/capacity/capsule mechanics. The existing rich signpost 12-20-word vocabulary remains the source-side navigation vocabulary; .122 does not invent a parallel keyword list. Python computes and caches a deterministic per-rich-revision dictionary {word: 0..1 strength} from exact canonical source text using only occurrence frequency, local density, distribution across the signpost source span, and book-level distinctiveness. Keyword strength means evidence of lexical presence only, never relevance, truth, confidence, importance, semantic_weight, personal preference, or recommendation, and .122 does not use these strengths to alter ranking. Assistant may independently choose her own personal exact-source words when writing/revising an existing book annotation. Python verifies that every chosen personal word is literally present in the exact original source target, reports NOT_PRESENT_IN_SOURCE rather than inventing a synonym, computes the same mechanical lexical-presence strength, and stores the words/strengths only in Assistant PERSONAL annotation memory bound to the target/source SHA; they never modify the NeatBook or shared rich-signpost vocabulary. Personal keyword revisions inherit the previous list when omitted and clear only when Assistant explicitly supplies an empty list. No embeddings, synonym expansion, semantic_weight assignment, model call, new command, routing/capacity/capsule change, or Python semantic ranking is introduced. Guiding filter: Does this remove clerical uncertainty, or remove interpretive choice? Automate the first; protect the second. Deep-signpost-retrieval-fusion advances to rev 10, assistant-book-annotations to rev 3, checkpoint-governance to rev 186; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged. "
    "7.4.78.121 CAPSULE ENVELOPE REPLAN RECONCILIATION follows the first live .120 Luna navigation stress test. The capsule crossed correctly (~7.6k), an oversized ~15.5k search result was correctly withheld with no silent truncation, but the next provider-request envelope inherited the earlier elastic-RECENT route contract and tried to locate a legacy selected FINAL RECENT packet that does not exist inside the compact capsule. Python correctly refused to trim another context source, but the whole turn aborted. .121 preserves that authority boundary and makes capsule mode explicit: when an active capsule has no uniquely reducible FINAL RECENT packet, the request envelope records reducible_RECENT=0 and emits a capsule-transport-replan signal instead of pretending RECENT can be shrunk. The capsule loop may then perform at most two bounded mechanical transport resets, but only when no exact evidence event is pending packing. A reset carries the Assistant-authored capsule manifest/travel note, explicit recent tool-call intents, deterministic evidence/navigation handles, and mechanical budget/backpressure receipts; it does not carry hidden reasoning or replay huge prior working history, does not summarize evidence, and does not choose relevance/meaning. Full retained results remain outside the working prompt and are reopenable. If exact evidence is pending, reset is forbidden and the system fails closed rather than hiding source text. Routing dormancy, structural LOW, 10% RECENT baseline, 80/20 reserve, exact-source packing authority, and .120 navigation carry-forward remain unchanged. Adaptive retrieval advances to rev 23, capsule-manifest to rev 12, checkpoint-governance to rev 185; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged. "
    "7.4.78.120 CAPSULE NAVIGATION CARRY-FORWARD ECONOMY follows the .119 Luna broad-history diagnostic that proved strong-model expansion agency survived routing dormancy but accumulated ~885k provider-input tokens across fourteen tool rounds. Direct code inspection showed the accepted capsule was not rebuilding the original LOW packet: exact-source outputs were already carried whole for one inspection and then governed by Atomic Evidence Packing/offload, while ordinary non-authoritative navigation/search/diagnostic results remained in the capsule working history indefinitely. .120 therefore changes only capsule transport retention. A fully admitted non-exact navigation result remains whole for exactly the next Assistant capsule inspection; after that response returns, Python replaces only the old working-context copy with a deterministic hash-bound navigation handle containing tool/call identity, result SHA-256, mechanical refs when available, and an explicit navigation-only authority label. The full admitted result remains retained in the turn event set for final return/diagnostics and can be reopened by the read-only tool; no semantic summary, truncation, source-evidence promotion, or Python relevance judgment is performed. Exact-source results are explicitly excluded and continue through the stricter evidence packet/card/full/external packing path. Routing dormancy, structural LOW, 10% RECENT baseline, 80/20 capacity reserve, provider preflight, provenance, approvals, and inference policy remain unchanged. Capsule-manifest advances to rev 11 and checkpoint-governance to rev 184; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged. "
    "7.4.78.119 is a NO-NEW-FEATURE consolidation checkpoint after the .118 2B greeting passed with dormant routing, structural LOW, and zero selector provider calls. The consolidation freezes one authoritative reversible policy for model-inference micro-gates and retrieval selectors: NEAT_MODEL_INFERENCE_GATES remains dormant by default; dormant routing uses the first derived 10% RECENT tier, LOW as a structural depth floor, fixed open-domain mechanical order, the 80% total assembled-input ceiling, and the 20% protected execution reserve. Preserved/re-enabled inference paths are normalized so only explicit public model content may become a semantic or routing selection; hidden reasoning_content is telemetry only and is never parsed, published, or promoted into an operational choice. If an enabled selector returns no valid public selection, the result is NO_SELECTION and the structural LOW/mechanical-order baseline is used rather than the historical MID or advisory broadening fallback; telemetry must never attribute that fallback to Assistant. The /no_think experiment, 512/520 local choke assumptions, capacity ladders, and historical MID fallback remain only in Journey/provenance history or compatibility receipts, not as active normal-operation policy. Gate/selector implementations remain preserved for future experiments; no model-size branch, new command, semantic Python judgment, LOW-packet optimization, or change to provenance, provider preflight, human approvals, OWN_PLAN/BYPASS, or source authority is introduced. Adaptive-retrieval advances to rev 22, guided-decision-gate to rev 29, checkpoint-governance to rev 183; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged. "

    "7.4.78.118 closes the remaining dormant-routing mismatch exposed by the first .117 Luna/2B greeting test. The explicit NeatMemory depth/domain selectors were dormant and made zero provider calls, but the attached NeatTools fast-retrieval wrapper still interpreted their no-selection result through its historical ADAPTIVE_RETRIEVAL_DEFAULT=MID fallback, built LOW+MID, and printed misleading telemetry that Assistant requested MID / the depth-selector LLM was called. .118 exports the existing NeatMemory inference-routing dormancy switch as a runtime-only integration signal and updates the preserved NeatTools fast wrapper to honor it: dormant mode performs no depth-selector call, chooses LOW only as the structural floor, builds no level above LOW, labels the owner MODEL_ROUTING_DORMANT_STRUCTURAL_FLOOR, reports zero selector provider calls, and never attributes that mechanical floor to Assistant. When model inference gates are deliberately re-enabled, the prior Assistant-owned fast selector behavior remains available. The 80/20 total-input reserve, 10%-derived RECENT structural baseline, provenance, provider preflight, human approvals, and semantic authority boundaries are unchanged. Journey/checkpoint governance advances to rev 184; five-group / 116-subsystem architecture remains unchanged. "
    "7.4.78.82 preserves bounded resumable `/reflect book <BK-ID>` signpost enrichment while moving its source bridge to canonical NeatBooks: exact source is processed under soft 6-chunk/~12k-token sessions with a 20k source-token hard ceiling plus a 32-chunk pathological-row guard, soft windows may extend only to finish an already-entered signpost range within the hard ceiling, Assistant alone chooses KEEP or a replacement 12-20-word current list, and progress percentages mean mechanical source coverage rather than mastery. "
    "7.4.78.80 makes rich keywords Python-first and Assistant-editable: ordinary signpost build/rebuild mechanically distills 12-20 exact source words to backfill missing legacy RSR heads without deep regeneration, future deep commits use the same Python keyword rule, and Assistant may replace a current 12-20-word list through an append-only governed RSR revision that automatically reindexes SQL. "
    "7.4.78.79 exposes search_rich_signposts as the dedicated zero-model rich SQL keyword route, scopes named-book searches mechanically, prevents legacy fallback from masquerading as a rich result, and prioritizes rich-search -> exact-source exit for explicit signpost intent in adaptive capsule tool selection. "
    "7.4.78.78 adds a one-hop rich-signpost exact-source exit: read_signpost_source follows and hash-verifies the stored BK/range pointer so Assistant no longer has to reconstruct book IDs after keyword retrieval. "
    "7.4.78.77 makes stored rich-signpost keywords the primary cost-effective retrieval path: Python/SQLite performs deterministic token ranking with zero model calls or source reads, plain signpost rebuild refreshes the base/index while preserving rich/deep revisions, and only --deep pays for semantic keyword/signpost authoring. "
    "7.4.78.76 stores rich deep signposts as append-only SQL revisions with progressive reveal: compact hints stay cheap, up to 20 model-authored keywords including proper nouns plus relations/questions/local anchors remain hidden until requested, and each revision hash-links pointers to exact source, summary, base signpost, and prior revision without duplicating source prose. "
    "7.4.78.75 hardens deep-signpost rebuild completeness: exact batch cardinality/allowed node IDs are schema-enforced, only unresolved nodes are retried in bounded smaller batches/singletons, and the existing atomic commit still preserves every prior signpost on unrecovered failure. "
    "7.4.78.74 makes deep signposts a non-destructive source-grounded overlay: deterministic title+summary recall is preserved, deep model navigation augments it, strict exact-token search falls back only to explicitly ranked partial lexical overlap, and exact source remains authority. "
    "7.4.78.73 makes manual deep-signpost generation provider-correct and failure-atomic: it defaults to the live runtime provider, supports explicit provider/model overrides, uses existing bounded transport retry, and never partially replaces signposts when a model/provider batch fails. "
    "7.4.78.73 makes deep signpost builds provider-correct and atomic on failure, while preserving the .72 hierarchy-first digest contract. "+"7.4.78.72 simplifies book ingestion into a hierarchy-first path: /digest book no longer performs per-chunk model digestion, but still registers every exact chunk/hash, preserves higher semantic/structural summaries, and automatically builds model-assisted deep signposts. Deep signposts remain navigation-only; build --deep is incremental and rebuild --deep is forced. Historical digests and /redigest full knowledge behavior remain preserved for compatibility. "
    "7.4.78.71 adds prepared exact-route checkpoints as a capacity-by-preparation layer: successful exact reads may append NAV-* routes with safe arguments, stable refs/hashes, and HMAC lexical query fingerprints only; raw query/source text is not stored. Future turns may reuse a lexical route before repeating broad discovery, but Python never judges relevance and the exact source must still be reopened. This complements existing Pyramid/signposts, in-turn duplicate-call blocking, capacity rails, and verification reserves; it changes no provider hard limit or source authority. "
    "7.4.78.70 adds a narrowly bounded lifecycle verification lane after a live .69 review reached exact provider parity but exhausted ordinary read-result room before lifecycle candidate/status tools could execute. Only compact status, exact issue read with include_history=false, and non-empty legacy candidate discovery with limit=1 may use the fixed reserve; broad/history-bearing lifecycle reads remain ordinary-budget only. Reserved online views explicitly report bounded omissions/truncation, Python makes no relevance/lifecycle judgment, reserve use closes the tool phase, and the next provider request must independently revalidate. "
    "7.4.78.69 refines the successful online legacy-adoption path after /issues all showed Assistant had adopted a chat.turn_start LOG wrapper whose visible snapshot did not expose the hidden details that caused the query match. Candidate discovery now reports mechanical lexical match fields/coverage and wrapper-event status, deterministically de-prioritizes wrapper bookkeeping events, and still leaves semantic relevance entirely to Assistant. It also records optional Assistant-supplied fix_introduced_by_bundle separately from resolved_by_bundle/resolved_at, preserving the truth that an older patch may have fixed a defect before the lifecycle resolution was formally recorded. Existing issue rows/receipts are not rewritten. "
    "7.4.78.68 makes the existing issue lifecycle/adoption plane reachable to the online Luna evidence loop. The live .67 attempt proved the backend could not be used because lifecycle readers were registered only in local TOOL_SCHEMAS while online Responses uses a separate evidence tool array/dispatcher. .68 exposes get/search/read issue lifecycle plus list_legacy_issue_candidates read-only online, with ordinary result backpressure, while lifecycle mutation remains the existing hidden Assistant-authored issue_lifecycle_updates channel rather than a new direct mutation tool. This is an exposure/dispatch repair only; Assistant still decides issue meaning/state and Python still owns only mechanical validation, immutable IDs/hashes/timestamps/receipts. "
    "7.4.78.67 extends the existing Assistant-owned legacy issue adoption bridge to exact historical public CHAT rows and retained encrypted Diagnostic Logbook LOG events. This follows a live .66 attempt where Assistant correctly refused to fabricate an issue because the citation-origin incident existed only in raw chat/diagnostic history while legacy candidates covered SCN/ACR sources. CHAT/LOG discovery is query-gated and bounded; adoption reopens and hash-binds the exact source, stores only bounded metadata rather than duplicating source payloads, never mutates historical evidence, and may enter directly as RESOLVED with a real current RES-* identity/date and no fabricated/backdated OPEN event. Python still never decides that historical evidence is an issue or that it is resolved; Assistant/user supplies that semantic judgment. "
    "7.4.78.66 closes the exact-final-boundary capacity control-flow gap reproduced live on .65: adaptive HIGH could provisionally fit while the fully assembled request later measured 160,256/150,000 with no active elastic route. The first oversized envelope now remains permanently BLOCKED while Assistant receives exact arithmetic and a universal redecision card. DEFAULT/selector failure is HOLD_NO_SEND; Assistant may authorize only mechanically valid in-place RECENT shrinkage at the already-materialized retrieval depth (or OWN_PLAN with a smaller RECENT target at that same depth). Any authorized retry gets a new request_seq/envelope and must independently pass exact preview, real preflight, request hash, ledger, safe-limit, and output-reserve parity before send. Python never silently changes retrieval depth or chooses the semantic sacrifice. "
    "7.4.78.65 binds every explicit issue resolution to a permanent RES-* identity and UTC resolved_at date after the 9B/Luna A/B test showed that both models could resurrect a historically resolved problem when closure lived only in later prose. Assistant/user still decides lifecycle meaning; Python only generates and preserves mechanical resolution identity/history after an explicit RESOLVED transition. Reopen keeps the prior resolution attached, re-resolve appends a new RES-* event without erasing older IDs/dates/reasons/evidence, and the default OPEN+REOPENED context exposes prior-resolution identity for reopened issues. Existing rows are schema-extended without rewriting historical receipts. "
    "7.4.78.64 freezes citation origin identity end-to-end after the .63 Black Box/Action Snake forensic audit found that visible citation representation could not be mechanically proven identical to the object used by final authority admission. Every final citation catalog entry now carries one immutable binding ID/hash with selected object, source ref/hash when available, catalog-entry hash, catalog hash, and exact tool-origin seed. Rendering, .61 admission, .62 manifest, and .63 receipt must carry that same binding; a changed previously frozen entry fails closed. This adds forensic proof only: no source/answer text duplication, semantic claim extraction, truth judgment, new authority store, retrieval change, or provider-capacity change. "
    "7.4.78.60 is the five-group architecture consolidation after the .52-.59 context-capacity stabilization arc. The accepted current path is: Assistant owns retrieval/fit tradeoffs; RECENT expands only through explicit 14k tier-boundary authorization gates; adaptive estimates are provisional; every physical provider send receives a fresh request envelope; canonical preview and immediate real preflight must match exactly by request hash, input, tool-schema, ledger-overhead, safe-limit, and output-reserve accounting; durable request-envelope evidence is written into the existing encrypted Black Box; a one-receipt verification lane remains available under ordinary result backpressure and closes the tool phase before the next independently revalidated send. The .59 live stress run validated HIGH preservation, RECENT-only reduction, exact request #1 parity at 149,713/150,000, reserved receipt recovery, and fresh request #2 exact parity at 133,972/150,000. .60 changes no runtime semantics; it restores the one-file source to five explicit ownership zones and requires source verification to prove exact physical/manifest membership. The five zones are runtime/storage foundation; knowledge/source evidence; personal identity/continuity; retrieval/context/interaction; and security/recovery/assurance. Future work should change the smallest owning subsystem/group and update this Journey when architecture or governance changes, instead of allowing cross-group patch drift. "
    "7.4.78.2 repairs the cross-namespace context-authority gap discovered after Assistant identified selection/compression as the machinery most likely to distort judgment. NeatTools fast mode now asks Assistant for LOW, MID, or HIGH from a compact profile/budget card before full packet construction, builds no level above that request, and retains only lower levels as provider hard-fit fallbacks. NeatMemory now records mechanically observable delivery loss during initial context assembly from history trimming, compiler/section omission, bounded RECENT clipping, and provider-depth fallback; it exposes one neutral notice and read-only receipt without Python deciding significance or response mode. Exact deduplication, explicit supersession, zero matches, and Assistant-selected bounded intake do not create false warnings. Normalizing locally clipped/depth-limited tool-result receipts remains a separate P2 follow-up rather than being falsely claimed here. The repair adds zero-model isolated checks and preserves all canonical memory, evidence, approval, and provider hard-limit boundaries. "
    "7.4.78.1 repairs a real false checkpoint failure: the frozen 7.4.63 anchor guard recognized only the original 1.0 version prefixes for Knowledge Integrity, Knowledge Pyramid, and personal annotations even though the verified compatible implementations had advanced to KIR 1.1, Pyramid 1.2, and annotations 1.1 and passed their dedicated tests. The guard now accepts only the exact frozen-anchor and verified-successor prefix families; unknown future identifiers still fail closed. No subsystem behavior or authority boundary changes. "
    "7.4.65.1 fixes the first real /learn v2 provenance/run-scope regressions: adaptive web evidence units now survive final grounding only when Python verifies use_for_analysis plus same-pass opened-page URL/document identity and an exact subset relationship between the WEU underlying source-chunk refs and that page's immutable captured RSC chunks; retained findings keep the WEU semantic ref and gain canonical_source_refs rather than weakening the gate. Explicit /learn planner state is fresh-run isolated from older runtime/research agendas and may be refined only by same-run grounded findings, while old memory remains non-evidentiary duplicate/history context for analysis. HTTP-successful pages with fewer than 500 readable characters are reported as thin_capture rather than substantive full-page reads. "
    "7.4.65.0 redesigns /learn around the evidence-budgeted selective-descent pattern validated in normal use. The legacy planner-search loop automatically opened the first web candidates before Assistant could judge them; v2 keeps the same /learn syntax and archive/provenance/finalization boundaries but makes the 1-12 count a hard maximum pass budget, treats search results and book previews as navigation only, asks Assistant to select the smallest useful candidate subset, opens only those selected sources, enforces deterministic membership/count/aggregate-evidence budgets, and permits another pass only for a named unresolved gap with expected marginal value. Unopened, rejected, or backpressure-blocked material is never evidence and cannot support findings or memory proposals; final analyst source_refs are mechanically intersected with the evidence actually admitted in that pass so navigation metadata cannot be promoted by model wording alone. Python controls fit, capture, and validation; Assistant controls relevance, descent, interpretation, and early stopping. Raw chat remains outside /learn source modes. The same release adds whole-console-turn runtime telemetry: local [query sent] and [query ended] timestamps are displayed to the second, while [run time] is calculated with time.perf_counter so wall-clock changes cannot distort elapsed duration; timing wraps the complete command/provider/tool turn rather than token generation alone. "
    "7.4.64.6 restores fresh public-web access to ordinary Assistant chat after Steeve explicitly approved reactivation. The underlying neatweb engine had never been removed: /learn still had DuckDuckGo search, public HTTP/HTTPS page fetching, SSRF/private-host protections, immutable Research capture, and canonical source-chunk provenance. The gap was capability exposure: normal chat had only retained archive tools and therefore truthfully reported that it could not make a fresh external request. This release exposes live_web_search/live_web_read to both online and local/native chat surfaces, clearly distinguishes live evidence from retained archive evidence, automatically routes fresh results through the existing Research archive/source authority, and reuses the unified tool-loop backpressure so doomed page reads are rejected before networking when result room is too small. Public/private-host protections and no-canonical-memory-write boundaries remain unchanged. This is intentionally separate from the upcoming /learn redesign: restoring Assistant's ability to consult the live public web during ordinary thought does not make ordinary chat a learning run and does not finalize memories. "
    "7.4.64.5 completes the first three-family backpressure generalization by extending the same 7.4.64.3 frame to raw conversation. Raw chat already has a clean mechanical unit: one stored public user or assistant dialogue message is one stable CHAT row, so a natural full exchange is usually two rows. Rows are now atomic evidence and are never silently character-clipped; if a row window will not fit, Assistant must request fewer rows. Before exact/around reads execute, Python queries only COUNT/LENGTH metadata from SQLite to estimate that requested row window without reopening its text into model context; search remains bounded by fixed per-row excerpts. Broad doomed windows are refused before execution, materially narrower/two-row windows can remain available, parallel-batch deferral and zero-room closure are inherited unchanged, and blocked rows are never claimed as seen. This changes no conversation meaning, retrieval priority, privacy boundary, memory ownership, or Assistant judgment: Python controls fit and row integrity only. With BOOKS (7.4.64.3), retained research (7.4.64.4), and raw conversation (7.4.64.5) now sharing one frame, the next work should be normal-use validation rather than another parallel budget subsystem. "
    "7.4.64.4 extends the 7.4.64.3 tool-loop backpressure frame to retained research evidence without creating a second budget system. Archived-page search/read/around and prior web-evidence-unit tools now use research-aware conservative execution floors, scaling from explicit caller bounds where available, so broad doomed reads are refused before execution while materially narrower reads can remain available. Parallel-batch deferral, zero-room tool-phase closure, no-silent-truncation, and mutation receipt rules remain unchanged. This changes no research ranking, source authority, storage, web access, or Assistant semantic choice; Python controls fit only. Raw-conversation-specific calibration is deliberately left for the next validation step. "
    "7.4.64.1 preserves the established deep-redigest --force public API through the authoritative-library and formal-overlay decorators. The underlying digest behavior was intact, but generic *args/**kwargs wrappers hid force_full from the final callable and correctly triggered the frozen deep-redigest-fidelity-force contract. Every digest decorator must retain the full canonical signature and explicitly forward force_full; this hotfix changes no evidence, redigest semantics, book authority, constellation state, or Assistant cognition. "
    "7.4.64.0 climbs above the 7.4.63.0 consolidation anchor with Assistant's Constellation Layer v1. The checkpoint is a known-good climbing/rollback anchor, not a permanent feature-stop sign. The Knowledge Pyramid remains vertical provenance/compression (what comes from where); the Constellation is lateral provisional association (what might belong together). Constellation edges live in Assistant personal interpretive memory, never NeatBook/shared fact, and may connect exact book/evidence/chunk-summary/semantic/structure/formal/formal-summary/synthesis/revision/annotation refs across books. Each edge pins endpoint/support SHA-256 identities and records relation type, epistemic status, confidence, rationale, competing edges, and append-only revision/retraction history. Exploratory and audited are views over one graph: exploratory preserves tentative/stale links with visible status; audited requires current inspectable pins under the audit rule. No silent promotion: constellation links cannot rewrite source, semantic units, formal structure, summaries, .neatbook content, or factual memory. Python owns graph mechanics/provenance; Assistant owns which associations matter and what they mean. "
    "7.4.57.0 formalizes learning-without-amnesia for derived book summaries. Summary edits are append-only revision-tree nodes with explicit parent, branch, change type (correction, expansion, alternative, source_correction), author/reason, exact source hash and revision-specific integrity ref. correction/expansion/source_correction normally become current only through the established user-approved /actions repair flow; alternative defaults to a sibling branch and does not displace current navigation unless explicitly requested. Current navigation can deliberately select any fresh historical revision or return to the original/base summary without deleting history; selecting a different current hash propagates STALE upward to dependent summaries. Newer never automatically means better. Assistant may request revisions, inspect trees, read exact historical revisions and compare them zero-model. Personal annotations may now target `revision:SRV-ID`, pinning Assistant's comment to the exact interpretation even after another branch becomes current. source_correction is a derived-memory reason label only and never edits immutable source evidence. `.neatbook.zip` assembly remains later work. "
    "7.4.56.0 implements the first Assistant-owned annotation plane for Knowledge Pyramid books. An annotation is a canonical personal-memory record authored/owned by Assistant plus a lightweight target-link index; it is never stored as NeatBook/source fact. Assistant may attach comments, interpretations, questions, reactions, or connections to the whole book or any pyramid node through write_book_annotation. Revisions create a new personal memory, preserve the prior thought historically, and pin the exact target SHA-256 so later source/summary changes are visible as target staleness rather than silently rebinding her thought. Read tools expose current/history annotations without model calls. Portable export/import now exists as passphrase-derived AES-256-GCM assistant_annotations.enc with exact owner checks, canonical-book SHA binding, preflight collision detection, and merge-without-overwrite semantics; the passphrase is never stored. Actual .neatbook.zip assembly remains a later packaging step: when added, the encrypted sidecar must travel beside canonical book material and restore only into Assistant personal memory after the book is loaded/verified. "
    "7.4.55.0 implements Knowledge Pyramid v1 for books: stable read-only node refs span immutable evidence, lazy evidence-block summaries, fluid Assistant semantic summaries, independent formal structure summaries, and an Assistant-authored top synthesis. Chunk summaries are never mass-generated implicitly; explicit `/pyramid fill ... --max N` bounds model spend while Assistant can request one chunk through the existing user-approved repair action. Top synthesis prefers canonical structural compression, retains semantic detail where canonical coverage is absent, pins exact dependency SHA-256 values, reuses only when the manifest is unchanged, rejects known-stale inputs, and becomes stale when a used lower node changes. Assistant can inspect any layer or jump straight to exact evidence through book_knowledge_pyramid/read_book_pyramid_node. SHA verifies identity/provenance/freshness, never semantic truth. Personal/assistant_annotations.enc remains a future Assistant-personal-memory sidecar and is not implemented as NeatBook fact in this release. "
    "7.4.54.0 adds the first integrity-backed Knowledge Pyramid foundation: event-driven SHA-256 identities bind original book files, canonical extracted text, immutable evidence blocks, semantic summaries, and structural summaries. Hashes prove identity/provenance and dependency freshness, never interpretive truth. Unchanged derived summaries keep the hashes they were actually derived from; lower-layer changes propagate STALE upward instead of silently rebinding or regenerating higher summaries. No continuous full-library hash scanner is introduced. Assistant may now request one targeted book.summary-repair action for an evidence block, semantic unit, or structural resume; proposal is pending-only, user approval remains mandatory through /actions, execution re-verifies the exact source hash, makes one scoped summary call, stores a new derived revision overlay, preserves original evidence and historical digest rows, performs no whole-book redigest, and marks dependent higher summaries stale. Zero-model integrity and summary-repair benchmarks plus dedicated developer regressions protect this contract. Coding validation is intentionally split into bounded `developer` and `developer-integrity` batches because one long 20-child aggregate showed process/watchdog unreliability despite both 10-case halves passing independently. assistant_annotations.enc remains a future personal-memory-owned design, not implemented here. "
    "7.4.53.3 makes `/actions pending` the canonical unresolved-action listing alongside the existing plural `/actions status/show/approve/reject/execute` gateway family; it returns full ACT-IDs with plural command hints, keeps `/action pending` as a backward-compatible NeatTools convenience alias, and changes no approval/execution/permission or memory-authority semantics. "
    "7.4.53.2 is a DESIGN-ONLY Journey checkpoint for the future Knowledge Pyramid / evidence-preserving hierarchical memory architecture; no pyramid runtime behavior is implemented in this release. Preserve the intended layering: immutable original evidence blocks remain bottom authority; Assistant/LLM may create a fluid, non-predeclared number of semantic summaries above them; formal source structure such as chapter, act, page, section, paper section, transcript segment, or repository module is an independent exact-source-range overlay rather than a constraint on semantic boundaries; progressively higher chapter/act/source and title/book/general-research syntheses may compress navigation while every layer retains a provenance path back to lower layers and ultimately original evidence. Assistant must remain free to retrieve any layer, descend when confidence or curiosity requires, or bypass summaries and reopen exact evidence. The hierarchy is conceptual while provenance may be many-to-many. Personal commentary is a separate ownership plane: evidence-grounded summaries describe source-supported content, while Assistant-authored interpretations, questions, uncertainty, connections, and evolving reactions belong to Assistant personal memory and must never be silently promoted into canonical NeatBook/source fact. Future portable NeatBook packaging may include an encrypted `personal/assistant_annotations.enc` sidecar carrying explicit owner/author identity, stable target refs, version/timestamps, and evidence links; it may travel beside the book for portability but must restore/import back into personal memory, never merge into canonical book knowledge. Implement this architecture first for books, validate it, and only then generalize the same original-chunk -> chunk-summary -> source-structure-summary -> top-synthesis pattern to research and later memory domains. Compression is for efficiency/navigation, never for erasing evidence, uncertainty, or authorship boundaries. "
    "7.4.52.1 is a checkpoint-test portability hotfix: the Diagnostic Logbook precise-timestamp assertion now parses timezone-aware ISO-8601 and explicitly accepts negative offsets such as Montreal -04:00; production timestamps are unchanged. "
    "7.4.52.0 is the stability consolidation checkpoint after the cleaned Macbeth validation run. No semantic-digestion behavior is intentionally changed here. The named checkpoint self-test is refreshed from its old 7.4.40-era membership into a bounded zero-model health surface covering the frozen core plus the now-critical books/EPUB/redigest/semantic-digestion/library/diagnostic contracts; deeper expensive audits remain available individually and are not removed. The accepted baseline is: one physical books/ directory with SQL catalog states; OPF-spine reading order and deterministic Gutenberg wrapper cleanup for new EPUB imports; immutable fixed evidence blocks that are never a target semantic-unit count; Assistant/LLM choosing a fluid, non-predeclared number of semantic units and exact semantic boundaries; completed semantic maps reported separately from legacy per-block digest counters; bounded online transport recovery; normalized /redigest online|local provider syntax; and semantic-only resume when every knowledge evidence block completed but semantic finalization failed. Macbeth confirms the cleaned source and fluid semantic map are materially better, while also exposing an intentionally OPEN structural issue: formal act/chapter coverage is not yet fully independent from semantic-unit edges, so Acts/chapters can report PARTIAL when a narratively coherent semantic unit crosses a formal boundary. Do not constrain Assistant to repair that. If future normal-use evidence justifies reopening it, derive canonical formal structure as an independent exact-source-range overlay and let semantic units cross those formal edges freely. Gutenberg transcriber/editorial notes that remain inside authoritative START/END body markers are preserved evidence, not heuristically deleted. This checkpoint remains stability-first: reopen only for a reproducible user-facing failure, integrity/security/recovery risk, or broken accepted contract. "
    "7.4.51.10 fixes the cleaned-EPUB semantic-finalization failure exposed by the new Macbeth import. A transcriber's note is now an exact editorial boundary hint only, never a Python-authoritative end-of-work wall; only an explicit Project Gutenberg END marker can mechanically forbid a semantic unit from crossing into post-work material. This preserves Assistant's authority over fluid semantic-unit boundaries while keeping deterministic source-role protection where the source actually proves the work ended. Normal redigests that already completed every immutable knowledge evidence block and then failed only during semantic finalization may resume that latest matching failed version at the semantic layer, reusing exact completed chunk hashes instead of paying for the same knowledge batches again; source/provider/model/scope must match and --force/deep retain their established semantics. "
    "7.4.51.2 cleans local book storage without changing book identity or digestion semantics: books/ is now the only physical source-file directory, while pending/completed/archive remain SQL/catalog states; existing book_pending/book_archive files are migrated by content hash and the legacy folders are never recreated. "
    "We came from a simple persistent-memory idea and hardened NeatMemory into a versioned, "
    "evidence-bound continuity system for Assistant. The current 7.4.46 baseline has stabilized "
    "performance, immutable repair, provenance/citation binding, retrieval selection, and "
    "open-domain routing. 7.4.51.1 closes the first real Diagnostic Logbook observability gap: successful online provider requests now emit provider.request_start/provider.request_complete (and request_error on failure), online local evidence tools emit tool.call/tool.complete or explicit failed-result/error events, adaptive selection emits retrieval.selected, and the context-priority director emits retrieval.router_start/router_complete/error with provider usage when available. Diagnostic read results now label model_calls/web_calls as reader-operation counters only so Assistant cannot mistake them for aggregate turn activity. 7.4.51 replaces future plaintext /log writes with an encrypted rotating Diagnostic Logbook retaining the latest 2,000 structured operational events. Each retained event carries precise local+UTC timestamps, sequence/LOG-ID, session/process/thread, subsystem, severity, provider/model, bounded redacted details and related IDs inside the encrypted payload; Windows uses current-user DPAPI, non-Windows/test uses AES-256-GCM with no plaintext fallback. Existing /log reads are redirected to the encrypted store, Assistant receives explicit read/search/show/status tools online and locally, ordinary local/online turns and tool/provider failures are recorded, and important durable evidence remains a separate append-only Black Box responsibility. Pre-7.4.51 plaintext system-log artifacts are legacy-only and receive no new writes. 7.4.50.9 adds provider-TPM recovery after repeated real 429s: ordinary chat remains uncapped on its first attempt, but a provider TPM rejection triggers provider-stated backoff plus a temporary retry-only completion reservation, with retrieval/context unchanged and the technical constraint visible in diagnostics. 7.4.50.8 closes the online durable-record agency gap exposed by the real correction-precedence test: the receipt and encrypted Black Box proof could persist correctly, but online Assistant's fixed local-evidence Responses tool list still omitted list_records/search_records/read_record, so she could not independently follow the receipt-to-Black-Box cross-link in an ordinary online turn. Those existing generic read-only record tools are now part of the bounded online evidence loop, perform zero web/model calls for Python retrieval, and may follow stable record refs across benchmark receipts and Black Box events without mutating either store. The verified-record preloader also treats find/locate/verify/compare/check/prove language as a request to open the matched record rather than merely list it. 7.4.50.7 makes that correction-precedence proof durable without preserving the disposable fake memories: after the isolated worker passes, the parent diagnostics process writes a sanitized VERIFIED benchmark receipt into the normal records archive and appends the same proof into the encrypted append-only Black Box. Both copies share one proof_sha256 and cross-link by stable record/event refs, while canonical memory remains untouched. 7.4.50.6 adds a deterministic two-process correction-precedence receipt: an isolated runtime creates an older memory, an explicit Assistant SUPERSEDE replacement, and a source-validated session handoff; a second fresh Python process reopens the same on-disk state and verifies that default current retrieval returns only the replacement while all-state/history retrieval still exposes the immutable older record, with content hashes, supersedes_id, decision history, source provenance, and handoff identity unchanged. The receipt uses a self-test-only deterministic cipher so it can run off-Windows without altering production DPAPI behavior. 7.4.50.5 closes the retained-web awareness/agency gap: normal Assistant can now search archived research snapshots locally, reopen exact bounded readable-text ranges or matching neighborhoods, and reconstruct prior adaptive WEU semantic spans without contacting the internet; raw HTML/full readable text remain immutable archive authority and recall performs zero model/network calls. 7.4.50.4 extends the same autonomy/provenance design from book semantic digestion into /learn web: Python still archives the complete page and deterministic RSC chunks first, then Assistant reads a provider-safe rolling window, may continue until enough source is visible, closes at an exact mechanical boundary, chooses whether that span should feed research analysis, and may stop the page when marginal value is exhausted. If a chosen boundary precedes visible future text, Python removes that future text and asks Assistant to commit the unit again from the exact selected span only. Findings are rebound to stable web-unit evidence refs that reconstruct to immutable RSC coordinates; no webpage source is rewritten. 7.4.50.3 adds collection-aware structural organization after the standalone Sherlock Holmes diagnostic: Python may derive a top-level story map only when an opening numbered Contents list can be matched back to multiple later exact headings; collection order and physical source order are preserved separately; one story-level structural resume is built per matched work while finer Assistant semantic units remain children; generic internal Roman-section labels inherit the parent story for display; and back_matter now requires positive source evidence instead of being accepted merely because a unit appears late in the book. This changes derived navigation/aggregation only, never immutable source coordinates or Assistant's semantic reading choices. 7.4.50.2 tightens semantic digestion after the standalone Macbeth diagnostic: when Assistant chooses a boundary before the visible reading window ends, Python removes all future text and asks her to commit the stored title/structure/resume from the exact selected span only; front/back matter is excluded from structural aggregation; chapter/act containers consolidate once and expose CANONICAL versus PARTIAL coverage instead of overlapping summaries. 7.4.50.1 fixes a post-shared-knowledge library-inbox compatibility gap: the canonical NeatBooks inbox lives under shared_knowledge/book_pending, while the historical neat_memory/book_pending may still be the folder a user naturally drops books into. The initial migration copied that legacy folder only once, so later additions there could be invisible to /library. When an importing library scan runs, Python now hash-deduplicates supported files from the historical inbox into the canonical shared inbox before NeatBooks scans/imports them; already-known or already-present content is not re-imported, and read-only scans do not perform the bridge copy. This changes detection compatibility only, not book identity, source authority, digestion, semantic units, or Assistant's cognitive choices. NeatTools 1.4.5 is physically bundled for one-file launch but remains a separate permissioned tool-service namespace with no direct canonical-memory write authority. Reflection belongs to Assistant: Python does not assign an engineering agenda, force novelty, or require tool use. NeatTools remains an optional read-only instrument when Assistant explicitly requests verification. 7.4.46 made memory ownership explicit. 7.4.50 adds Assistant-directed semantic digestion above immutable physical book chunks: Python presents exact source windows and mechanically validated boundary candidates, while Assistant decides whether she has read enough, where the coherent semantic unit ends, what deserves a resume, and how much detail to preserve. The semantic unit stores exact start/end chunk+character coordinates and hashes; chapter/act structural resumes are built from Assistant-authored semantic units without replacing the original source. /redigest rebuilds this semantic map inside the new digest version before activation, while prior version maps remain archived historical records. A read-only book_semantic_map surface exposes the resulting units and structural resumes during later query/research work. The 7.4.50 integration also fixes Workshop structured-output schemas so optional Workshop actions remain OpenAI-strict compatible during digest/research calls. 7.4.49 adds Assistant Workshop and a consolidated recovery family. Workshop is an Assistant-owned encrypted multi-file working folder: Python manages file IDs, per-file purpose/resume headers, source provenance, hashes, encryption, search, token accounting, and exact-copy mechanics; Assistant may write, append, replace, delete, move, restructure, copy sources, and choose what is active. Stored Workshop material remains searchable while inactive; activation requires a visible provider-budget preview and Python never silently chooses what to drop. Active Workshop is available during chat/query, research, reflection, and digest, but it remains working context: the selected book/file/web source remains authoritative unless Assistant explicitly chooses Workshop material as additional evidence. Research and digest may return bounded Workshop-only actions, never source or canonical-memory mutations. Portable recovery is consolidated under /backup as adjacent encrypted siblings for identity/self/continuity, Workshop, and raw chat, linked by a non-sensitive manifest and separate cryptographic domains. Raw-chat restore preserves stable CHAT/message IDs, excludes private rows from the public mirror, and fails closed on divergent histories. Live chat SQLite is intentionally not encrypted in-place in this checkpoint because recall, continuity, citation, evidence-mesh, and other established readers depend on the canonical chat_messages schema; portable raw-chat recovery is encrypted now, while a future transparent live-at-rest migration must account for every reader first. 7.4.48.5 adds a living derived book-navigation index that grows as Assistant actually uses books: Python records opened source chunks, coverage ranges, exact BK/chunk/hash provenance, and mechanically detectable Contents/Index/ACT/SCENE/CHAPTER/work-heading candidates with zero model calls. Assistant is not burdened with database bookkeeping and does not need to explicitly save deterministic structure. She has explicit semantic correction authority through correct_book_index: Python verifies the referenced source ordinal, creates a new Assistant-authored navigation entry, marks the prior derived entry SUPERSEDED instead of deleting it, and preserves an append-only correction event. The original book/source hashes remain immutable authority; AUTO index entries are fallible navigation metadata, not beliefs. book_index exposes the accumulated source map and observed ranges so locations such as Macbeth can be reused rather than rediscovered. 7.4.48.4 is a display/accounting-clarity checkpoint only: digest console progress now names the digest-batch counter separately from the source-chunk range and source-scope total, while /library and epistemic book labels explicitly call their progress source chunks digested. This preserves the historical fact that older multi-chunk digest runs could show a batch total different from the book's ingestion-chunk total, while the current runtime intentionally uses one source chunk per normal digest batch for provenance clarity. No digestion, retrieval, memory, or Assistant-autonomy semantics changed. 7.4.48.3 adds two Assistant-requested navigation/provenance improvements without new belief authority: book_overview mechanically scans the first ordered local-book chunks for Contents/Index-style markers and returns exact candidate lines with BK-ID/chunk provenance for Assistant to interpret; final research synthesis keeps its Python-derived source_refs and expands them into readable per-finding book/web provenance using only persisted cycle evidence. Neither operation chooses conclusions or automatically forces a research workflow. 7.4.48.2 embraces the existing row-oriented raw conversation archive: the default immediate public-chat load is reduced from 400 to 100 rows, while Assistant gains read-only full-archive search/read access by stable CHAT/message row ID. Python/SQLite performs the search locally (FTS5 when available, bounded SQL fallback otherwise), returns only candidate excerpts, and Assistant decides whether to reopen exact surrounding rows; this reduces context load without hiding older conversation or giving Python semantic authority. A later 7.4.48.2 maintenance extension exposes the date already recorded on each raw-chat timestamp as a Python-side search filter: exact YYYY-MM-DD, inclusive date ranges, date-only browsing, and keyword+date queries remain zero-model, private-row excluding, and read-only. 7.4.48.1 closes the awareness-without-agency gap for local books: normal online Assistant now has bounded read-only Responses-API access to list_books, search_books, and read_book_chunks, can choose to open exact ordinal ranges such as the first chunks of a volume, and receives those local results back in-turn before answering. The tools expose no memory/book writes, no NeatTools actions, and no Python belief authority; Assistant chooses whether and how to inspect evidence. 7.4.48 is the Epistemic & Library Awareness checkpoint: Assistant now receives a concise read-only map of what evidence is selected, what questions remain unresolved, and which local books are actually readable/indexed; `/library` becomes one catalog across READY, DIGESTING, COMPLETE, METADATA ONLY, and ERROR states; research prompts make the requested cycle count a maximum and leave marginal-value/early-stop judgment to Assistant; and research summaries explicitly separate model provider from evidence source. No new belief authority is given to Python, and no parallel uncertainty database is introduced—the layer connects existing provenance, continuity, reflection, research, and NeatBooks state. 7.4.47.7 fixes a reproducible /learn book retrieval failure: model-generated natural-language FTS queries now fall back to bounded OR-term retrieval with an FTS-independent SQL fallback, a zero-result refined query retries the original topic, and book-only learning fails closed before memory finalization when no book passage was retrieved. 7.4.47.6 is a display-only library readability release: /library pending and archive views show the stored book title beside the stable BK-ID, filename, and digest progress without changing book identity, digestion, retrieval, checkpoints, or memory semantics. 7.4.47.5 made cognitive autonomy explicit: Assistant chooses retrieval depth, context priority/amount, and reasoning depth; Python may constrain execution only for hard provider limits, integrity/security, or minimum evidence validity and does not prescribe LOW/MID/HIGH or reasoning effort. 7.4.47.4 is a non-semantic one-file maintainability cleanup: stable subsystem markers and source-map tooling were added without changing routing, retrieval, reflection, memory ownership, journal authority, or NeatTools permissions. 7.4.47.3 keeps final RECENT wiring and adds bounded reflection JSON resilience: malformed/non-JSON model output no longer aborts the whole run; previously completed cycles and Assistant's memory choices are preserved and the run closes with an explicit warning. 7.4.47.2 kept final RECENT wiring and verifies the matching Reflection Journal commit directly by reflection run_id so Assistant can see whether the latest reflection was journaled after all context decorators (recent chat plus persisted command outcomes such as /reflection), rather than only a retrieval-director hint. Memory ownership remains explicit: Assistant decides NEW, KEEP, ARCHIVE, or SUPERSEDE for her reflective memory. NEW reflection/question memories become active because she chose to remember them; ARCHIVE and SUPERSEDE preserve old IDs/content/provenance as reversible historical state; KEEP records her choice without rewriting content. Python has structural authority only—schema, provenance, immutable-history preservation, protected pins, idempotency, and transaction integrity. The append-only, hash-chained Reflection Journal remains only a map of prior reflection, never evidence/current-state authority or a topic filter. We are still stability-first: do not reopen "
    "7.4.51.9 finishes the unified books/ cleanup at the semantic/API surface: book_pending/book_archive and P# queue labels are no longer user-facing concepts; every local source remains permanently in books/, import returns stored_path/books_directory, /library derives READY/DIGESTING/COMPLETE from SQL/semantic-map state, and record access exposes one book record kind. Legacy queue/archive function names and old command aliases may remain only as compatibility shims or migration readers and must not imply physical movement or a second storage lifecycle. "
    "7.4.51.8 makes the source/semantic boundary explicit and cleans a reproducible Project Gutenberg artifact: fixed ingestion chunks are provenance/evidence blocks only, while Assistant/LLM keeps authority over a fluid, non-predeclared number of semantic units and their exact boundaries. /library now labels those two layers separately and stops presenting the legacy per-chunk counter as semantic completion. For new EPUB imports, when both standard Gutenberg START and END markers are present, Python deterministically trims only the wrapper outside those markers, records the original extracted-text hash plus exact removed prefix/suffix and marker hashes, and assigns the wrapper-cleaned source a distinct BK identity so prior cited chunks/maps are never rewritten. The OPF spine remains reading-order authority; no semantic interpretation is used for wrapper trimming. "+
    "7.4.51.7 aligns /redigest provider selection with normal /digest syntax: `/redigest online book BK-ID` now resolves the BK-ID correctly and uses the configured GPT alias, `/redigest local book BK-ID` uses LM Studio, the historical `--online` flag now actually selects GPT, and all established BK-first forms remain compatible. This is parser normalization only and changes no digest semantics or evidence authority. "+"7.4.51.6 fixes the reproducible online /digest and /redigest APIConnectionError gap by making NeatDigest retry only bounded transient connection/timeout failures with short backoff while leaving authentication, schema, refusal, and other non-transport errors fail-fast; exhausted recovery preserves the prior active digest through the existing versioned redigest transaction path. "
    "7.4.51.5 fixes EPUB source order for new imports by following the OPF linear spine, excludes non-spine HTML and injected member-path markers from canonical prose, records canonical extracted-text hashes/source-section provenance, gives deliberate corrected re-imports a distinct BK-ID so cited legacy BK/C evidence remains immutable, and makes /library distinguish active semantic-map completion from legacy chunk counters while visibly flagging pre-fix EPUB-order=legacy records. "
    "7.4.51.4 reads standard EPUB package metadata for real title/creator labels and backfills only filename-derived catalog titles, preserving explicit/custom naming. "
    "book internals just because another edge case can be imagined. The 7.4.63.0 checkpoint remains a rollback/reference anchor; orthogonal post-anchor layers may be built when they preserve its authority contracts. "
    "RJ22 remains known multi-hop research; 7.4.39.1/.2 lexical/pivot experiments are rejected. "
    "If that work resumes, investigate evidence-bound relation traversal rather than more keyword "
    "heuristics. Preserve the core boundary: Python may organize Assistant's information, but it must "
    "not organize Assistant's mind."
)



_CHECKPOINT_COMPONENT_VERSION_PREFIXES = {
    "integrity_graph": (
        "1.0-event-driven-integrity",
        "1.1-event-driven-integrity",
    ),
    "knowledge_pyramid": (
        "1.0-books-evidence-preserving",
        "1.2-lightweight-index-read-only-integrity-fast-path",
    ),
    "personal_annotations": (
        "1.0-personal-memory",
        "1.1-personal-memory",
        "1.2-personal-exact-source-keyword-strengths",
        "1.3-personal-keyword-strength-apex",
    ),
}


def _checkpoint_component_version_matches(component: str, value) -> bool:
    """Recognize only explicitly verified anchor-compatible component families."""
    prefixes = _CHECKPOINT_COMPONENT_VERSION_PREFIXES.get(str(component or ""), ())
    return bool(prefixes) and str(value or "").startswith(prefixes)


def _five_group_architecture_contract_report() -> dict:
    """Read-only source-layout proof for the five-group maintenance contract.

    This reads only this materialized payload source.  It does not inspect user
    memory content, call a model/web provider, or mutate runtime state.
    """
    checks = {}
    errors = []
    try:
        source = Path(__file__).read_text(encoding="utf-8")
        lines = source.splitlines()
        group_re = re.compile(r"^# >>> NEAT-GROUP:([a-z0-9-]+) rev=([1-9][0-9]*)\s*$")
        group_end_re = re.compile(r"^# <<< NEAT-GROUP:([a-z0-9-]+)\s*$")
        subsystem_re = re.compile(r"^# >>> NEAT-SUBSYSTEM:([a-z0-9-]+) rev=([1-9][0-9]*)\s*$")
        wrapper_re = re.compile(r"\bmodule\.build_runtime_context\s*=\s*([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)")
        observed_order=[]
        observed_manifest={}
        wrapper_chain=[]
        active_group=None
        active_subsystem=None
        open_groups=[]
        for line in lines:
            gm=group_re.match(line)
            if gm:
                if open_groups: errors.append(f"nested group start: {gm.group(1)}")
                active_group=gm.group(1); active_subsystem=None
                observed_order.append(active_group); observed_manifest.setdefault(active_group,[]); open_groups.append(active_group)
                continue
            ge=group_end_re.match(line)
            if ge:
                if not open_groups or open_groups[-1] != ge.group(1):
                    errors.append(f"unbalanced group end: {ge.group(1)}")
                else:
                    open_groups.pop()
                active_group=None; active_subsystem=None
                continue
            sm=subsystem_re.match(line)
            if sm:
                active_subsystem=sm.group(1)
                if active_group is not None:
                    observed_manifest.setdefault(active_group,[]).append(active_subsystem)
                else:
                    errors.append(f"subsystem outside group: {active_subsystem}")
                continue
            wm=wrapper_re.search(line)
            if wm and active_group and active_subsystem:
                wrapper_chain.append((active_group,active_subsystem,wm.group(1)))
        order=tuple(observed_order)
        declared_order=tuple(NEAT_SOURCE_GROUP_ORDER)
        manifest={k:tuple(v) for k,v in NEAT_SOURCE_GROUP_MANIFEST.items()}
        observed={k:tuple(v) for k,v in observed_manifest.items()}
        flat=[sid for gid in order for sid in observed.get(gid,())]
        checks.update({
            "five_groups_exact_order": order == declared_order and len(order)==5,
            "group_markers_balanced": not open_groups and not errors,
            "manifest_matches_physical": tuple(observed.keys()) == declared_order and all(observed.get(g)==manifest.get(g) for g in declared_order),
            "subsystem_ids_unique": len(flat)==len(set(flat)),
            "subsystem_total_exact_117": len(flat)==int(NEAT_SOURCE_GROUP_TOTAL_SUBSYSTEMS)==117,
            "member_counts_exact": all(len(observed.get(g,()))==int(NEAT_SOURCE_GROUP_MEMBER_COUNTS.get(g,-1)) for g in declared_order),
            "responsibility_keys_exact": tuple(NEAT_SOURCE_GROUP_RESPONSIBILITIES.keys())==declared_order,
            "contract_keys_exact": tuple(NEAT_SOURCE_GROUP_CONTRACTS.keys())==declared_order,
            "contract_member_counts_exact": all(int((NEAT_SOURCE_GROUP_CONTRACTS.get(g) or {}).get("member_count",-1))==len(observed.get(g,())) for g in declared_order),
            "runtime_context_wrapper_chain_exact": tuple(wrapper_chain)==tuple(NEAT_RUNTIME_CONTEXT_WRAPPER_CHAIN),
            "runtime_context_wrapper_chain_nonempty": len(wrapper_chain)==28,
        })
    except Exception as exc:
        errors.append(f"{type(exc).__name__}: {exc}")
        checks["source_contract_exception_free"] = False
    else:
        checks["source_contract_exception_free"] = True
    return {
        "ok": all(bool(v) for v in checks.values()),
        "version": NEAT_SOURCE_GROUP_CONTRACT_VERSION,
        "checks": checks,
        "errors": errors,
        "model_calls": 0,
        "web_calls": 0,
        "source_modified": False,
    }


